[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4e27d3-28a4-5c4c-b64c-c97318703993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477127} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f8030c-9e32-5ae9-ba46-541f78c07d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477127} :answer-field/id #uuid "4325ea6f-c825-59af-b418-4d03cd726cfe" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3faf9aed-2e42-5804-8239-bab232db5dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477129} {:answer/id #uuid "79b9715e-0243-5db8-8f75-37135a50d142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477130} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37b91e8-5472-5172-b3e5-dd6ac76e24fa" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477534} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4b88ba-409e-5305-8634-f9e216ca2a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477131} {:answer/id #uuid "39a4163f-abdc-5a7d-aba6-6ba3d2a8afc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477131} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b592d7-5a89-5dcf-83da-ad19a65e44f0" :answer-field/key "The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44bbbc90-f4f2-5074-bbff-3d0cc0edc535" :question/math-academy-id "q-124004" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u+v-1.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-2$ is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1=-2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $det(A)=-2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a03d5be-b43a-5223-989f-be4a85e36371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b2/b20fe76891c51a93d0bf4d74e09c7e0772fe9a011e3eec09ed8a02921cb60383.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387452} {:answer/id #uuid "8267479e-f6dd-5399-b623-dbb2b161a468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b4/b4b93aa372c7ca8328987e5aae70ab571eb802b5460ca1f01ac8aafba4c46209.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387453} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8a28ff-c5c8-5c83-9668-ecbf7b5f2716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/321b52ae83693a8f05f5ccf75ecbb27ba76f15a2788e76e181a860dc83d0df17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387454} {:answer/id #uuid "de05683f-69c8-5843-9fcc-ea6a3f92d7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d6/d68388247637bf10f9852aa25c89f73520149a9a2b3f237426ed04c88e8a8856.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387455} {:answer/id #uuid "12b30638-d1c7-5db1-a9a4-7f5be6da6ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6a/6a7ae9745765d4eff1e828316026eecfabd1422e7b080c81189cef10879dd4c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387454} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ecf2a9-dc8d-58d1-af49-1f925ab3c18d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "792b9e8d-f955-5d43-9589-7b1339149af4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124103" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4a1d43bc68a44db24d22767630573ebd9aafb646196e212640c24f2650e7b17.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1-u}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/0d/0d736c2e3fa2c4ba1872b045b8bdcf4def98a24337683e368bec337b810b6acb.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-1),(1,0),$ and $(-2,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1aed421f9d6458fb8b72a44c83703b3f1c0475cee9ef9ec2f0a4cfcaad74d7fc.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=3>0,$ this transformation preserves orientation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb68de75-4474-546d-86b8-f138d3e02e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177622} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed7a6a6-8520-54a7-839e-cdc981fe0e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177623} {:answer/id #uuid "4480be1c-4540-5e18-aec8-3414727ed2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177624} {:answer/id #uuid "47a455ed-a11c-5fd3-8140-40b883244967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177625} {:answer/id #uuid "498db3d4-987b-56f1-a074-e73e8096d60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177626}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0520a840-c83e-595e-9e75-bfd39bf1ffc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177621} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157a2168-9826-565a-8830-f23a709d7bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378485} {:answer/id #uuid "2b00227f-3181-5c81-833c-c343685c4e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378486} {:answer/id #uuid "4139bbf5-cb92-5a61-bdcf-80d6e60d8d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378487} {:answer/id #uuid "62dce562-3e92-5a70-ade6-fe2dcc38ed52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378488} {:answer/id #uuid "0e21f2b4-91b5-55f3-8ba2-c09c67989eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378488} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1fe27d-d0a0-5f02-86d9-789a27697fbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4e42d76-39ef-52d6-9aa7-90e3d7d61fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124109" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=-2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 2-6\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfe983a6-86f9-5e85-b458-f781e21c140d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448110} {:answer/id #uuid "0a98043d-5dff-5ea4-9e03-3d50d77054b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448110} :answer-field/id #uuid "41004b63-e782-515c-b8db-25e0f62efb36" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449095} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb4ef7fd-13ca-58f5-a761-f38a0e1a271e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448112} {:answer/id #uuid "800c874a-5daf-5173-b771-501d14f8edfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448112} :answer-field/id #uuid "d532ade3-90d1-5f1e-9570-f9069f48be56" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449096} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a595b56c-93c8-5082-873d-5f72788d8c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448114} {:answer/id #uuid "02c905a9-96d8-56f3-b13d-9f2cb1327c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448115} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9bbb31-cd77-53c4-9780-2f910cb04323" :answer-field/key "The transformation is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "267c4ee8-beb7-545b-be5a-57b42fce6778" :question/math-academy-id "q-124111" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation is invertible” is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The transformation is invertible” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f120e747-ca95-5759-b8ec-eafc3a35ba62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448122} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c25924-cf5c-5a7d-8af6-0e25982699fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448123}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448122} :answer-field/id #uuid "693e9f5c-89c9-56bd-af7c-801e081bcba7" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f54989c-7784-5627-8e6b-6f62adbd34df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448124} {:answer/id #uuid "9d55e342-05bd-5933-9261-faa213e3ffa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448124} :answer-field/id #uuid "34483a14-9440-50e5-bdd6-6b571ee1fd6c" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449102} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "714d09d4-f329-541c-9d1f-ea0d6c330506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448126} {:answer/id #uuid "a8422e70-f2b5-5477-bb06-828103e9ac69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448127} :answer-field/id #uuid "492621a3-4f38-5cae-b371-5b8ae037e9ba" :answer-field/key "The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91a59d9f-f2b5-5450-b850-f776f534f3f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124199" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-2$ is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02791ce3-0e59-59cb-a7c2-9c1f5f9b8efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256804} {:answer/id #uuid "190adee9-da90-5ae0-b72f-1d54cbec3732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256997} {:answer/id #uuid "560421a3-f826-5226-b6d1-b81db072eecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257522} {:answer/id #uuid "7390b29f-af98-55d8-908c-23b7a22da5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794} {:answer/id #uuid "b236cd5d-f995-5fc3-910f-4a9dd21013f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c7b60de-da7e-5307-9f2b-34e67ae5cd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec525dce-ff06-5bce-974f-75c3d14b6b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124597" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09128715-0b6a-5c9a-b7c4-82cfe1932e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414832} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a55ec-5d2e-5ff0-b433-60144af0675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414854} {:answer/id #uuid "64f18f1c-5e0b-5164-a6fc-70267c5edda2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414857} {:answer/id #uuid "76c57faf-8ecf-5560-9acf-a26154142b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414862} {:answer/id #uuid "c895dd03-f0b4-5188-9445-aa181d036f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414891} :answer-field/id #uuid "84019ffa-a424-5feb-9be8-03a303ad719a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a8909b1-1a48-5c56-ac06-8b954196fc3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124936" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={-t|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0e7d68-45fb-5911-aa84-8c889f5a6eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257184} {:answer/id #uuid "32c72cad-f159-5542-8a8c-4a3eaf08df88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257228} {:answer/id #uuid "449bf8b5-0413-5a45-b0be-01608c8d6c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257382} {:answer/id #uuid "6b33adab-0bcb-5f56-9951-3743a1c1156d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257728} {:answer/id #uuid "f6198c67-c429-58fc-a1c5-505d3a89af1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257184} :answer-field/id #uuid "2148376e-286c-5569-abfd-8ed21b82d782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9565c624-6479-5e2a-bbcb-90464f68b7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125039" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{2}⁡t)j \\\\  & =i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\text{ })\\cdot (i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{sin}^{4}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb882db-732a-5483-ac99-b04e8df958eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278730} {:answer/id #uuid "28977fa2-da01-505c-8f16-93bbe5d18d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278953} {:answer/id #uuid "69f0468f-7044-547a-8fc3-bca92e912400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279549} {:answer/id #uuid "a93ec374-5b3c-53d9-bf26-af26e0e1bc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280093} {:answer/id #uuid "c32d4cdb-a75a-5454-ac5d-3eba7d0a00f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280305} :answer-field/id #uuid "598f153e-1b92-57a2-a76c-7a9ae5ff1816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "23554130-d2fa-542b-a17b-f4f9de21608d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162141" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u+v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e9a443-79c4-5055-8bf5-e828053d84d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371995} {:answer/id #uuid "3172d89a-2dcc-560d-a62a-a221fb530105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371996} {:answer/id #uuid "9af5ab04-abbb-5e0d-b9e1-126b263417a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371997} {:answer/id #uuid "128276b9-8ed8-5cf9-b12a-9060590e5d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371998} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb0e067-3a51-5735-a6e9-204ac8516a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371995} :answer-field/id #uuid "423586d1-8fd5-5b16-a1d1-51c2288808b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b737dc54-d234-56ac-a9cc-ffa80f3129e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162142" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|2|=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278836} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "638bcae2-f573-5b91-97f0-f5ab7fc66eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} {:answer/id #uuid "7e024141-a94e-5f97-ae2f-a04b85d857ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279720} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ad425-777b-5f11-8db0-c06039f852ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222be89-5b86-5d7e-873f-1d11313d8d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" :question/math-academy-id "q-162151" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9c2945-081c-5afd-a5c3-bf5b1a6127d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln(2)+1}{3-\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541637} :answer-field/id #uuid "30df98ac-26e2-575f-9740-2cdd94af54be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a4d2c64-ecf2-540e-86cd-1d47fbb20b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-233243" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{3x+1}={2}^{x-1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+1} & ={2}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 3x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 3x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1 \\\\ x(3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407b1421-300f-5650-8cf7-dd126bc29f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413010} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8a1d3b-7cc7-56d2-8a0b-b470fcd35f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413021} {:answer/id #uuid "901a2e65-5fa9-5165-ba1b-6642336cd921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413022} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d6414c-0388-584f-92ec-491360dc6f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413028} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1d323b-e5df-5e29-88d7-6527a8a633a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413035} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e33dea3-f3c2-5a02-9f0a-3fc23f07af84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74ba859e-96c2-518a-a67a-a55e65c7d804" :question/math-academy-id "q-39681" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+{z}^{2}\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+{z}^{2}\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+{z}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+{z}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{t}^{2}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b58ef3-88cb-5f48-a5ad-2a8ebd9fe489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324024} {:answer/id #uuid "50d3ce4b-91e9-50e5-be2e-217bd0e8638d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324634} {:answer/id #uuid "6291e7cd-d04f-52bd-8607-0fc13a433b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324790} {:answer/id #uuid "7b429634-b6de-5be5-94db-a86b7b87bf65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325007} {:answer/id #uuid "a83831c3-ae0d-5759-ae8b-eb2f48f47f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325402}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324634} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84998b8-8912-50f1-b594-a9f825576cb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce49b8d5-a7c7-525b-89a9-21901c00319a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40255" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbd92b205693f1056e4dc2d0fc8154843e62b433b66d9b0ba8918d7f5bb3b8bf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{-2}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{-2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1269fb37-f5f5-5f49-8256-65a8aa8aade1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324137} {:answer/id #uuid "202d1e9c-c862-5c6c-ae37-26c7ec997456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324237} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba52178-68df-5d08-aaf9-5d4c9645bdbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325012} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a81ce8-7c24-5166-bd26-99041082a9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325380} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f82ca1-73f0-575a-951c-3ca7fa8e19e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325668}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324137} :answer-field/id #uuid "de1ba936-f5f5-59fd-91b3-8a8a870fa20a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d6d33094-0194-5a44-b10e-00c00343eeb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41145" :question/problem "![](images/78/78f8ca3e70afb8377748a18d7024ba297e330add59550a41a87025a0145b56bb.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$  traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e89690-a473-5e96-8e1b-8772141217ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349180} {:answer/id #uuid "530d4756-be46-5443-808a-7585bbe7565c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349205} {:answer/id #uuid "6697a40a-e34b-5bb9-950b-de39a8750a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349386} {:answer/id #uuid "921c24c0-b579-5b2b-8d7f-6191bf8532b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349754} {:answer/id #uuid "9aead026-b2ca-55fe-850d-efdababe6aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349754} :answer-field/id #uuid "245516cf-1ad0-5aae-a56c-6eb5c3eb71f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "233cf6a6-4ea2-585c-8c92-fd68b4e1c273" :question/math-academy-id "q-81192" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\cdot {x}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}-x\\cdot {x}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-1] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ad462e-980a-55e7-8603-ff255c0b0f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi (1-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413011} {:answer/id #uuid "5b212ba5-4523-505f-abd1-6ed94aa4f312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413014} {:answer/id #uuid "95804867-920f-57f6-9af2-84579578a567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi (2+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413023} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee67649-cfe6-55fe-8d15-2b91a6e5a085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi (1+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413025} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b321de-ff7e-5fef-afd8-96961b2c148e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413025} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a11798a-13e6-56d1-93c3-1b2f2fdacf74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "428d05f1-93a8-5aa4-b7d0-699c4ffafa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-90724" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1+t\\,dt \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi +\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1+\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180666 :tutorial/content "The most general way of solving exponential equations with different bases is to use logarithms on both sides of the equation. \nFor example, given the equation\n\n$$\n3^{x+1} = 5^{x},\n$$\n\nwe could apply $\\log_3$ on both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\log_3(3^{x+1}) = \\log_3(5^{x}).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{3}⁡({5}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law for logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{3}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}⁡(5) & =-1 \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}⁡(5)) & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{1-{log}_{3}⁡(5)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{log}_{3}⁡(5)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** in the first step, we also could have applied $\\log_5$ on both sides of our equation, instead of $\\log_3.$ Then, the answer would be numerically the same but expressed using $\\log_5$ instead of $\\log_3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46223175-9314-5060-b3a3-dc01af9f1b38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2799 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183965 :question/id #uuid "b4d39977-408e-5bb9-ad4d-5e39beae9b03" :question/math-academy-id "e-15085" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be Boolean functions of three variables defined as\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =max\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\}, \\\\ g({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =({x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3})^{{x}_{1}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nComplete the following table corresponding to the function $g(x_1,f(x_1,x_2,x_3),x_3)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x_1,f(x_1,x_2,x_3),x_3)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "A Boolean function of $n$ variables $x_1,x_2,x_3\\ldots,x_n$ is a function of the the form $f: \\{0,1\\}^n \\to \\{0,1\\}$ whose variables are either $1$'s or $0$'s and whose value is either $1$ or $0$.\n\nFirst, we complete the table for $f(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ using the definition of the function:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\max\\{x_1, x_2, x_3\\}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we complete the table for $g(x_1,f(x_1,x_2,x_3), x_3)$ using the definition of the function with inputs\n\n$$\nx_1, x_2:= f(x_1,x_2,x_3)\n$$\n\nand $x_3$, respectively:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f= \\max\\{x_1, x_2, x_3\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$(f \\cdot x_3)^{x_1+1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183976 :question/id #uuid "c7175d7b-47bf-5337-a937-31bf52a3654a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15114" :question/problem "Which of the following Boolean functions are truth-preserving?\n\n1. $f(x_1) \\equiv \\min\\{x_1^2, \\overline{x_1}\\}$\n2. $g(x_1,x_2) \\equiv x_1 \\cdot x_2$\n3. $h(x_1,x_2,x_3) \\equiv (x_1 \\Rightarrow \\overline{x_2}) \\land x_3$" :question/worked-solution "A Boolean function (logical operation) is truth-preserving if it returns $1$ (true) when all its variables are assigned a value of $1$ (true).\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- Function I is *not* truth-preserving since substituting $x_1=1$ does not give a value of $1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =min\\{{1}^{2},\\overset{―}{1}\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function II is truth-preserving since substituting $x_1=x_2=1$ gives a value of $1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1,1) & =1\\cdot 1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function III is *not* truth-preserving. Indeed, substituting $x_1=x_2=x_3=1$ does not give a value of $1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1,1,1) & =(1\\Rightarrow \\overset{―}{1})∧1 \\\\  & =(1\\Rightarrow 0)∧1 \\\\  & =0∧1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183977 :question/id #uuid "0408a0cd-d432-562a-8e29-f184fce2e8f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-15115" :question/problem "Which of the following Boolean functions are falsity-preserving?\n\n1. $f(x_1) \\equiv \\max\\{x_1,\\overline{x_1}\\}$\n2. $g(x_1,x_2) \\equiv \\overline{x_1 \\Rightarrow x_2}$\n3. $h(x_1,x_2,x_3) \\equiv \\min\\{x_1,x_2\\} \\lor x_3$" :question/worked-solution "A Boolean function (logical operation) is falsity-preserving if it returns $0$ (false) when all its variables are assigned a value of $0$ (false).\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- Function I is *not* falsity-preserving since substituting $x_1=0$ does not give a value of $0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =max\\{0,\\overset{―}{0}\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function II is falsity-preserving since substituting $x_1=x_2=0$ gives a value of $0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0,0) & =\\overset{―}{0\\Rightarrow 0} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function III is falsity-preserving. Indeed, substituting $x_1=x_2=x_3=0$ gives a value of $0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =min\\{0,0\\}∨0 \\\\  & =0∨0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184052 :question/id #uuid "c049aaef-f59a-5113-b739-20cc7a6d802c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3520" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184053 :question/id #uuid "5da3337e-31d2-5f2d-b236-60088b4bf64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3524" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184132 :question/id #uuid "06df772e-eb43-55e8-9ede-3fc2f7ae291d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9984" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{7}{25}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184135 :question/id #uuid "8877f6b8-8914-5022-9ec6-d4e8e0d3d159" :question/math-academy-id "e-9992" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \n\\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{9}^{2}}{{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{225}-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{225}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{12}{15}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{75}+\\frac{36}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48d7c891-85d3-5f18-98e4-2c7a4c8db324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251161} {:answer/id #uuid "9eeb417b-418b-5dc9-baa8-c6446ee30e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251924} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a76c3f-b1bb-51e7-92c8-cf13cf7d4c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252177} {:answer/id #uuid "cf09de8d-51aa-5ea3-bc9a-c5def2f65da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252382} {:answer/id #uuid "eacca78c-6a14-5ec3-97c0-98ba3650b2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3fd2f76-ecce-5fba-aee2-6f5e6dff1202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ba92bee5-a4eb-55dd-b31e-91c198d4e5df" :question/math-academy-id "q-119179" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{5}-b}{24},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{5}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{24}-\\frac{11}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-11}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=11,$ and $a+b=16.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a9cefb8-a0e4-5b79-8c26-5ac76834bb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181417} {:answer/id #uuid "b8927739-bda7-5c7d-9801-ca342ae0aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181418} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cf2677-bb1c-5019-88a5-62130e41078a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181419} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb88dbe-2a14-5a9b-a7a2-c04f09113dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181420} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb9827b-2023-5742-96d5-5dadfc0ba955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186181421} :answer-field/id #uuid "82513cd4-0854-5641-b6b1-efbbfd7c3b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a50a050b-9ee8-550e-9478-f50c032731af" :question/math-academy-id "q-1774" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{7}{25}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{24}{25}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{125}-\\frac{72}{125} \\\\  & =-\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a39aa27d-70d9-5f6f-a4fd-25897917b267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181491} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd27856-2d6c-5018-b3db-d589d9d9b7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181492}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bc92e3f8-5b8a-5512-b09b-0b083aafc9d3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181490} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046cc8d8-6847-5c44-aaab-67b26aa05e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181494} {:answer/id #uuid "244e4f0b-5163-5db5-8a71-7d19f2d8a526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181495}] :answer-field/id #uuid "238839f4-ae42-5b74-b0f0-c5bfaf2ccc29" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2642a40b-4790-5bf0-914b-cf7f93af7685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181497} {:answer/id #uuid "5292dac5-89b1-5b0a-a243-f4e3d7db10ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181498}] :answer-field/id #uuid "943530cd-0df3-54b6-b9a5-ea220e5a2ab6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8e7a97-604b-5437-9f7f-7d221923a184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181500} {:answer/id #uuid "56dcec10-987d-5a36-a0de-4db0e1678413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id