300c86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334492} :answer-field/id #uuid "f048515c-6126-5ffd-a26b-08ea4ff82c9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4aa47f46-244d-51e1-8773-d950dc59e732" :question/math-academy-id "q-65423" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35078d74-667f-5d42-9d17-366be03c50b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333475} {:answer/id #uuid "a68ca484-4871-5d33-a32d-9c7dd67ecbc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334449} {:answer/id #uuid "d31b1ea6-9889-5698-98e1-878acc489e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334837} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dd2f79-e39a-58bc-aee5-01f3ef542729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334963} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5bd80c-b257-5028-b120-1e6934062109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335207} :answer-field/id #uuid "50abe121-f986-569c-96f5-0b825aa3ad90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "397bffc1-1dbf-51bd-b606-1b9fd6b7e36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65570" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4afd6e9f2b3343a8364f088653aa99cc4ceeeeef7c53f9014b2dd83314f66118.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/8a/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5116a423-b446-57a3-8da2-be77e9442cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339870} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8fa4ef-2acd-5a1e-8486-2335929cdcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{74}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340379} {:answer/id #uuid "a944a8d9-e058-59d2-86db-62cd8d3ee107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340669} {:answer/id #uuid "d16bd773-8156-5744-b6b9-91c9bd46d5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340986} {:answer/id #uuid "fc682f3a-332d-52ce-8dee-73e4f392d88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341358}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339870} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e771ed-b822-507c-a459-71c3773ee7d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f39d2c0-c3fb-5d82-bb15-c8596eec6f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-74457" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277e201b-e0c9-5bb8-ad51-e4b2e0d637b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339470} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6330a8-df16-5431-b62c-d45668a202bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340257} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed22792-51df-5b84-95c2-b048ca49b484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340411} {:answer/id #uuid "90e49300-9189-5f87-9da1-72bc332ef1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340426} {:answer/id #uuid "a443b359-ec05-54c8-b490-653511bd6a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339470} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1673985-f31f-5367-b121-471d3646c64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c420f71d-937d-5553-a82e-dad726247670" :question/math-academy-id "q-74459" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b}{35},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}{{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{49}-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}}{35}-\\frac{15}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}-15}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,b=15,$ and $a+b=23.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25efffe0-a97f-5bba-872f-819835e25610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345695} {:answer/id #uuid "2d97bafb-5a87-5ba7-b763-927943b3e7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345755} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef6fff-f3d4-57ee-b1a4-d5aada807fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346056} {:answer/id #uuid "b12badcd-f45e-5665-aa07-1cbd839e3523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346899} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf326fc-b3ea-5f91-bf0c-6759d86b03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc3c1af-059f-504a-97e2-7584a4073b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c0fa171-9c01-5949-8df7-ed01d22c88ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-79194" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41a721eab2eb97e8b7b30ddf877e37a48d2b74eeef82ab0a4b72f3bb4a93603.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f0/f08846ce1f79cd14c593fbf37360fb8169a26704c956506ca0deedb8025e3b39.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185313 :tutorial/content "When we take a power of a trigonometric function, we write the power after the function but before the argument. \n\nFor instance $\\sin^2 30^\\circ$ is the same as $(\\sin30^\\circ)^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\tan^4 \\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right)$ is the same as $\\left(\\tan \\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right)\\right)^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03c172f7-f624-575b-9b19-ecc0979231dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2726 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185347 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for sine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v = \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\sin u = -\\dfrac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\sin\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{v}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{u}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{12}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}+\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "699a9021-a261-5b19-b767-75fd52b030bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185350 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for cosine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = -\\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\cos(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\right) \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) +  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\dfrac{3}{15} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}+3}{15}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75e177a6-4974-5699-b211-c562c0b8bafe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187986 :question/id #uuid "deaef5cc-99fb-57e5-a6f2-22f17a7f00af" :question/math-academy-id "e-10033" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdeef265fec0703b42b8d04e55502da49e8e84624a761368edd1dd5c106c7dbc.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187999 :question/id #uuid "be43d5ab-221c-53c0-a3a4-a03c17d1af21" :question/math-academy-id "e-10091" :question/problem "Let $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{110} \\sim U[1,5]$ be an I.I.D. sample of size $110$ from the continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[1,5]$. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{110} X_i.\n$$\n\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nMoreover, if we define\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nthen, by the properties of mean and variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = n\\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = n\\sigma^2\n$$\n\nand consequently,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a uniform random variable $X_i \\sim U[a,b]$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 110, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{a + b}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{2} \\\\\n&= 3, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= ((b - a)^{2})/(12) \\\\\n&= ((5 - 1)^{2})/(12) \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of $X$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX \\mid ∼N(nμ, nσ^{2}); ∼N(110 \\cdot 3, 110 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}); ∼N(330, \\frac{440}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188000 :question/id #uuid "6cfeed33-4982-5c93-96b3-bd34e3603659" :question/math-academy-id "e-10092" :question/problem "Let $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(0.4)$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,80$ be independent geometric random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{80} X_i.\n$$\n\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nMoreover, if we define\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nthen by the properties of mean and variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = n\\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = n\\sigma^2\n$$\n\nand consequently,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a geometric random variable $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1}{p}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 80, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{0.4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - p}{p^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 0.4}{0.4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.6}{0.16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of $X$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX \\mid ∼N(nμ, nσ^{2}); ∼N(80 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}, 80 \\cdot \\frac{15}{4}); ∼N(200, 300)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188001 :question/id #uuid "1205c813-39f9-517c-89ec-d237d901f09a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10093" :question/problem "Given that $X_i\\sim B\\left(25,\\dfrac{9}{20}\\right)$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,66$ are independent binomial random variables, what is the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a binomial random variable $X_i \\sim B(m,p)$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = mp, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = mp(1-p)\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 66, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= mp \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{45}{4}, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= mp(1 - p) \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\cdot \\frac{11}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{99}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}); ∼N(\\frac{45}{4}, \\frac{99}{16} \\cdot \\frac{1}{66}); ∼N(\\frac{45}{4}, \\frac{3}{32})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188023 :question/id #uuid "2175ea86-60e7-5402-810d-1372b455c28d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10244" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right).$ Given that \n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty\n$$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $y'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188197 :question/id #uuid "04ad6c63-8642-51e7-bf95-70d8b97f4f9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-16378" :question/problem "Given that $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Po}(12)$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,60$ are independent Poisson random variables, what is the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a Poisson random variable $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\lambda\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 60, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] = 12, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] = 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\overline{X} & \\displaystyle  ∼ N \\hspace{-0.167em} ( \\mu  , \\frac{ {\\sigma }^{2}}{n} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ∼ N \\hspace{-0.167em} ( 12 , {\\displaystyle \\frac{12}{60}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ∼ N \\hspace{-0.167em} ( \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}12}} , \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}}}} ) .\\end{array}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188200 :question/id #uuid "b0e16600-3365-562f-8cc8-bf25e0c1638a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3144" :question/problem "Express $\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1$ in terms of $\\sec x$ only." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (1)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1 = \\sec^2 x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188201 :question/id #uuid "91980d9e-0e4f-546e-b9ee-3117c4950aed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3455" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue} 1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188202 :question/id #uuid "915ff3d9-c631-5134-82ff-58dbf0167602" :question/math-academy-id "e-3463" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\sec{\\theta}+\\tan{\\theta}} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}\\sec^2{\\theta} - \\tan^2{\\theta}}{\\sec{\\theta} + \\tan{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta} =  \\sec{\\theta} - \\tan{\\theta}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188205 :question/id #uuid "366bcb1d-3fe4-5047-8306-bd28935b5431" :question/math-academy-id "e-631" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -5\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 5\\cos\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{ 5\\cos\\theta }{  -5\\sin\\theta} =-\\cot\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b47647-2bba-505f-8d7e-311d4a4c980d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185425} {:answer/id #uuid "c30e5ea3-e2d6-5c94-88aa-d763c1948734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185426} {:answer/id #uuid "c68099e9-f773-54d9-a3e5-42bd9bdb5311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185427} {:answer/id #uuid "d288b09f-39a9-530c-aa67-bbf726fea5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185428} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bca3e6-7175-5376-9f18-97b44e4a39fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185428} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38f958f-f702-57d9-a4a7-e10129a07961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee6627c5-4c31-5514-890e-94be51b161ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-104045" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f757ffa45b20e043ad461cb0dfa9c486cf73466b1d8d87fa0ffad00bc01af960.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $1,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(5)=-8.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =-8 \\\\ f(3) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8-1 \\\\  & =-9 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8-(-4+1) \\\\  & =-8-(-3) \\\\  & =-8+3 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-8,-9,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9eca73a-1249-5ae1-b362-ffc094617276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185443} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b4cbdf-1577-5a6a-99f5-35e017e43b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185444} {:answer/id #uuid "1345e68d-d5bf-5260-bf4d-cd225fed96a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185445} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb6dbb3-fbbe-59d2-9fe0-e3e1bfcc09bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185446} {:answer/id #uuid "5e94f044-8c76-5981-9173-610f747c5013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185443} :answer-field/id #uuid "d153408e-bf16-54d4-a353-ba2449e79d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ab229b6-ba1f-5ab4-bb83-4e18f639baa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104271" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fedd81f87921d865c375ecdbf9b83439092d138686e07753bb20ae522b6e49c9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(0)=56.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ rectangles and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/96/962bea79c8308d1d63937aa15f19492f2dd0c31f278e2957a71255dbbd92a8ca.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =56 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56-(-2-2) \\\\  & =60 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56+2+2 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{56,60,60\\}=56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=56,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae0aa42-137c-5929-9f50-9cbe99413f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185575} {:answer/id #uuid "ce606e18-ea99-51a2-9414-cc1bc9672aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185576} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6aec67-3e69-505f-8510-f7f1d30022ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185577} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c64601-4ae3-5cd3-bc62-afc2d6af9c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185578} {:answer/id #uuid "33bcd1d5-ce07-586f-94e3-5a964f24ff68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185579}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9a4818-249d-52b4-9b2a-b4b918db983b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46c918b6-7554-51a9-bee3-16f04d66b4a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139768" :question/problem "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$25.2, 24.8, 28.9, 27.5, 28.9$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $max(X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{n}) - min(X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{n})$ for this particular sample.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68ad5e9-7fe7-596a-8c2b-19ee3b81de60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185785} {:answer/id #uuid "15178949-e15f-5295-b833-173787b1d3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} {:answer/id #uuid "23daea13-16e4-5115-a876-009e017926b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185787} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24e81a-7cd0-52ae-a4ec-7a2d4936cd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185788} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecf83c7-e917-54b8-9b79-79ccd5e0603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb7bcb5-fc17-5e6e-81c2-7dacca31243a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c577835a-f79d-5fd0-ba9c-ce150ab9e53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144828" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d3726847858c1afc66497820a48fa6d6bbe39e4d909b84b562a2daeea085bb4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function comprises $2$ triangles, $2$ rectangles, and $1$ trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/99/99f558b3c1d86c5638543361a63823f13a9e01f2cc5042d0f781080f9f33c8a2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{3}{2}+1) \\\\  & =2-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+2+4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}\\}=\\frac{19}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{19}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9bf8e0-79ed-5d01-89a2-3af4798a573f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175