86045486} :question/id #uuid "98997ad6-1ba3-510a-969b-b1a491e9f8b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-65392" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006ac565-3dd4-545b-88ff-1ba93330516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354659} {:answer/id #uuid "0af68300-902b-59ad-9366-74244493cd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354766} {:answer/id #uuid "191e22e7-a43e-5d6f-9cfb-2f0afa7a8cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354889} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4859f5-0bd1-5b69-a467-aa61fad86a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355479} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b652fe-5e94-5b4f-9539-d7592a59c3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354659} :answer-field/id #uuid "00204e71-88c6-55ef-b26d-a1004280d383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a7256ac7-6551-5109-b9b8-6e1497bbcfbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-9388" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2edd37e6318a7d78b3e6f496bb3f688d006949ccab983f75cece9993817f6b69.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189359 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a distribution with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$. Let's define a new random variable as the sum of all $X_i$'s as follows:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nThe sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\nn\\cdot \\overline{X} =\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nTherefore, our random variable $X$ is simply the product of $n$ with the sample mean:\n\n$$\nX = n\\cdot \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nNow, the central limit theorem (CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline X] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline X] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ using the properties of mean and variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[nX^(―)] \\\\\n&= n \\cdot E[X^(―)] \\\\\n&= nμ \\\\\nVar[X] &= Var[nX^(―)] \\\\\n&= n^{2} \\cdot Var[X^(―)] \\\\\n&= n^{2} \\cdot \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\\\\n&= nσ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\overline{X}$ is (approximately) normally distributed, we have that $X$ is also approximately normal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThis is an important result. It tells us that provided that we have a sufficiently large I.I.D. sample, the sum of the elements from that sample is approximately normally distributed with mean $n\\mu$ and variance $n\\sigma^2$. This result does not depend on the distribution of the $X_i$'s!" :tutorial/id #uuid "f31ce5bb-4e62-59d4-8adf-4ac6ec126cfe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10564 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189364 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\cos^2 \\theta,$ we obtain our secant-tangent identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we subtract $1$ from both sides, we isolate the $\\tan^2\\theta$ term.\n\n$$\n\\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nThis is an **alternate form** of the secant-tangent identity. \n\nWe can get a second alternate form if we again start with \n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubtracting $\\tan^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can use these alternate forms to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d5b1ac1-9a35-55c8-aafd-4634168aa751" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2487 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189368 :tutorial/content "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ whose graph is shown below \n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula $\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ to simplify our expression for $y.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-(2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "adced1fe-4c40-50d1-a9b4-12fcf213b6e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6236 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189391 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a54c219-b6df-5e73-8673-14f48b1d69af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6384 :tutorial/title "Using Limits to Find Horizontal Tangents"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192526 :question/id #uuid "701d023b-6d62-5ebd-ac25-cb79a7dd886e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11147" :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 15$ and $n_y = 16$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. You're given that unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are $\\overline{x}=30$ and $\\overline{y}=31$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=1^2$ and $s_y^2=3^2$.\n\nConduct a hypothesis test at the $1\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>t_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$16$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.337\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.746\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.120\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.583\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.921\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(15 - 1, 16 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(14, 15) \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (30 - 31 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{1^{2}}{15} + \\frac{3^{2}}{16}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.261\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $1\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 14$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 2.624\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\,\\color{blue}2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$16$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.337\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.746\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.120\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.583\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.921\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the $t$-distribution. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2.624}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-1.261)$ does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85dbb739807649887fa53b60e9df4d281e982ecc5e39ef32eee3653302902581.png)\n\nSo, we don't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $1\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192527 :question/id #uuid "ae8fc61e-c6b6-55c7-a9c4-e1ccec7d255f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11148" :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 15$ and $n_y = 12$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. You're given that unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are $\\overline{x}=8$ and $\\overline{y}=9$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=0.75^2$ and $s_y^2=1.2^2$.\n\nCarry out a hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ does not equal the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>t_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.372\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.812\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.228\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.764\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.169\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.363\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.796\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.201\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.718\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.106\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.356\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.782\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.179\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.681\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.055\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$ is the alternative (two-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= min(15 - 1, 12 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(14, 11) \\\\\n&= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (8 - 9 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75^{2}}{15} + \\frac{1.2^{2}}{12}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 2.520\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $5\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the\n\n$$\n5\\% \\div 2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\n*one-tailed* critical value) for $k = 11$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 2.101\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.372\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.812\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.228\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.764\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.169\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.363\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.796\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\,\\color{blue}2.201\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.718\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.106\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.356\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.782\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.179\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.681\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.055\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2.201} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad T \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.201}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-2.520)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66df920face74df61db66b8ab8e4d9ebc454972869bb417028fd29d633222c4e.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192528 :question/id #uuid "c7b773e3-8efc-52e3-bac1-6b4bc95de054" :question/math-academy-id "e-11149" :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 42$ and $n_y = 47$ from independent populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. You're given that unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are $\\overline{x}=2.1$ and $\\overline{y}=3.8$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=8^2$ and $s_y^2=3^2$.\n\nCarry out a hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>t_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$40$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.684\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.021\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.423\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.704\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$41$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.683\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.020\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.421\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.701\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$42$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.302\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.682\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.018\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.418\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.698\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$. However, the sample sizes\n\n$$\nn_x = 42 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn_y = 47 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nare *sufficiently large.* Therefore, we can apply the central limit theorem.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated using a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= min(42 - 1, 47 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(41, 46) \\\\\n&= 41\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (2.1 - 3.8 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{8^{2}}{42} + \\frac{3^{2}}{47}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.298\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $5\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 41$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.683\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$40$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.684\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.021\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.423\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.704\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$41$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,1.683\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.020\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.421\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.701\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$42$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.302\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.682\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.018\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.418\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.698\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the normal distribution. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-1.683}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-1.298)$ lies outside the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd12bb6686f2b79a3e4c96a95dad4179d0dab57957da96fd7177f6fd6d4f7add.png)\n\nSo, we don't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8436a7f-8d64-54e1-80c3-ec7f6d677955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "θ^{11}e^{-18.2θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189797} {:answer/id #uuid "12933f75-1192-55ce-8182-9f37bad8199b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "θ^{18.2}e^{-7θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189798} {:answer/id #uuid "26ddf660-c544-5611-8c57-23b96b789b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "θ^{7}e^{-18.2θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189799} {:answer/id #uuid "3e59c6ea-ea1f-5985-bac4-7084f5d8e0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "θ^{-7}e^{18.2θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189800} {:answer/id #uuid "4ece9261-bd5e-5dfc-bf33-5690cc192a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "θ^{-18.2}e^{7θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189801}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0a12856b-0c5c-57df-9ac1-8d56f39f0d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0df3572a-0fd0-5a5c-b2e0-870107821e66" :question/math-academy-id "q-143475" :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{7}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$ and unknown rate parameter $θ$. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{7}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(7)x_{i} = 18.2.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by*\n$f(x; θ) = θe^{- θx}, x \\ge 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193895 :tutorial/content "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 14$ and $n_y = 18$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances, where the unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=47\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overline{y}=40\n$$\n\nand unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=7^2$ and $s_y^2=8^2$.\n\nWe wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is larger than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nTo solve a problem like this, we proceed as follows.\n\nWe are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example, the null and alternative hypotheses are the following:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x > \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= min(14 - 1, 18 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(13, 17) \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (47 - 40 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{7^{2}}{14} + \\frac{8^{2}}{18}}) \\\\\n&\\approx 2.635\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the critical region corresponding to our significance level.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{k,p}) = p\n$$\n\nfor some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,1.350\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.771\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.160\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.650\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.012\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, the $10\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 13$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.350\n$$\n\nSince the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$, we must consider the right tail. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.350}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that our test statistic $(2.635)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/813c8270ac07466c74e50fbc9377d83cfad9e773057d40183550d5dbd204868a.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude that there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ce70000e-5984-5ef9-a054-816a3c979671" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6987 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198079 :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596" :question/problem "![](images/ee/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198167 :question/id #uuid "b817524b-bf24-5f11-a0c8-d544f026b0b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13188" :question/problem "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $A = \\left[0,3\\right]?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- $x_{\\max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if $x_{\\max} \\in S$ and $x \\leq x_{\\max}$ for all $x \\in S,$ and\n\n- $x_{\\min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if $x_{\\min} \\in S$ and $x_{\\min} \\leq x$ for all $x \\in S.$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(A) & =min[0,3]=0 \\\\ max(A) & =max[0,3]=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198208 :question/id #uuid "4a21148b-be3d-51d9-bae1-743960130617" :question/math-academy-id "e-13324" :question/problem "Let $N = 10^{n}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_0$ be a positive integer,\nwhere $a_{n}, \\ldots, a_2, a_{1}, a_{0}$ are the digits of the number. Prove that if $13 \\mid N$, then\n$13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)$." :question/worked-solution "We need to prove the implication\n\n$$\n13 \\mid N \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the statement in terms of congruences:\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $13 \\mid N \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad N \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:13)$.\n\nNow, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n> Substituting the expression for $N$ into the congruence, we get\n> $10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod13)$.\n\nNow, we need to show that this implies\n\n$$\n10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0}≡0 \\mid (mod13)\n$$\n\nThe idea is as follows:\n\n- We add $13ka_0$ for some integer $k$ to the left-hand side. This will give an expression that's also congruent to zero.\n- The value of $k$ that we pick should give a multiple of $10a_0$ when combined with the existing $a_0$ term. This allows us to factor $10$ from the left-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, note that\n\n$$\na_0+3\\cdot 13 a_0 = a_0+39 a_0 = 40a_0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Now, without changing the congruence, we can add $39a_0$ to the left-hand side since it's congruent to $0$ modulo $13{:}$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} + 39a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod13) \\\\ 10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + 40a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod13) \\\\ 10(10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0}) & ≡0 & (mod13)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we cancel out the factor of $10$.\n\n> Since $10$ and $13$ are coprime, we can use a division rule for congruences and cancel out the factor of $10$ to get\n> $10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod13)$.\n\nFinally, we can write down that conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation means that\n> $13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)$.\n\n**Note:** All the transformations we performed above also work in reverse, which means that the right-to-left implication is also true.\n\nThe obtained equivalence statement is known in arithmetic as the rule of divisibility by $13{:}$\n\n> *\"A number is divisible by $13$ if and only if the sum of four times the last digit and the number formed by the rest of the digits is divisible by $13$.\"*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x,$ write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v.$ \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right)  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\left(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}\\right))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3e58ec-a069-5c04-b3f3-191e368fce34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266375} {:answer/id #uuid "26450e7f-99ef-532b-a5bd-fc05e3212848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266486} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf29a7d-c26f-5e2d-aea5-c1086ff7611f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266534} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb1a41e-e722-5153-a1d7-614c622c72b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266558} {:answer/id #uuid "315990ad-db42-56b4-b470-e9bc2c220bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266375} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ecba40d-5f78-569a-ba4e-84129b21bab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0419637b-40c5-5740-b2f6-97e085201516" :question/math-academy-id "q-136827" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410175} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410180} {:answer/id #uuid "a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410183} {:answer/id #uuid "f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13f589cb-239b-530f-99ce-e58d907d8d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-136828" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb041804-f145-50b4-816c-fcab9c17bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388519} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e13d89-d82e-50de-b738-ce617b4eb4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388520} {:answer/id #uuid "4da080a5-de45-50af-a433-06854c96c579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2330d3-d405-54f2-a9a6-48d3b6275ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522} {:answer/id #uuid "3e679990-9d14-53c0-a924-3aa2912773d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c95db7-7a90-557c-8b94-0b154d58f86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e8e756a-b2ab-5c3c-9c14-7a7f8b61663e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138085" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1512bf0f-a0b6-5737-8456-15bf7348d13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414668} {:answer/id #uuid "189d044b-20ef-594f-9050-30d36ea1c805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414669} {:answer/id #uuid "3cba96ef-b9b9-582c-9ce3-fb19b45e7859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414670} {:answer/id #uuid "b75669b6-5915-5577-8d99-85ede9b169dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414671} {:answer/id #uuid "e53e0f0c-01ad-5114-93c5-0442b0a0663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414668} :answer-field/id #uuid "72e392f4-71c8-5797-8085-77f0d25ff7b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48e09a9b-efe0-5a6d-96f4-c82dbcd33806" :question/math-academy-id "q-138095" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $3.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065a15b8-12c9-53ae-bd0a-e9f6748d1fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278649} {:answer/id #uuid "38deba95-8400-5c27-8920-c039ae2ec912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279089} {:answer/id #uuid "872538af-19dc-5963-896a-a34a8710c00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279800} {:answer/id #uuid "96e8a11f-4b02-5eec-ab22-0ea6ff0592d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279927} {:answer/id #uuid "f527702e-055f-5e6c-a545-ed1e3c4a13b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278649} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f681284-a969-5632-9ce6-9a2c49f0747a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2172c015-c92e-5e1e-9610-119af892797f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163061" :question/problem "The lamina $$\n\\displaystyle D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{sin}^{3}⁡y]}_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "307a7ca4-01fa-5646-885b-a0e8867f3b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279016} {:answer/id #uuid "501aac95-5532-5856-a642-0d3b1d67f719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279322} {:answer/id #uuid "5327eac7-a93d-5b6d-af9d-e6bb83813164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279347} {:answer/id #uuid "9996b21a-d3a1-58ce-91cf-3cc1dfc91e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{21}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279948} {:answer/id #uuid "f72fd95a-5b27-5a58-83e0-471231a84783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{15}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279347} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c3c972-8140-526a-b8d9-d9d228cd316b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b31c8b99-cc6d-5598-a2c8-85f537ed1a0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163586" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b8ddb36e814517af21d790374e1fb192141bd6ef0fd99e83a147c6ed5a62aea.png)\n\nA plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ and the coordinate axes, as shown above. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{7}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{7}Mr\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-0+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{31M}{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31M}{35}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084f7ce5-201e-5ad6-9bce-d9e103302f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281759} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0ebcf9-e9a6-5cca-97f6-85bfa563e4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cebb42-cb22-5ab6-8b45-cbd08c48d13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283255} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dcb565-f7cc-56d6-babf-2810305e6f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283387} {:answer/id #uuid "dd543f2a-9082-5f1c-b191-79aeac5df263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283255} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7dce51-5a8d-500a-a178-44f5571990b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "38171cb4-52a5-5ebe-b692-159c5b30da34" :question/math-academy-id "q-170138" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $40.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{bmatrix}-40\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964ff685-0c8f-5399-a6db-e7e9ff284b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194742} {:answer/id #uuid "c4372638-eac2-5651-a42e-b474aa8e5014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194743} {:answer/id #uuid "e585951e-1933-5d5a-a55e-f897fdd4664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194744} {:answer/id #uuid "135fadcd-1711-57c3-92f2-65d7240f6b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194745} {:answer/id #uuid "5642d232-0433-5476-bf0e-5697fbc0a389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186194746} :answer-field/id #uuid "223a3270-62e6-5297-b31c-f1bd2215a506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a89d201-6c7f-5b6a-9c34-e445b93c0f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-170160" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}-16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $25{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=100.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=5$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -16 \\\\ -40 & -20\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -80 \\\\ -80 & -100\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}20-\\lambda  & -80 \\\\ -80 & -100-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (20-\\lambda )(-100-\\lambda )-6\\,400 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+80\\lambda -8\\,400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -60)(\\lambda +140) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =60,\\,-140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-140.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "baa14cce-b7cb-5a32-ab6e-5df43e5f5784" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195511} {:answer-field/id #uuid "291d10b5-8aa9-56be-9ba4-9b5151da6e9e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8a5d360-a9c2-56aa-83b8-ef7ddfdb7058" :question/math-academy-id "q-181716" :question/problem "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $A = {1, π, 3,-2}$?$min(A) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$max(A) =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9944a089-a76b-5528-a82d-ebb0e9d2c79b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195513} {:answer-field/id #uuid "c5eb9d0b-6737-5807-a155-db93244a4bef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "350762c8-b041-599f-99c6-3ca6acd477bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181717" :question/problem "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $B = [- 1, \\sqrt{5}]$?$min(A) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$max(A) =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c24015-2433-5836-b081-d24c5852f0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406714} {:answer/id #uuid "74fb204e-62de-5113-a57a-b0d722924c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406721} {:answer/id #uuid "8524fdad-28da-528a-92f9-faca4791d98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406727} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9753b3-62da-5662-8688-049fcc1bf71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406728} {:answer/id #uuid "f6902130-3b72-504d-8069-c5b5ea38e581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406728} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea78180-0fde-56dc-91ec-da9439574c93" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b966bc22-d89b-5ccc-b68e-01f630cd7e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406731} {:answer/id #uuid "c06f4cc3-c59f-582b-b122-f00bdde60517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406731} :answer-field/id #uuid "59fbeaf4-1e54-5e93-87c0-e9ae1f0701cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0185113e-67ca-5fea-8414-80405b6d2c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406712} {:answer/id #uuid "17e03875-19f4-5839-86f0-459f64514f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406717} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b32247-1da0-5476-8f92-1c3ff937c2ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3570e8ac-e013-59f3-be0f-4ce10d5893a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181726" :question/problem "What are the minimum and maximum of the set ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n\nThe set has no {{field-1}}, while the {{field-2}} is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition, ${R}_{0}^{-}=(-\\infty ,0].$\n\nThis set has $\\text{no minimum},$ while \n\n$$\nmax(A)=max({R}_{0}^{-})=max(-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546e6982-88ec-5d0a-a189-1dddc2c9f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t∣u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196769} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8a8f0e-02a2-5dc8-b3da-f91548763652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s∣t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196770} {:answer/id #uuid "618b0793-1781-5ac1-8614-17b3edec9e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t∣s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196771} {:answer/id #uuid "6e03f898-0bdc-5988-b599-76924029cb4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u∣t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196772}] :answer-field/id #uuid "805b7aba-fd23-5a1e-9504-4b546f2309f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196768}] :question/id #uuid "da87f877-c0b8-5528-95b1-622ab7bce569" :question/math-academy-id "q-184522" :question/problem "Let $s, t$, and $u$ be integers. If $s∣tu$ and $gcd(s, u) = 1$ then {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b36e600-edc9-5157-a498-f287a4b7cd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {csc}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300349} {:answer/id #uuid "5f88b745-9bdc-54be-b721-128fb29a0ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+5t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300450} {:answer/id #uuid "750059bf-1fbd-5d5f-8f3d-a88ccf5cd385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300542} {:answer/id #uuid "7711775d-8561-53a9-898c-4c7c3ae498a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300550} {:answer/id #uuid "c43980f9-e21c-54e7-b1c7-684a8cbfbaa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300542} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75336b5-b6e4-5687-9b0b-4bf729d1b5c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74e54a04-5388-5c59-b810-bdb93752e4b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-32415" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(t)$ given that $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({6}^{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4b2945-a4d0-5bce-b353-a84930fe45bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324233} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2d4c35-a518-5e33-9f4e-3d37542a8c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324475} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea6a043-eff8-50b5-9e35-f6f89f5b26da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324881} {:answer/id #uuid "82107318-bc44-50b3-8232-320d38affd14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325064} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa71fce-c80e-5ee7-8c4f-8aabedcefe25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324475} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b36148-a3ac-5121-9b08-a7b1f99f0bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b6fbab0f-40fd-5c65-87d5-1b56f0245d59" :question/math-academy-id "q-38825" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$ in the first quadrant. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{8}Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{8}Mr\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199559 :tutorial/content "The **minimum** of a set $S$ is the element $x_{\\min} \\in S$ such that \n\n$$\nx_{\\min} \\leq x\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in S.$ In other words, the minimum is the element of $S$ that is *smaller than or equal to* any other element.\n\nSimilarly, the **maximum** of a set $S$ is the element $x_{\\max} \\in S$ such that \n\n$$\nx \\leq x_{\\max}\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in S.$ In other words, the maximum is the element of $S$ that is *larger than or equal to* any other element.\n\nFor example, what are the minimum and maximum of the following set? \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ 2, \\: -\\sqrt{5}, \\: 3, \\: -1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNote that $-\\sqrt{5} \\approx -2.236.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(A) & =min\\{2,\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,3,\\,-1\\}=-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ max(A) & =max\\{2,\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,3,\\,-1\\}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome sets may not have a maximum or minimum. \n\nFor example, consider the semi-open interval of real numbers $B=[1, 5).$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\min(B) = \\min[1, 5) = 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, since $5 \\notin B,$ the set has no maximum!\n\nFinally, note the following:\n\n- The maximum and minimum elements *must* belong to the underlying set.\n\n- If the maximum or minimum elements exist, they are unique. In other words, there can be, at most, only one maximum and only one minimum.\n\n- If the \"less than or equal to\" operation is not defined *for all* elements of a set $S,$ then the set has no maximum or minimum. For example, the following set has no minimum and maximum:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{\\mathbb R, \\clubsuit, 2, 3, \\langle 1, 2, 3\\rangle \\big\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9377b093-e187-5c5e-9f3d-768297aaf57e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8236 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199625 :tutorial/content "You will have learned certain *divisibility tests* early in your mathematical career. One such rule is determining whether a number is divisible by $3$.\n\n> *An integer is divisible $3$ if and only if the sum of the digits is divisible by $3$.*\n\nFor example, we can use this rule to check that $2\\,387\\,465\\,652$ is divisible by $3$. The sum of the digits is given by\n\n$$\n2+3+8+7+4+6+5+6+5+2 = 48\n$$\n\nand $48$ is divisible by $3$. Therefore, $2\\,387\\,465\\,652$ is divisible by $3$.\n\nThese rules may have seemed mysterious when you first studied them. However, we can prove them using the properties of modular congruence!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's prove the \"is divisible by $3$\" rule.\n\nFirst, let $N$ be a positive integer. We'll express $N$ in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\nN = 10^{n}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_0\n$$\n\nHere, $a_{n}, \\ldots, a_2, a_{1}, a_{0}$ are the digits of the number.\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n3 \\mid N \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad 3 \\mid \\big(a_{n} + \\cdots + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_0 \\big)\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the statement in terms of congruences:\n\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $3 \\mid N \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad N \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nNow, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n> *Expressing $N$ in expanded form, we get*\n> $10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod3)$.\n\nNext, we need to show that this implies\n\n$$\na_{n} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_{0}≡0 \\mid (mod3)\n$$\n\nTo do this, recall the multiplication properties of modular arithmetic:\n\n- For any $k\\in \\mathbb N$, we have $a \\equiv b \\:\\Leftrightarrow\\: a^k\\equiv b^k \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\: n)$.\n- For any $c\\in \\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$, we have $a \\equiv b \\:\\Leftrightarrow\\: ac \\equiv bc \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\: n)$.\n\nWith this in mind, note that\n$10 \\equiv 1 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 10^k a_k \\equiv 1^k a_k \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\: 3)$.\n\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *Now, without changing the congruence, we can swap each occurrence of $10$ with $1$ on the left-hand side since they're congruent modulo $3{:}$*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod3) \\\\ (1)^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + (1)^{2}a_{2} + (1)a_{1} + a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod3) \\\\ a_{n} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write down that conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation means that*\n> $3 \\mid \\big(a_{n} + \\cdots + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_0 \\big)$.\n\nAnd we're done!\n\n**Note:** All the transformations we performed above also work in reverse, which means that the right-to-left implication is also true.\n\nWe can also use modular congruence to prove slightly more complex divisibility rules. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "66a41a25-2e81-52d5-809d-ac7873505dea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9347 :tutorial/title "Divisibility Tests"} {:answer/id #uuid "8049c5c6-6fba-5a2f-916d-8a9391f541f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a≡b(mod60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199963} {:answer/id #uuid "30d974ac-1efe-58b3-9807-3e66d9533ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a≢b(mod20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199964} {:answer/id #uuid "75be01a4-f864-5496-b503-ac3d0875155a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a≢b(mod60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199965} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1ac6d0-b045-51e3-9419-5c365a3e652c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a≢b(mod20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199973} {:answer/id #uuid "fa899658-4db3-5df7-a59b-893925fe8482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a≡b(mod20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199974} {:answer/id #uuid "6b84ab74-5991-52ff-9cb4-4cb8810eaca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a≡b(mod60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199975} {:answer/id #uuid "fc56b60c-2440-52b7-be5f-6d3c52dc60f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡y(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200108} {:answer/id #uuid "68d0d875-75a0-5825-b075-f964f95d20e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡2y(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200110} {:answer/id #uuid "0523b98c-9819-59ea-a39a-257814be2ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "gcd(26, 34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200225} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0060ca-ae7e-554f-a14f-4353a18504e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "lcm(26, 34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200226} {:answer/id #uuid "a07f0650-ebae-5a3f-8ea9-5e42f9fab249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡2(mod7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200310} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2931a2-7d4e-5160-bcbc-bba49bca9692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡3(mod7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200311} {:answer/id #uuid "0357d2ca-871f-5101-adbb-1b6fb241f1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡5(mod7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200312} {:answer/id #uuid "32ec5fa7-575c-55b2-b498-d758dfbf5ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡6(mod7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200313} {:answer/id #uuid "637d0f94-86ae-512b-826c-4b24d2e2b48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡4(mod7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200314} {:answer/id #uuid "819e0fe9-684f-5ec8-bcc8-ac56d9084b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "gcd(a_{1}, n_{1}) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200846} {:answer/id #uuid "abeaf315-0d4d-5f69-945e-22321219f366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "gcd(a_{1}, n_{1}) > 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200847} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4581aa-d082-5cad-b447-ba835ba254a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "gcd(a, n) = k > 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200927} {:answer/id #uuid "181ff3d6-c2a4-500b-8589-3680bd0a93c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "gcd(a, n) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200955} {:answer/id #uuid "47bea481-2e5c-5a92-befa-c21be1982c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$gcd(x_{1}, n) = 1$ and $gcd(x_{2}, n) = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200957} {:answer/id #uuid "a289ab73-539a-557c-b7d9-a969ed7ee11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡1(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203603} {:answer/id #uuid "d6346627-5f5d-5f2e-a44a-85503cce90e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡4(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203604} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f187ed-d120-57b3-96b5-785e6006046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡0(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203605} {:answer/id #uuid "60ceb8ca-e9be-5eb6-a1c8-a5ad90d16feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡3(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203606} {:answer/id #uuid "70366fc1-a6e8-5b44-aa5d-28ed68bf951f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡2(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203607} {:answer/id #uuid "999c1a47-894d-51fe-8363-0f05af61dc9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡8(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203609} {:answer/id #uuid "a5aa5db5-d569-5281-bcb7-74a720b8f5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡15(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203610} {:answer/id #uuid "eb73c51e-4e0e-5a53-9a14-180ed2c7eb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡10(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203611} {:answer/id #uuid "42a5c55f-203c-5f48-abdd-d0c23c139d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡16(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203612} {:answer/id #uuid "4e14fb2d-8fb6-5dc4-abaa-12d65347d630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡12(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203613} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3f5688-5de2-5cb9-bb80-f2d42d147c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡18(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203615} {:answer/id #uuid "d9da7751-4327-511b-840b-0b2a368c8d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡24(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203616} {:answer/id #uuid "07ffc3ce-ba76-532f-b043-ec2065ad7dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡17(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203617} {:answer/id #uuid "16d358e2-96d0-572d-9a7f-afb586fd654f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡16(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203618} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f6d52-1a04-55cb-809d-c4e3e469ca5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡13(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203800 :question/id #uuid "74e0e460-f92c-5d84-a4a4-f2724945e2a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-16380" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{15}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{15} x_i^2 = 9.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{9}{15}=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nThe variables $X_i$ follow a Bernoulli distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\theta(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= θ(1 - θ) + θ^{2} \\\\\n&= θ - θ^{2} + θ^{2} \\\\\n&= θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, equating the sample and theoretical first moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get the following estimate for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{5}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203801 :question/id #uuid "77223e04-b4a3-5021-8d2d-9506d8ff0aa8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16381" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim U(\\theta, \\theta+6)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter and $\\theta>0$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, x_3\\ldots,x_{5}$ you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{5} x_i^2 = 140.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{140}{5}=28\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables follow a uniform distribution $U(a,b)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= (((θ + 6) - θ)^{2})/(12) + ((θ + (θ + 6))/(2))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{12} + (θ + 3)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + θ^{2} + 6θ + 9 \\\\\n&= θ^{2} + 6θ + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical second moments and using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + 6\\widehat{θ}_{MM} + 12 &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + 6\\widehat{θ}_{MM} + 12 &= 28 \\\\\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + 6\\widehat{θ}_{MM} - 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(\\widehat{θ}_{MM} + 8)(\\widehat{θ}_{MM} - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= -8, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= 2\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since we are given that $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203805 :question/id #uuid "7daa2313-4672-5624-aac2-e3b87b6ec366" :question/math-academy-id "e-16401" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $35$ paired observations $x_{1}, x_2, \\ldots, x_{35},$ and $y_{1}, y_2, \\ldots, y_{35}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=35,\\,{S}_{yy}=133,\\,{S}_{xy}=105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\mathrm{Cov}(x, y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations $x_{1}, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n}$ and $y_{1}, y_2, \\ldots, y_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of $x_i$ and $y_i$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that $S_{xy} = 105.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{105}{35} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203810 :question/id #uuid "2a677d19-4b4c-526a-af9c-cb78a92ea88e" :question/math-academy-id "e-16438" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{12}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i^2 = 360.\n$$\n\nCalculate the method of moments estimate for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{360}{12}=30\n$$\n\nThe variables $X_i$ follow a Poisson distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\theta\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= θ + θ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the sample and theoretical second moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}^2 + \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 30\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + \\widehat{θ}_{MM} &= 30 \\\\\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + \\widehat{θ}_{MM} - 30 &= 0 \\\\\n(\\widehat{θ}_{MM} + 6)(\\widehat{θ}_{MM} - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = -6, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 5\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203811 :question/id #uuid "1aa0966b-84d2-554e-bd7d-0d7b8deff82b" :question/math-academy-id "e-16439" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{15}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Geom}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{15} x_i^2 = 30.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$. Round your final answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{30}{15} =2\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow a Geometric distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1}{\\theta}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1-\\theta}{\\theta^2}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - θ}{θ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{θ^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - θ}{θ^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the sample and theoretical second moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2-\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}}{\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}^2} = 2\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 - \\widehat{θ}_{MM})/(\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2}) &= 2 \\\\\n2 - \\widehat{θ}_{MM} &= 2\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} \\\\\n2\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + \\widehat{θ}_{MM} - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM} &= (-1 ± \\sqrt{1^{2} - 4(2)(-2)})/(2 \\cdot 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} \\approx 0.781, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} \\approx -1.281\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.781}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203813 :question/id #uuid "b20979c7-dca4-5e73-b171-12adc7b77852" :question/math-academy-id "e-16441" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim B(3, \\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2,\\ldots, x_{6}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{6} x_i^2 = 10.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{10}{6}=\\dfrac53\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables follow a binomial distribution $B(m,\\theta)$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = m\\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = m\\theta (1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= mθ(1 - θ) + m^{2}θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 3θ(1 - θ) + 3^{2}θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 3θ - 3θ^{2} + 9θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 6θ^{2} + 3θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical second moments and using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + 3\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n18\\widehat{θ}_{MM}^{2} + 9\\widehat{θ}_{MM} - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM} &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(18)(-5)})/(2(18)) \\\\\n\\widehat{θ}_{MM} &= \\frac{-3 ± 7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= -\\dfrac{5}{6}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\dfrac 13\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203814 :question/id #uuid "3fe4a424-0394-55e2-a316-97abf62aa416" :question/math-academy-id "e-16442" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{10}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\mu, \\, \\sigma^2)$, where $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are unknown parameters. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 70, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i^2 = 550.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimates $\\widehat{\\mu}_{MM}$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma^2}_{MM}$ for $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the values in the sample and the sum of squares. So, we can calculate the first and second sample moments:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} = \\frac{70}{10} = 7 \\\\\nm_{2} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} = \\frac{550}{10} = 55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow a normal distribution. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}] &= μ \\\\\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} = σ^{2} + μ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical first and second moments, using the estimators $\\widehat\\mu_{MM}$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma^2}_{MM}$ in place of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{μ}_{MM} &= 7, \\\\\n\\widehat{σ^{2}}_{MM} + \\widehat{μ}_{MM}^{2} &= 55 \\\\\n\\widehat{σ^{2}}_{MM} + 7^{2} &= 55 \\\\\n\\widehat{σ^{2}}_{MM} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203857 :question/id #uuid "ca1002c8-c615-5f7b-8876-82cf87c8864f" :question/math-academy-id "e-17349" :question/problem "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ - 4 & 10\\end{array} \\right]}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n$$\n\\nabla_X f(X)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{11}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{12}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{1 n}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{21}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{22}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{2 n}}} \\\\ \\vdots  & \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m 1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m 2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m n}}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2det(X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ - 4 & 10\\end{array} \\right]},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-5-(-6))\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203861 :question/id #uuid "2c05f4d2-abec-5a8d-a429-9fd1d4ed2374" :question/math-academy-id "e-17658" :question/problem "Three delivery drivers, Dana, Eli, and Fiona, each drove for $10$ hours. The average speed of Dana was $55\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}$, Eli’s was $65\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}$, and Fiona’s was $75\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}$. What was their average speed over the $30$ hours combined?" :question/worked-solution "Let the combined data set of all driving speeds be denoted by $S$.\n\nBecause each driver drove the same number of hours, we can compute the overall average by taking the mean of the three individual speeds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(S) &= \\frac{55 + 65 + 75}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{195}{3} \\\\\n&= 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average score the $30$ hours is\n\n$$\n65\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203862 :question/id #uuid "b25e70b9-4bb8-54c0-9fdc-895ab5100f4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-17659" :question/problem "Suppose data set $D$ contains $n_D$ data points and data set $E$ contains $10$ data points. The mean of data set $D$ is $20$, and the mean of data set $E$ is $32$. If the mean of the combined data set is $30$, what is the value of $n_D$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the data set that combines all the data points from data sets $D$ and $E$.\n\nWe introduce the following notation:\n\n- $n_D$ is the unknown size of data set $D$.\n- $\\text{mean}(D) = 20$ is the mean of data set $D$.\n- $n_E = 10$ is the size of data set $E$.\n- $\\text{mean}(E) = 32$ is the mean of data set $E$.\n- $\\text{mean}(F) = 30$ is the mean of the combined data set.\n\nThe total number of data points is\n\n$$\nn_D + n_E = n_D + 10\n$$\n\nWe compute the combined mean using a weighted average:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(F) &= (n_{D} \\cdot mean(D) + n_{E} \\cdot mean(E))/(n_{D} + n_{E}) \\\\\n30 &= \\frac{n_{D} \\cdot 20 + 10 \\cdot 32}{n_{D} + 10} \\\\\n30 &= \\frac{20n_{D} + 320}{n_{D} + 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve for $n_D$. To do this, we first multiply both sides by $(n_D + 10){:}$\n\n$$\n30(n_D + 10) = 20n_D + 320\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the distributive law to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n30n_D + 300 = 20n_D + 320\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $20n_D$ and $300$ from both sides. This gives\n\n$$\n10n_D = 20\n$$\n\nFinally, divide both sides by $10$:\n\n$$\nn_D = 2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203879 :question/id #uuid "9d0eebea-9ae9-5e15-8552-b04c465e6777" :question/math-academy-id "e-18893" :question/problem "Suppose data set $A$ contains $5$ data points, data set $B$ contains $15$ data points, and data set $C$ contains $10$ data points. The mean of data set $A$ is $24$, the mean of data set $B$ is $42$, and the mean of data set $C$ is $36$. What is the mean of the combined data set?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the data set that combines all the data points from data sets $A$, $B$, and $C$.\n\nWe introduce the following notation:\n\n- $n_A = 5$ is the size of data set $A$.\n- $\\text{mean}(A) = 24$ is the mean of data set $A$.\n- $n_B = 15$ is the size of data set $B$.\n- $\\text{mean}(B) = 42$ is the mean of data set $B$.\n- $n_C = 10$ is the size of data set $C$.\n- $\\text{mean}(C) = 36$ is the mean of data set $C$.\n\nThe total number of data points is\n\n$$\nn_A + n_B + n_C = 5 + 15 + 10 = 30\n$$\n\nWe compute the combined mean using a weighted average:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(D) &= (n_{A} \\cdot mean(A) + n_{B} \\cdot mean(B) + n_{C} \\cdot mean(C))/(n_{A} + n_{B} + n_{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 24 + 15 \\cdot 42 + 10 \\cdot 36}{5 + 10 + 15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{120 + 630 + 360}{30} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1110}{30} \\\\\n&= 37\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean of the combined data set is $37$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7edc9cb-9d6a-5de9-8182-b9ff40de7fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p = q^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199877} {:answer/id #uuid "b71768fc-351c-5d27-9e56-b3e8eaa77660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "q = p^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199878} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5a3434-223d-5e37-a0b7-d9239c02b97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a = p^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199879} {:answer/id #uuid "d146ff24-36fd-5330-ad9b-a7bdeb8ad69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a = p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199880} {:answer/id #uuid "352836a2-ee49-5cc1-ba3b-8b8e77097445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "q = p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199881}] :answer-field/id #uuid "520c256d-3b4d-5d14-902e-b5f74f3aab50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b01563f-4725-53e7-8f9f-727988826b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-184726" :question/problem "If $p$ and $q$ are primes, $gcd(a, p) = 1$ and $p \\mid aq^{3}$, then {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefb30e1-6b82-51f2-a162-1556802f8362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199931} {:answer/id #uuid "c10d746d-6806-56ba-8dd0-8af7fffb1104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199932} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4b6b3e-eabb-5039-96ae-f4010cbccc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199933} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac467ca-6eee-58ec-bd1e-cc10347b21ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199934}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ae0044af-cf8d-5682-9e1d-27d2afb8c63a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :a