> $L6$: We can rewrite this equality as $a_{1}(b_{1}u) + n_{1}(b_{1}v) = b_{1}$.\n> $L7$: So, we have that $a_{1}(b_{1}u) + n_{1}(b_{1}v)≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L8$: This implies that $a_{1}(b_{1}u)≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L9$: So, $x_{1} = b_{1}u$ is a solution of the congruence $a_{1}x≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L10$: Now, it is true that $a_{1}x_{i}≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$, for $x_{i} = x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}, 1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L11$: Therefore, $(ka_{1})x_{i}≡(kb_{1})(modkn_{1})$.\n> $L12$: Thus, $ax_{i}≡b(modn)$.\n> $L13$: This means that $x_{i}$ is solution for original congruence $ax≡b(modn)$ for each $1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L14$: Now, suppose that $i, j ∈ {1, …, k - 1}, i \\ne j$ and $x_{i}≡x_{j}(modn)$.\n> $L15$: Then, $x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}≡x_{1} + (j - 1)n_{1}(modn)$.\n> $L16$: So, $(i - 1)n_{1}≡(j - 1)n_{1}(modn_{1}k)$.\n> $L17$: And we get that $i≡j(modk)$.\n> $L18$: Therefore, $i = j$, which is a contradiction.\n> $L19$: This means that the solutions $x_{i}$ are distinct and mutually non-congruent modulo $n$.\n> $L20$: Finally, we conclude that the congruence has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from {{field-1}} in line $L1$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L8$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the fact that {{field-4}}.\n\nLine $L17$ follows from line $L16$ by the {{field-5}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "877dac5d-305d-5c5b-9550-7fe48825fe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200946} {:answer/id #uuid "36ce4d67-08aa-5543-bcbd-9c78c402ba0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "division" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200947} {:answer/id #uuid "4b72f1c6-007f-5885-a7b5-7dbda7b6a642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200948}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f91271-c4fe-5794-9776-27ef0a39