e $L5$ follows from line $L4$ by the fact that {{field-3}} and applying the {{field-4}} property of congruences."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95b87486-98e9-5607-9ab0-b7aba65dbca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201044} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c686d3-7aa7-50ff-b077-92e193c8c015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∨" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201045} {:answer/id #uuid "7edf4d7f-b3db-5493-b842-be8600b5e5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∧" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201046}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0f65b828-1e3c-59cc-8ad7-2a15e520b09f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1c492ab-b898-5812-8f33-12c01886c846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "disjunctions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201048} {:answer/id #uuid "139acff4-2145-5667-86a6-0352a3411274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "equivalences" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201049} {:answer/id #uuid "1816930f-7420-548c-a908-7a408bdd53fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "conjunctions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201050} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf994bf-a8fa-51e8-bd3f-6c96b4a3300d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "implications" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201051}] :answer-field/id #uuid "364c6c5e-ea67-5841-9d99-ad8027c79f45" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ac771a-debd-5133-a17e-0014b931c35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⇐" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201053} {:answer/id #uuid "754b7f37-5bf8-597e-94c3-e97df3a69cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⇔" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201054} {:answer/id #uuid "759f3aa3-17a3-57b8-bfc0-3ebd4f03475b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⇒" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201055}] :answer-field/id #uuid "874a89a1-0ea3-5409-b6b1-99aba11fbe4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201052}] :question/id #uuid "0222d694-e77e-57b0-8dbb-f8f941f446a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-225414" :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $35∣n$ if and only if $5∣n$ and $7∣n$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5 \\cdot 3 + 7 \\cdot (-2) = gcd(5, 7) = 1\n$$\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n$⇒$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThis is the same as proving two {{field-3}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c141d048-aa2f-53f1-8ac0-0315cb432808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "implications" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201057} {:answer/id #uuid "d769778a-6335-5f95-8b9a-1018c6438cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "equivalences" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201058} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb7256a-6c51-5622-8c0b-a7be35ead9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "conjunctions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201059} {:answer/id #uuid "30399aee-de96-5025-9e0d-bbe01b94fde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "disjunctions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201060}] :answer-field/id #uuid "70de6e42-6e78-5d20-8b97-f17025671eab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3cbd10c-66d4-53af-a269-c3e72156be42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⇐" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201062} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0f0a21-25b5-5cce-82a3-8b2795acd8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⇔" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201063} {:answer/id #uuid "348aa90d-4111-5acc-97de-fbfe446422b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⇒" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201064}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e005f169-5e95-5347-a141-566a8bcc49c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20522aa2-ded3-5f1b-a0ae-4c3d337d8ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201066} {:answer/id #uuid "20550c58-42b4-5487-b211-0137fefe4ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∧" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201067} {:answer/id #uuid "59b2218d-c9d0-55a9-b4e8-b99b3d2f5717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∨" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201068}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d21eb4fb-95dd-5bef-982a-74572e3e96cf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201065}] :question/id #uuid "2549221d-f2c9-53fc-b6fc-350cbcb4598c" :question/math-academy-id "q-225428" :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $a≡b(mod15)$ if and only if $a≡b(mod3)$ and $a≡b(mod5)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\cdot (-3) + 5 \\cdot 2 = gcd(3, 5) = 1\n$$\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n$⇐$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThis is the same as proving two {{field-3}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453923a5-93c8-5c6f-bfec-b6cdf2b78c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536389} :answer-field/id #uuid "813072e4-450e-5ca8-9448-486d80a9833f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5f36809-3c9f-5a12-9f4a-44402dbf641c" :question/math-academy-id "q-251495" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $25$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{25},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{25}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=125,\\,{S}_{yy}=37,\\,{S}_{xy}=64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=64.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{64}{25} \\\\  & =2.56.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c612d34-d7a5-57fe-b19f-09c8902eb671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202735} {:answer/id #uuid "aa839f7a-87c9-560b-88e5-c08cf23dc8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202736} {:answer/id #uuid "cc47e52f-0d1a-58fa-84ce-63b1dcb2fa66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202737} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0dff7c-26ca-56d4-a879-59086a8873c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202738} {:answer/id #uuid "3de04d07-885e-57d7-8370-6cda775fea3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202739}] :answer-field/id #uuid "49859b68-0851-5e4a-a714-d2d38bb8fe1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202734}] :question/id #uuid "f50d6131-f9a9-5565-991c-674f27f5f11c" :question/math-academy-id "q-252131" :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{8}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$, where $θ$ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{8}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(8)x_{i}^{2} = 4.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{θ}_{MM}$ for $θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d9e873-8450-5a14-9062-11377affe05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202741} {:answer/id #uuid "af7094a3-4976-5c33-9dd4-5fe943dd9c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202742} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b0f98b-8b47-50a1-a46b-f883d2e753a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202743} {:answer/id #uuid "603e454a-b028-5dee-ae9f-4071ce6dc745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202744} {:answer/id #uuid "6f36dd02-3a3c-5575-81d3-427712ede7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\widehat{θ}_{MM} = \\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202745}] :answer-field/id #uuid "05a535cb-c686-54ad-b516-9a485711a82a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202740}] :question/id #uuid "6d4b5d01-8e3b-553f-a1ba-affabf4a0817" :question/math-academy-id "q-252136" :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{14}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$, where $θ$ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{14}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(14)x_{i} = \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{θ}_{MM}$ for $θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c47d4b1-a923-5d13-9ea2-f25e49d50c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203338} {:answer/id #uuid "23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533229} {:answer/id #uuid "3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533286} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533683} {:answer/id #uuid "efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533683} :answer-field/id #uuid "18959201-dc75-5139-b452-5ab59d2a87d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8781dff9-d5f6-57b2-92f1-799f898568b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-264512" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{4-1} & \\frac{1}{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4-1} & -\\frac{2}{4-1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a289ab73-539a-557c-b7d9-a969ed7ee11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡1(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203603} {:answer/id #uuid "d6346627-5f5d-5f2e-a44a-85503cce90e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡4(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203604} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f187ed-d120-57b3-96b5-785e6006046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡0(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203605} {:answer/id #uuid "60ceb8ca-e9be-5eb6-a1c8-a5ad90d16feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡3(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203606} {:answer/id #uuid "70366fc1-a6e8-5b44-aa5d-28ed68bf951f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡2(mod5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203607}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee8f6d9-2dac-5145-b0c1-da38fcbf1331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203602}] :question/id #uuid "6450b556-5428-57e6-8599-662c800d0c0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-78146" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $4x≡3(mod5)$ using trial and error."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999c1a47-894d-51fe-8363-0f05af61dc9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡8(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203609} {:answer/id #uuid "a5aa5db5-d569-5281-bcb7-74a720b8f5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡15(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203610} {:answer/id #uuid "eb73c51e-4e0e-5a53-9a14-180ed2c7eb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡10(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203611} {:answer/id #uuid "42a5c55f-203c-5f48-abdd-d0c23c139d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡16(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203612} {:answer/id #uuid "4e14fb2d-8fb6-5dc4-abaa-12d65347d630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡12(mod22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203613}] :answer-field/id #uuid "622215e5-9181-55cf-9dc6-28cf112ccf9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203608}] :question/id #uuid "ddfa78b3-31e1-589a-bafb-55c4060ce9f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78169" :question/problem "Solve the modular equation $3x≡8(mod22)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3f5688-5de2-5cb9-bb80-f2d42d147c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡18(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203615} {:answer/id #uuid "d9da7751-4327-511b-840b-0b2a368c8d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡24(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203616} {:answer/id #uuid "07ffc3ce-ba76-532f-b043-ec2065ad7dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡17(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203617} {:answer/id #uuid "16d358e2-96d0-572d-9a7f-afb586fd654f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡16(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203618} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f6d52-1a04-55cb-809d-c4e3e469ca5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≡13(mod55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203619}] :answer-field/id #uuid "493b639d-9e27-5dcf-95c4-d46556087bdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "065e2437-df04-58ff-85af-9bdbbfad9639" :question/math-academy-id "q-78184" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $14x≡6(mod55)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205159 :tutorial/content "In general, the probability mass function of a hypergeometric random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(N,K,n)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{K}{x} \\right) \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N - K}{n - x} \\right)}}{ {\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{n} \\right)}}} , & \\text{if } max ( 0 , n + K - N ) \\le  x \\le  min ( n , K ) \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's break down the structure of this formula:\n\n- The factor $\\displaystyle \\binom{K}{x}$ in the numerator gives the number of ways getting $x$ successes out of $K$.\n- The factor $\\displaystyle \\binom{N-K}{n-x}$ in the numerator gives the number of ways getting $n-x$ failures out of $N-K$.\n- The value $\\displaystyle \\binom{N}{n}$ in the denominator gives the number of ways to select $n$ objects out of $N$.\n\nWe also have the following upper-bound:\n\n$$\nX \\leq \\min(n, K)\n$$\n\nThis states that the number of successes cannot be larger than $n$ (the number of draws) and $K$ (the number of items considered successes).\n\nAdditionally, we have the following lower-bound:\n\n$$\n\\max(0, n+K-N) \\leq X\n$$\n\nIf the number of possible failures $N-K$ is smaller than $n$ (the number of draws), we must get *at least* $n-(N-K)$ successes.\n\nFor example, suppose there are ${\\color{blue}5}$ candies in a bag, where there are ${\\color{red}4}$ red,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{red}4} = 1\n$$\n\nyellow. We randomly select ${\\color{violet}3}$ candies and count the number of red ones.\n\nConsider the worst-case scenario where we select the smallest possible number of red candies. One way to do this is to select all the yellow candies before selecting red. We cannot avoid selecting some red candies since the number of draws $({\\color{violet}3})$ exceeds the number of yellow candies\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{red}4} = 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we must select a minimum of\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}3} - \\left({\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{red}4}\\right) = 2\n$$\n\nred candies." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d5dd2fa-5b75-5927-b5b3-bf7609e70b8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10951 :tutorial/title "The Structure of the Probability Mass Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205185 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two data sets, $X$ and $Y$, each containing $8$ data points:\n\n- the mean of data set $X$ is $44$, and\n- the mean of data set $Y$ is $36$.\n\nWhat is the mean of the combined data set?\n\nSince both $X$ and $Y$ contain *the same number of data points,* the mean of the combined data set $Z$ is simply the average of the two means:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean}(Z) =\\dfrac{\\textrm{mean}(X)+\\textrm{mean}(Y)}{2}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, in our example, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(Z) &= \\frac{44 + 36}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{80}{2} \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important that the two data sets have the *same number of data points*! When the sizes differ, this formula *no longer applies!* We'll see how to handle that situation later in the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f69c9f5-8953-565a-a4d8-cc1c7082454b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12105 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f(A) = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{11}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{12}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{1 n}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{21}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{22}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{2 n}}} \\\\ \\vdots  & \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{m 1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{m 2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {a}_{m n}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A.$ It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n$$\nX = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}.\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}).\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & -\\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(X)}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n$$\nX = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & - 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6-5 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =-\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output $f(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))$ would change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change." :tutorial/id #uuid "4ace4da9-e2b2-56f1-b064-78e25344a30e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205209 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the case where the data sets have different sizes.\n\nSuppose we have data sets $X$ and $Y$ with the following information.\n\n- $n_X = 6$ is the size of data set $X$.\n- $\\text{mean}(X) = 24$ is the mean of data set $X$.\n- $n_Y = 14$ is the size of data set $Y$.\n- $\\text{mean}(Y) = 30$ is the mean of data set $Y$.\n\nLet $Z$ be the data set that combines all the data points from data sets $X$ and $Y$. What is the mean of the *combined* data set $Z$?\n\nThe total number of data points is\n\n$$\nn_X + n_Y = 6 + 14 = 20\n$$\n\nWe compute the combined mean using a so-called **weighted average:**\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean}(Z)= \\dfrac{n_X \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(X) + n_Y \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(Y)}{n_X + n_Y}\n$$\n\nWe'll explain why this formula gives the combined mean at the end of the lesson.\n\nIn our example, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(Z) &= \\frac{6 \\cdot 24 + 14 \\cdot 30}{6 + 14} \\\\\n&= \\frac{144 + 420}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{564}{20} \\\\\n&= 28.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use a weighted average to find the combined mean of three data sets." :tutorial/id #uuid "c1170a70-d6b4-5a45-9172-9d64e0c35e6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13441 :tutorial/title "Finding a Combined Mean Using a Weighted Average"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205210 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have data sets $A$ and $B$, and we create a new data set $C$ by combining all the elements from $A$ and $B$. We're given the following information:\n\n- $n_A = 4$ is the size of data set $A$.\n- $n_B = 16$ is the size of data set $B$.\n- $\\text{mean}(B) = 40$ is the mean of data set $B$.\n- $\\text{mean}(C) = 37$ is the mean of the combined data set.\n\nNotice that we do not know $\\textrm{mean}(A)$. How can we find this using the information given above?\n\nThe total number of data points is\n\n$$\nn_A + n_B = 4 + 16 = 20\n$$\n\nThe combined mean is found using a weighted average:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean}(C) = \\dfrac{n_A \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(A) + n_B \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(B)}{n_A + n_B}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known numbers, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(C) &= (n_{A} \\cdot mean(A) + n_{B} \\cdot mean(B))/(n_{A} + n_{B}) \\\\\n37 &= (4 \\cdot mean(A) + 16 \\cdot 40)/(4 + 16) \\\\\n37 &= (4 \\cdot mean(A) + 640)/(20)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve for $\\textrm{mean}(A)$. To do this, we first multiply both sides by $20$. This gives\n\n$$\n740 = 4 \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(A) + 640\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $640$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n100 = 4 \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(A)\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean}(A) = 25\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a similar problem where one of the data sets contains an unknown number of elements." :tutorial/id #uuid "fd75e23a-86c7-5d94-b34e-9246a3c834ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13442 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Smaller Mean"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205211 :tutorial/content "Let\n\n$$\nX = \\{x_1, \\dots, x_{n_X}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY = \\{y_1, \\dots, y_{n_Y}\\}\n$$\n\ncontain $n_X$ and $n_Y$ elements, respectively.\nThen, the means of $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean}(X) = \\dfrac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{n_X}}{n_X}, \\qquad \\textrm{mean}(Y) = \\dfrac{y_1 + y_2 + \\cdots + y_{n_Y}}{n_Y}\n$$\n\nThe total sum of values in the combined set $Z$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{sum}(Z) = (x_1 + \\cdots + x_{n_X}) + (y_1 + \\cdots + y_{n_Y}) = n_X \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(X) + n_Y \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(Y)\n$$\n\nand the total number of values is $n_X + n_Y$. Therefore, the mean of the combined set is the weighted average of the individual means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(Z) &= (n_{X} \\cdot mean(X) + n_{Y} \\cdot mean(Y))/(n_{X} + n_{Y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "164507fb-91a6-5993-a351-445543d0d657" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13528 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula With the Weighted Average"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205212 :tutorial/content "Let\n\n$$\nX = \\{x_1, \\dots, x_n\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY = \\{y_1, \\dots, y_n\\}\n$$\n\neach contain $n$ elements.\nThen, the means of $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean}(X) = \\dfrac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n}, \\qquad \\textrm{mean}(Y) = \\dfrac{y_1 + y_2 + \\cdots + y_n}{n}\n$$\n\nThe total sum of values in the combined set $Z$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{sum}(Z) = (x_1 + \\cdots + x_n) + (y_1 + \\cdots + y_n) = n \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(X) + n \\cdot \\textrm{mean}(Y)\n$$\n\nand the total number of values is\n\n$$\nn + n = 2n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean of the combined set is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(Z) &= (n \\cdot mean(X) + n \\cdot mean(Y))/(2n) \\\\\n&= (n(mean(X) + mean(Y)))/(2n) \\\\\n&= (mean(X) + mean(Y))/(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2019b6ca-e70f-5163-8de4-53cb83f691c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13529 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula for Equally Sized Data Sets"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208559 :question/id #uuid "8ccc1ad2-f845-5c9c-b346-eb0065dc7106" :question/math-academy-id "e-22019" :question/problem "$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nGiven the matrices above, write $M\\mathbf{u}^T \\odot (\\mathbf{v} - \\mathbf{u}^T)$ as a matrix product." :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\odot \\mathbf{b} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{a})\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$$\nM\\mathbf{u}^T\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} - \\mathbf{u}^T\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n-\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that they are of the same size; hence, their Hadamard product is well-defined. We conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMu^{T}⊙(v - u^{T}) &= diag(Mu^{T})(v - u^{T}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209961 :tutorial/content "In machine learning and linear algebra, it’s often convenient to express the Hadamard product of two vectors using matrix multiplication. If $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\odot \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v}) \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{u}) \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix product\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nwe notice that the left matrix is diagonal. So, we can write it as a Hadamard product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= diag\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's see a concrete example of expressing a Hadamard product as a matrix product in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "c1cd3e4f-790d-5bdc-a6de-4c5c50eb3d9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14136 :tutorial/title "Connections Between the Hadamard Product and Matrix Product"} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9cceca-b78f-514f-9e6f-e2881756667c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.0\\frac{km}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212581} {:answer/id #uuid "f550cff3-f88f-5626-82d0-c9db9a2a611b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.6\\frac{km}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212582} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5b1126-a233-57d9-86a9-3cb246e3701e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.3\\frac{km}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212583} {:answer/id #uuid "4467d3a3-667f-5cc8-9a0d-04d826c59793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.1\\frac{km}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212584} {:answer/id #uuid "5b36fe29-e8a2-5b3d-9f99-dc49f73ea5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.4\\frac{km}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212585} {:answer/id #uuid "058c5aa1-ee6c-56bc-a2fc-6cb6460b6a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.7\\frac{ft}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212587} {:answer/id #uuid "3991383c-ad7e-5c14-a16b-b296ee471fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.2\\frac{ft}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212588} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5196ee-5024-57fb-ba8b-81b1485ad752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.9\\frac{ft}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212589} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1f902a-7866-5527-9845-08217aee9dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.8\\frac{ft}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212590} {:answer/id #uuid "77dbe74b-1ab1-5a0a-bda0-9a12afde382c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.6\\frac{ft}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214332 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following dataset:\n\n$$\n30, \\; 30, \\; 30, \\; 30\n$$\n\nNote that the mean of this data set is $30{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean &= \\frac{30 + 30 + 30 + 30}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot 30}{4} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose a new data point with a value of $\\color{blue}24$ is added to create a new dataset.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{24}}, \\; 30, \\; 30, \\; 30, \\; 30\n$$\n\nIn this example, the new value $({{\\color{blue}{24}}})$ is *less than* the original mean ($30$). As a result, the mean will *decrease* when this data point is included. Therefore, the mean of the new dataset is *smaller* than the mean of the original dataset.\n\nWe can convince ourselves that this is true by calculating the mean of the second data set and showing that it's smaller than $30{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean(new) &= \\frac{24 + 30 + 30 + 30 + 30}{5} \\\\\n&= 28.8 \\\\\n&< 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the mean of the new dataset *is* smaller than that of the original, as predicted!" :tutorial/id #uuid "54c0e9ef-241e-553f-a43d-4a2808988f6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14219 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216841 :question/id #uuid "958852d3-a6f3-57ae-bbda-8c7f0ff52033" :question/math-academy-id "e-19654" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b752a3bb5f5ce53216e6765914da7d306acbf3063ca20293b503e69727a093dd.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the **smallest possible difference** between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(\\text{mean}(B)) & =\\frac{220+342+590+427+189}{4+6+10+7+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1768}{30} \\\\  & \\approx 58.933\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216877 :question/id #uuid "1911d0db-b5a2-5ab8-a596-47b7351942da" :question/math-academy-id "e-19708" :question/problem "If $1\\leq p \\leq 5$ and $6\\leq q \\leq 13$, what's the smallest possible value of $pq$?" :question/worked-solution "To minimize the product of two positive numbers $p$ and $q$, we must multiply the smallest possible value of $p$ by the smallest possible value of $q$.\n\nMore precisely, if $a, b, c$, and $d$ are positive numbers such that\n\n$$\na\\leq p\\leq b\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad c\\leq q\\leq d\n$$\n\nthen the minimum value of $pq$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(pq) &= min(p) \\cdot min(q) \\\\\n&= ac\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $\\min(p) = 1$ and\n\n$$\n\\min(q) = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nmin(p) \\cdot min(q) = 1 \\cdot 6 = 6\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216878 :question/id #uuid "a7d8425c-67f2-5afd-859f-9b69e1ea1bfc" :question/math-academy-id "e-19709" :question/problem "If $3\\leq u \\leq 15$ and $4\\leq v \\leq 27$, what's the smallest possible value of $\\dfrac{u}{v}$?" :question/worked-solution "To minimize the quotient of two positive numbers $u$ and $v$, we take the smallest possible value of $u$ and divide by the largest possible value of $v$.\n\nMore precisely, if $a, b, c$, and $d$ are positive numbers such that\n\n$$\na\\leq u\\leq b\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad c\\leq v\\leq d\n$$\n\nthen the minimum value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{u}{v}\n$$\n\nequals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin\\left(\\frac{u}{v}\\right) &= (min(u))/(max(v)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{a}{d}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $\\min(u) = 3$ and\n\n$$\n\\max(v) = 27\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(min(u))/(max(v)) = \\frac{3}{27} = \\frac{1}{9}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216885 :question/id #uuid "af865b17-f05f-5cf9-951e-acf477c650c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-19719" :question/problem "If $a\\in [m,n]$ and $b\\in [p,q]$ are real numbers, find $\\min(a-b)$." :question/worked-solution "To minimize the difference of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the smallest possible value of $a$ and subtract the largest possible value of $b$.\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\nm\\leq a\\leq n\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad p\\leq b\\leq q\n$$\n\nthen the minimum value of $a-b$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(a - b) &= min(a) - max(b) \\\\\n&= m - q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de8e52b-1f29-5f4c-8bf5-003e884b7c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "c + d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215604} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cd9d9b-2301-53c7-ab06-73b2bed74aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b + d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215605} {:answer/id #uuid "b52047c7-1b9e-5450-8c45-3026deeaa91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a + b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215606} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d26eba-aec0-5adf-8c6c-47b1c7d1124b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b + c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215607} {:answer/id #uuid "fffbdb59-6474-5630-8121-3e9658ed5235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a + c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215608}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a650f1b-39fd-5728-89a0-1a65b8209c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215603}] :question/id #uuid "94a13caa-855d-5e71-95ee-708f3d2aeaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-311926" :question/problem "If $x ∈ [a, b]$ and $y ∈ [c, d]$ are real numbers, then $max(x + y) =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8882514-dd82-5324-907b-87645ce1e310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a - d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215630} {:answer/id #uuid "e7588239-c5d3-5b72-8b61-bc5a10e8cfa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b - d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215631} {:answer/id #uuid "04d7ca8a-4d27-5a02-925f-4e7bde92862c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "c - d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215632} {:answer/id #uuid "5df263e5-b679-5ba0-ae95-08548fe16b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215633} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfc2bea-c776-522b-a482-312037c44971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215634}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebe4571-130d-5f9e-82cf-4a1ecf66c953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215629}] :question/id #uuid "c642b81e-70eb-5777-8c8e-d63c0bf1265e" :question/math-academy-id "q-312175" :question/problem "If $x ∈ [a, b]$ and $y ∈ [c, d]$ are real numbers, then $max(x - y) =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218242 :tutorial/content "Finally, we'll discuss how to bound the *difference* between two statistics. This is often useful when comparing two data sets, and we want to see how large or small differences in their metrics could be.\n\n![](images/89/898b8ceb1d145a843b51ff173c5471675b403189781793fc6156222c844d4c23.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. What is the *largest possible difference* between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$? We can write this symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B) - \\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the *largest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *largest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *smallest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the largest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our data set as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the *largest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.\nValue $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{max}}$$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$$80-84$$2$$84$$2\\cdot 84 = 168$$84-88$$3$$88$$3\\cdot 88 = 264$$88-92$$7$$92$$7\\cdot 92 = 644$$92-96$$5$$96$$5\\cdot 96 = 480$$96-100$$3$$100$$3\\cdot 100 = 300$\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}max(\\text{mean}(B)) & =\\frac{168+264+644+480+300}{2+3+7+5+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1856}{20} \\\\  & =92.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Next, we compute the smallest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $36 - 39$. Therefore, the *smallest* possible value of the median is\n$\\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 36$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the largest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(mean(B)) - min(median(A)) &= 92.8 - 36 \\\\\n&= 56.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bea75204-7429-5b8b-946d-f0cd30943076" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13749 :tutorial/title "Bounding the Difference Between Two Statistics"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218265 :tutorial/content "We need to be more careful when finding the maximum or minimum of a difference or a quotient!\n\nFor example, suppose we have two numbers $a$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n1\\leq a \\leq 12\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4\\leq b \\leq 8\n$$\n\nWhat is the largest possible value of $a - b$?\n\nWe can answer this question by applying the following principle:\n\n> *To **maximize** the difference of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **largest possible** value of $a$ and subtract the **smallest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nTake a moment to appreciate why this must be true. If we're trying to maximize a difference, we want to start with the largest number possible and then subtract as little as possible from it.\n\nSo, let's compute the largest possible value of $a$, the smallest possible value of $b$, and find their difference:\n\n- Since $1\\leq a \\leq 12$, the largest possible value of $a$ is $12$.\n$\\max(a) = 12$\n- Since $4\\leq b \\leq 8$, the smallest possible value of $b$ is $4$.\n$\\min(b) = 4$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(a - b) &= max(a) - min(b) \\\\\n&= 12 - 4 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rule for maximizing a *quotient* is similar, though it only works for positive numbers:\n\n> *To **maximize** the quotient of two **positive** numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **largest possible** value of $a$ and divide by the **smallest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nAgain, take a moment to appreciate why this must be true. We want the dividend to be as large as possible, while the divisor should be as small as possible.\n\nSo, in this example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) &= (max(a))/(min(b)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{4} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9444e1f-e03b-5ad8-9111-89a19a16fe6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13781 :tutorial/title "Maximizing Differences and Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218294 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two numbers $a$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n1\\leq a \\leq 7\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4\\leq b \\leq 8\n$$\n\nWhat is the largest possible value of $a + b$?\n\nWe can answer this question by applying the following principle:\n\n> *To **maximize** the sum of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we must add the largest possible value of $a$ to the largest possible value of $b$.*\n\nSo, let's compute the largest possible values of $a$ and $b$, and find their sum:\n\n- Since $1\\leq a \\leq 7$, the largest possible value of $a$ is $7$. We'll write this as follows:\n$\\max(a) = 7$\n- Since $4\\leq b \\leq 8$, the largest possible value of $b$ is $8$.\n$\\max(b) = 8$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(a + b) &= max(a) + max(b) \\\\\n&= 7 + 8 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rule for maximizing a *product* is similar, though it only works for positive numbers:\n\n> *To **maximize** the product of two **positive** numbers $a$ and $b$, we must multiply the largest possible value of $a$ by the largest possible value of $b$.*\n\nSo, in this example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(ab) &= max(a) \\cdot max(b) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: We can think of $\\max(a)$ as a *function* that takes a number $a$ as input and returns the largest possible value of $a$ as output, and similarly for $\\max(b)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d0b009e-eee3-5987-a3a7-c26e01aab43a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13833 :tutorial/title "Maximizing a Sum or Product"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218295 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our intervals once more.\n\n$$\n1\\leq a \\leq 12\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4\\leq b \\leq 8\n$$\n\nWhat is the smallest possible value of $a - b$?\n\nWe can answer this question by applying the following principle:\n\n> *To **minimize** the difference of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **smallest possible** value of $a$ and subtract the **largest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nAgain, take a moment to appreciate why this must be true. If we're trying to minimize a difference, we want to start with the smallest number possible and then subtract as much as possible from it.\n\nSo, let's compute the smallest possible value of $a$, the largest possible value of $b$, and find their difference:\n\n- Since $1\\leq a \\leq 12$, the smallest possible value of $a$ is $1$.\n$\\min(a) = 1$\n- Since $4\\leq b \\leq 8$, the largest possible value of $b$ is $8$.\n$\\max(b) = 8$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(a - b) &= min(a) - max(b) \\\\\n&= 1 - 8 \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rule for minimizing a *quotient* is similar, though it only works for positive numbers:\n\n> *To **minimize** the quotient of two **positive** numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **smallest possible** value of $a$ and divide by the **largest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nAgain, take a moment to appreciate why this must be true.\n\nSo, in this example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) &= (min(a))/(max(b)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\\\\n&= 0.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b0924a5a-26c8-553e-af55-7958a960fd70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13834 :tutorial/title "Minimizing Differences and Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218296 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our two intervals once more:\n\n$$\n1\\leq a \\leq 7\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4\\leq b \\leq 8\n$$\n\nWhat is the *smallest* possible value of $a + b$?\n\nWe can answer this question by applying the following principle:\n\n> *To **minimize** the sum of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we must add the smallest possible value of $a$ to the smallest possible value of $b$.*\n\nSo, let's compute the smallest possible values of $a$ and $b$, and find their sum:\n\n- Since $1\\leq a \\leq 7$, the smallest possible value of $a$ is $1$. We'll write this as follows:\n$\\min(a) = 1$\n- Since $4\\leq b \\leq 8$, the smallest possible value of $b$ is $4$.\n$\\min(b) = 4$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(a + b) &= min(a) + min(b) \\\\\n&= 1 + 4 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rule for minimizing a *product* is similar, though it only works for positive numbers:\n\n> *To **minimize** the product of two **positive** numbers $a$ and $b$, we must multiply the smallest possible value of $a$ by the smallest possible value of $b$.*\n\nSo, in this example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(ab) &= min(a) \\cdot min(b) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "28e5a927-c180-59ea-bb0f-522b79b3d9e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13835 :tutorial/title "Minimizing a Sum or Product"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221166 :question/id #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21582" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(9\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t+36\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡36\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡36\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221184 :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is $\\approx \\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X X}}}.$\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is $\\approx \\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X X}}}.$\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $\\approx \\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X X}}}.$\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X X}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =4(0.4)^{3}+(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.064+1\\cdot 0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.256+0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.281\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.282.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+1\\cdot 0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.032+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 2^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈8.380\\,952… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(8.380\\,95…) \\\\ p & ≈3.067….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221185 :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.987… \\\\ p & ≈2.987….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938281c5-3c7c-5056-9193-18ad0f6430cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300703} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a73c5a-fe2a-56fd-9398-1d8d7a48427b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c55f9ab8-e7b6-5ec8-9696-dd445096cc3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-322731" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec36de9-38d5-53dd-bc19-76c5d3cd02c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300015} {:answer/id #uuid "206ca4c1-d460-546c-9921-999db6cdcd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answ