{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d0bc9b3-fb9a-5c65-810c-3439f828b300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300511} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2c739d-8b06-5026-9082-212742c45529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300512} {:answer/id #uuid "c1107ce9-7656-5627-b9fb-a8ce8b8b2411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300930} :answer-field/id #uuid "57fc7241-73f5-56dc-bff8-6667181c0047" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9b6c6b-0f14-5c0b-aeb7-b1cc6915f073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300013} {:answer/id #uuid "1cda2bf2-3bbc-5b35-96d8-fa49e2d816d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300095} {:answer/id #uuid "b92cd1ee-6a33-52a8-85f0-f31f1e839848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300095} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee5a383-d4be-5f73-abb1-0575dc5afdb8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdbdb57-3e0a-5343-8d86-26fcec65cc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300453} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc420c5-048b-549f-9bcf-b88c86349b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300510} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd16590-ec9a-514d-a8a4-0505858ff8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300453} :answer-field/id #uuid "ada24269-91ea-59c0-8960-3ff339a67aac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "611cf62a-767c-5a58-9e5c-4ad812d2faf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-322737" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-6{x}_{1}(t)+7{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. $\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(7{e}^{-4t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{-4t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+7\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (-2{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot (-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot (-2{e}^{t})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7{e}^{-4t})\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3958bcf4-f7e7-5bb1-8701-7e4f15f72bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305700} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b44415-f6c8-512a-87d5-a64e5dd7b1a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7bfec16a-0969-50de-9ad7-04282efcdd7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333695" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20c43b-aebe-57e6-9524-dcd0d1e5d276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305899} {:answer/id #uuid "4e918a44-c564-5fed-a0ad-e307dda2bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb35312-b7af-5bdf-9c23-68c4c07a3a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45bf27-6034-5a2b-b224-609af04300e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1834450-0072-5bfe-83d8-37d9f92b8791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} :answer-field/id #uuid "346676e0-48f8-5066-ac65-2d01b43c9ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1959d41f-52be-52c1-871e-b699293b1307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.769" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305381} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec87537-6fa0-5eda-86d9-4554032f1a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305444} {:answer/id #uuid "37d717c9-2678-5acd-9e7b-470aba738582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17.671" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305687} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8bd609-2c76-56f5-ab76-2b1b93bba25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305707} {:answer/id #uuid "9a41b78c-ad27-5209-95b9-87e703d4f1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.864" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305381} :answer-field/id #uuid "02c14e40-2f8c-5353-919f-1e2b81c06a0a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307524} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345d0530-b807-5eb6-8028-e9e60219419a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.025" :d