 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305323} {:answer/id #uuid "64767d88-6051-5480-91b6-c41e9bf49c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306121} {:answer/id #uuid "74ede194-9cb5-53af-a0a1-2e82afd1d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306264} {:answer/id #uuid "83d61e9d-685a-5a5f-a732-336138f71219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306408} {:answer/id #uuid "86751a4c-86a9-5438-b05a-af0255c79861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306264} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7dc62ec-6aa3-5187-b8d0-4ca8606aada0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "36473f35-3093-5439-832b-c821da150814" :question/math-academy-id "q-333706" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =7(0.5)^{4}-2(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0.062\\,5-2\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =7(0.25)^{4}-2(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (0.003\\,906…)-2\\cdot (0.000\\,976…) \\\\  & =0.025\\,39… \\\\  & ≈0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.375$ to $0.025\\,39….$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.375}{(0.025\\,39…)}=14.769\\,23…≈14.769.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈14.769\\,23… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(14.769\\,23…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.884… \\\\ p & ≈2.884….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8335968c-9d4c-5fbb-9ffa-75dcaa53d606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b01ba2-8f40-5f19-b36b-a5bc0a007ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220593} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f3e3a-b149-5084-b607-3cd5fd0d18db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220594} {:answer/id #uuid "6493f73f-365f-5404-a5da-5e40752fbe4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592} :answer-field/id #uuid "93036466-1c3c-5e59-947c-e8ff8d157797" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220591} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e243901-1d01-5ec9-9cdc-6cb103946683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442487} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcb761f-eaed-55a9-ad0a-3da04843c7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442494} {:answer/id #uuid "4832e2c7-75c3-5827-8346-bf8d89ce6137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442505} {:answer/id #uuid "f947d07b-6d00-57b7-a3fe-19a08102e66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442494} :answer-field/id #uuid "dccda4ab-5506-5030-89e0-e232b37f3a8b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967d6780-0d69-5d91-b012-b91bfef745e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220602} {:answer/id #uuid "e753d893-e42e-50d3-bb33-dac9396f5d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the zero factor theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220604} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2aa18-1cb1-5685-99ca-77c79bd3efdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the properties of determinants" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220605} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6b0878-88fc-5098-98f5-9eeb896f07d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220605} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb71a74-1627-56af-a5ab-668a47e38b61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "920ced52-32ff-52a3-a836-fb31a23c82e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333722" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the properties of determinants}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c17e72-3c87-54d1-b6ea-4917ff8ed4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305213} {:answer/id #uuid "43802c24-c488-5561-8613-243e888225d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305817} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4bf1d2-a687-5329-9cec-692f5c3dee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305817} :answer-field/id #uuid "275fd4c0-e684-55ac-b1f8-2ce4001d2404" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307645} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1596630b-dfa5-5b06-9030-d746c97f49c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305339} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe672b2-61c5-5f8c-88e4-a03226af332b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305928} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1f500e-c6df-5443-b038-e5821c9410d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306025} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a78c53-6d41-504d-978d-6c4bcf1fb567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305928} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4c0e5d-37e0-5d52-ac86-71b59366c805" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484714c7-7cfd-538b-b8f1-2d9ed998c4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305854} {:answer/id #uuid "85ed7377-d563-5a3e-8275-ffdbb574d008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306429} {:answer/id #uuid "f47fa7e4-3794-5897-8ea1-94f011683cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307414} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b0f4b3-35b9-5fd0-8e74-64a4e4ef31d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36b5721c-1260-58ca-a074-b5fdeb826aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333862" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df52e550-d288-52d8-9356-52b87bad5743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220679} {:answer/id #uuid "79ecc47c-e22d-5be8-bd7a-51040d9ca7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220680}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0c50116e-4367-5915-b798-f3246597df5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82f11b0-8f23-58ab-b3d4-66f2815c0309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220682} {:answer/id #uuid "52970cf2-6527-5298-97a9-828edf1cfb57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220683}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6ae71b-329a-53e7-aa99-f620bc843042" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220681} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79719874-96fe-5073-b494-dcf6d5a39471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379457} {:answer/id #uuid "c087f033-2079-5492-a778-ebf13b0aa243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379458} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a5f2a6-4329-523f-bdfa-bd93fc6dcb08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4fc12ef-e62d-5f25-b2d5-c621b1f9b0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379460} {:answer/id #uuid "6e30df69-6079-5d65-a898-c04251f0c0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379460} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec905529-c1ae-530d-9318-a6613bebacc1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1cf1c26-42ff-5096-9a83-58747b0e81ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379463} {:answer/id #uuid "79ec9a96-7661-5c4f-b3a2-58eb54e26370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a,b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379464} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1dc870-11d2-5899-bb2e-437bd24a1e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379465} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc547c72-11aa-5bb3-944d-9aee4e86879b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379462} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de54d068-cb7f-5e7c-9923-b0162bfa3009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379467} {:answer/id #uuid "578fb3c2-e5c5-585c-b268-f9d617528ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379468} {:answer/id #uuid "5f07d735-41c4-5865-8a02-bedbb00fc27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379469} {:answer/id #uuid "8e42122a-a7c6-53f8-bb24-d851a762eddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379470} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc71b2be-f2f3-5c7c-8407-4663197d02db" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfa5a288-aff6-5b73-8541-d44328af6f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379472} {:answer/id #uuid "45cfe385-a82e-56cf-82a9-7ee704351d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379473} {:answer/id #uuid "658cc981-bde6-5d8c-9ce2-1b76bdbe9b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a(t),b(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379473} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5671624-2cf1-56c4-9357-41d067291c4e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7f64c5a-6afb-522f-9e51-c80d65a53800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379476} {:answer/id #uuid "508da7b5-1fe5-5acd-ba4b-5eb143716fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379477} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e0058d-cad5-5a7d-9be8-d8ba13bc9489" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c608e74-fcfe-5599-a5f9-f981f117823c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334405" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot {e}^{3t}+1\\cdot {e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\cdot {e}^{3t}+2\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ 1\\cdot {e}^{t}+2\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(5{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (5{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}15{e}^{3t} \\\\ 15{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 5{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\cdot 5{e}^{3t}+2\\cdot 5{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}15{e}^{3t} \\\\ 15{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}15{e}^{3t} \\\\ 15{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{3t} & 5{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & 5{e}^{3t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{3t})(5{e}^{3t})-(5{e}^{3t})({e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{6t}-5{e}^{6t} \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222498 :tutorial/content "Each step of a numerical method produces a local truncation error $\\tau,$ which, over multiple steps, accumulates into a global truncation error $e.$ Both LTE and GTE depend on step size $\\Delta x.$\n\nThe **order** of a numerical method describes how fast these errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\nA numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the *largest positive* integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e\\left(\\frac{Δx}{k}\\right) & ≈K{\\left(\\frac{Δx}{k}\\right)}^{p}=\\frac{K(Δx)^{p}}{{k}^{p}}≈\\frac{e(Δx)}{{k}^{p}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate with an example. Suppose a numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.21,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.21) = 0.785.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.07,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.07) = 0.010.$\n\nUsing this data, we can estimate the order of the numerical method.\n\nThe step size decreases from $0.21$ to $0.07.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.21}{0.07} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 3^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.785$ to $0.010.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.785}{0.010} = 78.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈78.5 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(78.5) \\\\ p & ≈3.97….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$\n\nBut why is order important? Well, a higher order means that the error decreases faster as $\\Delta x$ decreases. As a result, higher-order methods can achieve a target accuracy *with fewer steps*. In this way, order predicts the accuracy you gain when you make steps smaller." :tutorial/id #uuid "40e84a8b-32df-5566-99ff-2b37f338d69f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222514 :tutorial/content "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)cos\\,(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,d\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bb98db0-8e34-5cf2-8911-56a6971740ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14681 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cbf813-044b-55ee-8ba9-d3d35aace3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.5\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226808} {:answer/id #uuid "facfa45d-4659-5d0c-9ff4-2c52cc693981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15.2\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226809} {:answer/id #uuid "11401acc-e216-529f-91f4-1de7bee4fe80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.4\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226810} {:answer/id #uuid "4d09d315-d298-588e-a30e-9c84aa06bc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.9\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226811} {:answer/id #uuid "6603f96b-de52-546f-b730-ae64e1dc6487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.3\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226812} {:answer/id #uuid "9f74e849-f0a0-5ad6-953e-0a07ac0b1594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.3\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226814} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd322d-dfbd-5d6e-8f6e-95596bc489a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.1\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226815} {:answer/id #uuid "0621492f-8c46-5184-a7a1-d7ec06af1e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.2\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226816} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1a09eb-a026-54ef-8f2d-37e26f77a2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.5\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226817} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba941f7-7dcf-5da5-ac79-3da563793575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.6\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226818} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ef8ea1-dd48-59b6-98ca-0b4b4df69ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A) \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228461} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd473dd-10d8-5658-aaa1-12d5a8b9d7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228462} {:answer/id #uuid "be409127-48c5-5103-998f-04deb78b5b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A) \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228475} {:answer/id #uuid "4840943c-4da7-5bcf-9ab7-2a6ba8acd348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228844 :question/id #uuid "a84b3eb8-e84b-5b50-8b53-8b26961126df" :question/math-academy-id "e-21396" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b752a3bb5f5ce53216e6765914da7d306acbf3063ca20293b503e69727a093dd.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the **smallest possible difference** between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(\\text{mean}(B)) & =\\frac{220+342+590+427+189}{4+6+10+7+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1768}{30} \\\\  & \\approx 58.933\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228852 :question/id #uuid "cd481c39-c4ff-56fe-92cd-6a2f31c4c015" :question/math-academy-id "e-21407" :question/problem "The dot plot above shows the $19$ values in the data set $C$. Data set $D$ is formed by subtracting $12$ to every value in data set $C$. Write the statement that compares the medians and the ranges of data sets $C$ and $D$.\n\n![](images/51/513ba0338ebaaa07691872b14a393975c48c51a8197ad4769769a0a28e36ed23.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's compare the medians and ranges of data sets $C$ and $D{:}$\n\n- Medians:There are $19$ values, so the median of $C$ is the $10$th value when ordered. Counting frequencies across $9,10,11,12,13$ gives cumulative counts $3,7,12,16,19$, so the $10$th value lies at\n$\\text{Median}(C)=11$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the median of $D$ will still be the $10$th value, but shifted down by $12{:}$\n$\\text{Median}(D)=11-12=-1$.Thus, the median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$.\n- Ranges: The range of data set $C{:}$\n$\\text{Range}(C)=\\max(C)-\\min(C)=13-9=4$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the range of data set $D{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Range}(D) & =(max(C)-12)-(min(C)-12) \\\\  & =max(C)-12-min(C)+12 \\\\  & =max(C)-min(C) \\\\  & =\\text{Range}(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$Thus, the ranges are equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is “The median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$, and the range of data set $D$ is equal to the range of data set $C$.”"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228934 :question/id #uuid "b40412bb-d2d7-519e-aad6-60f53f4a1fcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21797" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' - 3y = 0,  \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( 1 ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( 1 ) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( 3 ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( 3 )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$  \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\alpha }_{1} {y}_{1} ( a ) + {\\beta }_{1} {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( a ) & {\\alpha }_{1} {y}_{2} ( a ) + {\\beta }_{1} {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( a ) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2} {y}_{1} ( b ) + {\\beta }_{2} {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( b ) & {\\alpha }_{2} {y}_{2} ( b ) + {\\beta }_{2} {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( b )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions.  So, setting $\\alpha_i=0,$ $\\beta_i=1,$ $a=1,$ and $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( 1 ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( 1 ) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( 3 ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( 3 )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3(1)} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3(3)} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{9} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3{e}^{3})(-{e}^{-3})-(-{e}^{-1})(3{e}^{9}) \\\\  & =-3+3{e}^{8} \\\\  & =3({e}^{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\ne0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228935 :question/id #uuid "837c1385-88d1-5909-b79a-83df25a66838" :question/math-academy-id "e-21798" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x^3 e^{x},  \\qquad y'(0) = y'(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( \\pi  ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( \\pi  )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\quad$ The absolute value of $\\det(A)$ is $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A}}}$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP  $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A}}}$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP  $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y \\equiv 0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\hspace{0.167em} {\\alpha }_{1} {y}_{1} ( a ) + {\\beta }_{1} {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( a ) & {\\alpha }_{1} {y}_{2} ( a ) + {\\beta }_{1} {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( a ) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2} {y}_{1} ( b ) + {\\beta }_{2} {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( b ) & {\\alpha }_{2} {y}_{2} ( b ) + {\\beta }_{2} {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( b )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i = 0, \\beta_i = 1, a = 0,$ and $b = \\pi,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( \\pi  ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( \\pi  )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 4\\textrm i.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = -4\\sin 4x, \\qquad y_2'(x) = 4\\cos 4x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\pi ) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|0\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) = 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions}.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions or no solution}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228946 :question/id #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-21860" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\ny' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2sin\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228951 :question/id #uuid "04f55d6c-825e-587c-8ef4-a5527d1f374e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6569" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta.$*" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity $1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x,$ we can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228953 :question/id #uuid "533cdb5e-1d90-5aae-961b-4fe7daa30b0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6617" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,\\underbrace{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9806c712-ad73-5fd3-ba44-d2d5cb482a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309970} {:answer/id #uuid "9882de77-3dd8-5aef-9235-ed09ccd34df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309975} {:answer/id #uuid "a558b1dc-bd96-569a-b2f4-ea8b6fd92e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310081} {:answer/id #uuid "aae009e8-9ae4-5f6f-bd35-5d353b947905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310129} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8ce1d6-dbf4-5b6d-9de0-c032be27279c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309975} :answer-field/id #uuid "af80e23f-8786-51a5-834f-b1354d265b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00fa4d0d-a8d6-536d-9f44-d4c9796c3f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337950" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(B-\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\underbrace{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9325e5d1-fa4a-5ce0-80f1-2b1d73d21c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228440} {:answer/id #uuid "96716f40-d0f8-5848-8816-2a9ef9ed6bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228441} {:answer/id #uuid "977267b3-794b-5090-b748-b772bdf84ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\cot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228442} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e374c-61a5-55b6-af69-e44d8a4b2b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228443} {:answer/id #uuid "55f4c050-06c9-5796-a080-e7181bc8bb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228444}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81796914-d852-595b-8050-53c08ac44ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c4f0de-65d4-55a3-992e-a647b139e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365833} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c65982-0650-5e0d-996a-e78f9bb5b6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365834} {:answer/id #uuid "12d444c7-9ae8-512f-98e6-830e37b66435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365835} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ab968b-1634-5de2-9e80-f73f99b4e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365836} {:answer/id #uuid "56e33ae8-094c-5ec7-a04f-c7ad165cabe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365835} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9008acd-b16a-5420-b119-9306ceb1ba1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9674aca-ff32-53a9-8aab-6b30c0139952" :question/math-academy-id "q-337952" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y={sec}^{3}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={sec}^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\underbrace{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0358aeb8-08eb-5a5c-8562-39a2a513f9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311988} {:answer/id #uuid "913ec94d-28eb-5171-b472-adde6ea698c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313132} {:answer/id #uuid "9d007060-8464-5e5d-801c-5a75a999d876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313217} {:answer/id #uuid "d70abdbc-bb49-53c1-84c5-b501c3eb62c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313668} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e93dc7-8896-5921-ae19-96885943fd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313132} :answer-field/id #uuid "41cd0bdb-c020-5c85-bd71-c73efe71c0c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2785d5fd-1764-5f97-a587-0f8481691389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312277} {:answer/id #uuid "7e821c05-f7cc-5936-aebf-19dc944488de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312993} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e73f990-7dc3-519b-8083-09add7a308da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211b3316-e14b-53cc-bca8-eeae6615ca65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312227} {:answer/id #uuid "53261f6a-fc0f-5141-a079-e3de4fecad89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312650} {:answer/id #uuid "5583217a-1950-5b16-a58c-e36f1cf2b53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312660} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2b5a00-7894-59d0-ae19-8549458eae93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df0727d-275b-5b99-bd44-980e9689f12c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27306a98-ab58-521e-897c-e8c6b4fd5f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312271} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba5141a-01c8-5c24-b777-8232943d2563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312459} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2b7770-44b5-582b-b090-e0032b4ff3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{4}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313203} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c52932-8cd2-50be-be74-bb9deebcf7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313355} {:answer/id #uuid "f18eaf83-12ed-51a0-8f84-b703b9c2c13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313355} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4dca355-4e7b-506c-8ea5-f35f73afab42" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdee9cef-7184-5408-a29d-c97a87168d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-338525" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{3} & {e}^{-1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cf2bce-9588-5c59-92e6-a2e2908e6d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y_{1}(x) = \\cos 2x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\sin 2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228464} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb9949-a7db-545b-82c7-a59d71b205ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y_{1}(x) = \\cos 4x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\sin 4x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228465} {:answer/id #uuid "edfccc1f-b63c-58b5-932f-7ef0097be23e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y_{1}(x) = e^{x}$ and $y_{2}(x) = e^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228466} {:answer/id #uuid "60103a12-aff7-5130-8c7d-20eca56950f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_{2}(x) = e^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228467}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2fec4aba-8209-589c-818e-b44c26f5fc90" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "915e0c02-d674-57e8-843b-e1e88a4643c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228469} {:answer/id #uuid "bf37ad9d-1651-5f42-8d5d-622b8b0584cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos (1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228470} {:answer/id #uuid "d49d4c40-ba78-5983-8302-04fe8c7f83ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228471} {:answer/id #uuid "df8abefa-394d-569e-8b85-c1f820877279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228472} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2f59a9-61d0-51a6-a8