:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db3a73b-a87d-5959-9a69-e2ec6de1de8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368232} {:answer/id #uuid "94808729-9a3c-5f41-bb94-cf29ffb339b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368232} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2c6d81-b133-5fcd-82bc-15a43ec0a279" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c70a6431-713f-597d-8854-0d87d2f495bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368235} {:answer/id #uuid "d44fda72-e8eb-5dce-b636-31f01e8d76c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368236} {:answer/id #uuid "5255af29-de10-522a-ac33-deb42318ba90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368236} :answer-field/id #uuid "525e61f9-84fb-59e5-ac44-9b2878181efc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368234} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143f0b2e-2851-5817-82f4-c219903dadb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368239} {:answer/id #uuid "6194c272-bfd2-5613-9887-654731e62d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368240} {:answer/id #uuid "29c6c542-4bee-53ef-89aa-7b3d6db6b8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368241} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a1b63c-e53c-507d-9c47-d1c40d02463c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368239} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e0cee0-1285-504e-878c-f31011bfad17" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fbb6bf8-3f47-5abe-8cfd-8e34907d1303" :question/math-academy-id "q-338534" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}^{′}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=0,{\\beta }_{i}=1,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}^{′}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8ca1719-ded4-59cc-bec7-96db919c82d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2} + e^{4}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228484} {:answer/id #uuid "cda3ab9c-c86e-5394-864e-4105cb809959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{3} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228485} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4331bf-56b9-5e17-b41d-ded9e34cb7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228486} {:answer/id #uuid "037c4b21-dcad-5011-8891-d34bbfd74c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos (1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228487} {:answer/id #uuid "206574fa-24e9-59ba-9cc4-9f155f2bbcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4} - 1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228488}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd6df2d-a13a-54c6-bb63-700a2121d920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228483} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa004bf9-c2e7-56b7-8d65-5509ad04cca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228490} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fd7282-55c8-597a-a520-062bd6378e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228491} {:answer/id #uuid "40116fb0-f4e0-551c-a63d-ded054dc69d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228492}] :answer-field/id #uuid "36a272ce-8761-5fed-bd13-31fb6d237b08" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d91fb7ce-04b9-5563-839b-4d56c1e183a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has at most one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228494} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c9626c-c045-58fa-88f8-4cc5ea4f40a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions or no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228495} {:answer/id #uuid "0266de86-5213-5a29-a2a0-74bf4ba2c0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228496} {:answer/id #uuid "32cd3126-712a-5a34-a051-60e607b243d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228497}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1d163f-3934-5ff1-a678-e283311e5c8a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228493}] :question/id #uuid "85de65f6-2c04-5197-bb5e-97c893c6b266" :question/math-academy-id "q-338547" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 5x, y(0) = 2, y(1) = 5, 0 \\le x \\le 1\n$$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8276da20-2f6d-5799-aba3-8e9612a6fe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228499} {:answer/id #uuid "a0103813-4c2d-5c15-a54e-0f17e9e7e5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions or no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228500} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bfc954-a3f8-59d3-b2ae-3bf8b5077866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has at most one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228501} {:answer/id #uuid "0de43afd-e698-55f6-a6c4-c163499816e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228502}] :answer-field/id #uuid "456597df-cfa1-5625-bcf4-d86dd8c7efbd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "914006ec-f5df-54e0-a4e5-30106b9420e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228504} {:answer/id #uuid "9d117557-200b-58e1-906e-a3f827b7d7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos (1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228505} {:answer/id #uuid "f32682e9-c70b-5974-bcc0-a62b0f892dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228506} {:answer/id #uuid "f715f459-e20a-5fd6-a122-e5a9a5dd5060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228507} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee58c86-21e4-59ee-8a32-c46d5acca726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228508}] :answer-field/id #uuid "72a424b7-f2b5-5e27-9478-210aa4578a63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eeb7484-4622-5cd0-a842-384b72b3954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228510} {:answer/id #uuid "d96f5f92-5217-5834-a87a-e847188e18f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228511} {:answer/id #uuid "151d2aac-fd2e-5c56-9223-b3a18f4523dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228512}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bcab3d1f-1789-5b60-979a-ddc4ad29e93d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228509}] :question/id #uuid "c8b72fd4-5bd1-5dfe-a6ae-e9d6af1421c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338548" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} + 4y = x^{2}e^{2x}, y^{′}(0) = y^{′}(π) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le π\n$$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230216 :tutorial/content "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 0,  \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\quad y\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the differential equation to find the *fundamental solutions*, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ \n\nSet $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\nm^2 + 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3i,$ and thus the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 3x \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 3x.\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the boundary condition matrix.\n\nThe BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if the determinant of the boundary matrix $A$ is non-zero.\nNotice that our boundary conditions are in the form $\\alpha y + \\beta y' = 0$ with coefficients:\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot y(0) + 0 \\cdot y'(0) = 0, \\qquad\n1 \\cdot y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + 0 \\cdot y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe define the matrix $A$ using these coefficients and our fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot {y}_{1}(0)+0\\cdot {y}_{1}^{'}(0) & 1\\cdot {y}_{2}(0)+0\\cdot {y}_{2}^{'}(0) \\\\ 1\\cdot {y}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+0\\cdot {y}_{1}^{'}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & 1\\cdot {y}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+0\\cdot {y}_{2}^{'}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(0) & \\operatorname{sin}(0) \\\\ cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = (1)(1) - (0)(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\neq 0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique solution $y\\equiv 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "96e6953a-3af4-5995-b093-def8a970f52b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14642 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "498bed50-28c7-50fa-a5df-0080495261c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5abd7c36-14a4-56ef-ada9-6c206b80a9d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "968f331f-040e-5a16-a459-23a91c08dfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343473" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb9ae0a-ccab-5851-bc46-d8fb84f0673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537298} {:answer/id #uuid "391bcc0e-c46d-58de-a271-e5921f5359be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537346} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf55ac9-9213-51d1-bb90-ad44b88e7647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537354} {:answer/id #uuid "93cc08d4-e51b-59b2-b080-974e07e72850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537298} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b2f25e-b1a1-5a98-851d-8c3126524b1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c02a55f-b3d6-55fc-b2ef-8b484c0659e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343663" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6120990c-dd87-590f-9590-a3cccfefa184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333872} {:answer/id #uuid "67cb7725-deba-57f1-9c4e-bb8958890bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333941} {:answer/id #uuid "7524c61f-03fa-542b-9792-537e1b0aff14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334047} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb304a4-9c70-5db5-baef-50819e9100dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334387} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b04ce6-0d22-5ddb-aaca-e9709a6604d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334387} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb1da30-fb1a-5be4-af3c-533bece1f592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6af51c6-8a3b-5e30-952e-168721f14820" :question/math-academy-id "q-64650" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =4.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef75135-19a4-52a8-ad6d-a258b56de06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525363} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3ff2ff-ba42-5ab5-8146-22b30c6d4fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525389} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac4b53a-68f0-5fef-800d-e0192121b32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525552} {:answer/id #uuid "941a4670-57a3-5a46-81db-969321edf9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525585} {:answer/id #uuid "daa3c953-83ea-5e0d-add7-38558f47a086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525552} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cfcf2f0-782a-59a2-a6ca-6394fc9db061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c83d3104-e874-508c-b1b2-d01d79f86eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-100631" :question/problem "If ${log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233201 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1dc5c87c-64df-54e7-9938-40291c767c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-113958" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C, \\\\ \\int sin\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x-2(-\\operatorname{cot}x)+(-\\operatorname{cos}x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x+2\\operatorname{cot}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070ad91b-8594-5a54-bd22-effa4b3824f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250609} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4884cc-8e4f-54c9-9b69-4852a3a71ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250791} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae1ce24-2485-5108-9fd4-bf13db223503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251176} {:answer/id #uuid "861d53de-3a6c-5cea-b897-b08c52349d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251676} {:answer/id #uuid "ad477e53-678e-5262-adb3-20a5abeb8572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250791} :answer-field/id #uuid "6855c777-d148-5b64-b557-3af5ffaeb2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6dd4ef9-dcc4-5324-91b6-a18f8265b17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-119865" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d273204-9c71-5dd0-b69b-1a90276ae151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716952} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37edfe3-8d04-5659-9550-117d4c4acd83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed3ae951-db6d-557c-8a34-9c013751cf9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127614" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63746cd7-d78b-55d9-9dd3-7e1c9980ab08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503174} {:answer/id #uuid "8b920ce3-ff7b-5bb6-8fdf-35583cef9643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503263} {:answer/id #uuid "a0187cce-8b24-5027-bfa7-f2e2f49ae2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-5x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503301} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e1935b-7c69-571c-988f-1bd72cb529a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503311} {:answer/id #uuid "f352bcca-38a2-5ecb-8851-59eb03ee8d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503477} :answer-field/id #uuid "968ccea1-eddc-5df5-b531-eb97516921db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ac66977-fb7e-55ea-b01a-306cc1fa6e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138848" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c52374-9b9f-5ea2-9b1d-640924d6082b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no horizontal asymptotes." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562989} {:answer/id #uuid "44521491-c131-5639-a63c-726e10f92842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563098} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6d8c85-9923-5fdb-8597-d5d5d1259719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563154} {:answer/id #uuid "7569d322-3718-5d60-86d6-93dea8795fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563204} {:answer/id #uuid "a4afd188-4d20-517e-a47b-c6882d781b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562989} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ad94da-747c-5f20-8c1e-e2c0668e7607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "58b2d6c3-6089-5685-8952-ba465d3a1547" :question/math-academy-id "q-156741" :question/problem "Which of the following is a horizontal asymptote of $y={log}_{6}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/41/4177d9520b3758b29926eba7a7a74ed4c508d861e3c343f46c372ec79500fc9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0f5b8f-e5e0-519d-aa21-ccb7de143a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562962} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1effa9-7e1a-55a3-81d4-aac75787131d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563047} {:answer/id #uuid "5963bb65-6486-5a51-a43b-a6e3dcb86561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563145} {:answer/id #uuid "d9334f19-c8ae-5204-a395-20c07315d341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563420} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5aa106-d5f2-58b4-962e-4d9b7feff98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563047} :answer-field/id #uuid "b179eeb3-b9e7-569b-a20d-09ef65081dfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19c56fe1-1bea-5145-bd09-d55715bd1967" :question/math-academy-id "q-156743" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{6}⁡x?$ \n\nI. It has no zeros\n\nII. $y\\to 6$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. It has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8d3f9dc995d8801d451b15129860362efa772178cdc3ef07be5adb6a1da5c45.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a zero at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound. Therefore, $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233555 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd3ffbc5-745a-5661-ae98-ce279b6b1255" :question/math-academy-id "q-166947" :question/problem "If you deposit $\\$12,000$ into an account with a $8\\%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $\\$18,000$ in the account?" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $\\$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $\\$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $\\$18,000$ in the account."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b6d851-07bf-50a6-ba74-60a93aaab1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232335} :answer-field/id #uuid "80d6b8d0-cd10-519f-aa6e-beaba5dff6e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37b84130-ad1d-51d8-b7bf-029b37f28c20" :question/math-academy-id "q-228899" :question/problem "If ${e}^{2x+3}=7,$ then $x≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d98f75-ec98-55b8-bd6d-557ed859ed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{83}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534309} {:answer/id #uuid "48c860bd-e06a-5669-aacd-322bd3817f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{68}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534403} {:answer/id #uuid "51a1eea1-22ad-5e39-840b-a993eeed3ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534427} {:answer/id #uuid "c020f3d7-85a1-5452-a318-b9aba6af4d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534693} {:answer/id #uuid "e55c01a0-412d-5e01-991f-926ab7486bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534693} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b66e190-3a44-5bcd-8fa8-92a479622c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96612b32-04f7-536b-8298-ef008f709116" :question/math-academy-id "q-25135" :question/problem "Find $x$ such that ${log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2) & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)}=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2 & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 3x-2 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =66 \\\\ x & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0c30166-be09-5f1b-b281-e6267c80f897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232382} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f9ad2c-88b0-5178-9d53-259722f6c543" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "feba1750-dc58-51d4-aac2-da5ff78e21ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-263084" :question/problem "If ${e}^{1-4x}=\\sqrt[3]{15},$ then $x\\approx ${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-4x} & =\\sqrt[3]{15} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[3]{15}) \\\\ 1-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}({15}^{1/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15) \\\\ -4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15)-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{ln}(15)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx 0.024\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f578a9-cbd3-500d-b55c-d564c80794ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{log}_{9}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290982} {:answer/id #uuid "61a24d9b-5760-5004-b4f0-752020b46818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291046} {:answer/id #uuid "709a2b05-5bae-57d8-9933-96272b7cd86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291115} {:answer/id #uuid "d86a0c7b-8348-5b84-927c-9b00881731e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-{log}_{9}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291537} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fb9a89-cac2-5b97-bff0-c2669ccb7ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{9}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291115} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8bf575-05c7-5a0b-9353-56ca03ff699f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a53c677-f896-5476-b37e-b5aa2f8ba1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-26387" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of ${log}_{9},$ what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${9}^{2y+1}={5}^{-y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}⁡(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}⁡(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09287577-5ed3-56fe-b855-6d26e3c207e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538257} {:answer/id #uuid "31c812be-9c24-5035-bd82-28fd6df10b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538334} {:answer/id #uuid "4a53b6e2-d85d-503b-9005-7384f32b967b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538387} {:answer/id #uuid "6611c66a-d2cb-5b3f-b7f9-124be1499657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538459} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ab6c9e-dc89-5fdb-b731-b962cf7d6239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538334} :answer-field/id #uuid "de40916c-8324-553d-9096-bab0579bf8a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97031323-cb39-5f57-8186-f731d54414db" :question/math-academy-id "q-26532" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{2}⁡(3x+1)={log}_{2}⁡(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}⁡(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f89b295-c0ea-51e0-baed-7b397b5dcf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232581} :answer-field/id #uuid "a886217b-6842-59bb-806c-1abaff801bc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1592937d-0c9f-5407-8fea-82da11709905" :question/math-academy-id "q-319487" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=3+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0285aa91-2267-505a-9e1d-470bdb4e3618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232585} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7f0a80-f55b-5dc3-ab7d-f8cf4328aca7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "247e6fd4-010c-5cfd-adfe-f21a9b3e2d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-319554" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-6\\sqrt{3}-6\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/ea/ea995ef688b12c8d58076fc832c9a5c735ff5933aff3ac6c347ae27850adbb2e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453880c3-1ae3-55e4-a4e9-b1cd442aeb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232609} :answer-field/id #uuid "495a7f1c-94c5-5185-9c3e-b28de350339f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab5a32f4-676c-579d-8de1-7499420b539a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321138" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7302f26f-ebc6-523f-92e8-b936a249ad82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232771} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db918a0-0670-54b6-adae-fd1988d94e7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3fb378b5-e05e-5063-a284-de8062325055" :question/math-academy-id "q-338444" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641d0678-b1c1-501e-a5f8-9c02ad64c16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232790} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c93bd5-bde9-54f0-8b44-22ceceb7471b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21051a8d-2827-54c0-93e4-0d23c3a26dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339489" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874eaf2f-ee83-5b63-ae37-7674f43245ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232804} :answer-field/id #uuid "93b27d0f-e6bb-5aaa-8730-16adcc1c9623" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d752eb5-b4a0-54de-ad34-0377f4b8b36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232806} :answer-field/id #uuid "816c812a-3fa6-573b-aec0-001cf4da9755" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e2d6850-1117-5e88-9ace-62b7a7c29058" :question/math-academy-id "q-339964" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=1$ from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+3x-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}[(x-2)^{2}+3x-5] \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+3(1)-5 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+3-5 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4))+2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "544a8683-5289-585f-b4d7-bb014b60135d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87d4421d-bfbd-5a5d-a096-b04302f17b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-344572" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b036fe4-7233-5ebc-8f0c-3cf98501a5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y>9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563109} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0c9f33-790a-501e-b19f-3f8e49dc71a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563158} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa24a05-46a0-58d6-9f18-9c9e0b452ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563197} {:answer/id #uuid "7f349f14-4593-5f2a-895a-e0b5fea4b00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563224} {:answer/id #uuid "98d3f8f9-ad50-5b5b-ab14-47a7ba9a65a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563289} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b5fafb-5a7a-5f32-a04d-538e33ff8b78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c872bbea-0830-58ac-9bad-a456f39f833d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47778" :question/problem "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{9}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{9}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/40/40664f83ba162a93ed06970798d37798db90b85c21d44a9ed20f338df4e1b4e1.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{9}⁡x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All real numbers.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441e43be-1210-543b-a060-7ab6375d721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504153} {:answer/id #uuid "5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504193} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9bc1c5-97ac-53e3-8d0c-ec9cfcd0faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504218} {:answer/id #uuid "7610f7a5-3984-5ff3-a2fc-d3a6f3076d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x-\\pi )$ and $b=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504233} {:answer/id #uuid "d377c5cf-aeae-5ea0-8d58-7d902cda0add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504193} :answer-field/id #uuid "59673acb-10a2-5c4a-860c-d21ad0c6bfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b0c16e42-a6fd-5291-a75a-a545c6a864c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-49233" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ec0d1a-fbc1-5041-8e40-636cd7efa33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542747} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc1e664-6c62-5c39-b7eb-8b0cdd0da386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542848} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecfee0c-ab43-511e-bbf7-24b4470eee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542854} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0a2323-dacf-55d7-8bd0-de0f8bb99950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543142} {:answer/id #uuid "f507a8d2-4685-5d46-9b8b-f4be0c73f455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542747} :answer-field/id #uuid "51e26732-12ca-5a69-9f16-67129aa50da4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f70da637-39bf-585b-b7da-8cee169442e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66772" :question/problem "$6{log}_{5}⁡(100)-6{log}_{5}⁡(4)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{5}⁡(100)-6{log}_{5}⁡(4) & =6({log}_{5}⁡(100)-{log}_{5}⁡(4)) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{100}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}⁡(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${5}^{2}=25$, then ${log}_{5}⁡(25)={log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2})=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n6{log}_{5}⁡(25)=6\\cdot 2=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82826981-110c-5a1a-858e-43f3412d0ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{{log}_{4}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544370} {:answer/id #uuid "9de9a0bc-28a2-5c16-8937-334d75654754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={log}_{4}⁡(33)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544428} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc73e5b-9e38-59e8-b84c-76a4fa387198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={log}_{4}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544562} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f5a6cf-f068-59be-af8a-466cd2c91f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{log}_{4}⁡(11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544573} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9486c9-bf88-5eab-bce1-b8a6a8aa9b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={log}_{4}⁡(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544593} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a67648-6807-50b6-a6d7-45ce416912c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7f69989d-58f3-5c44-9cd6-bba8083f79ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-66832" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to the equation $3({4}^{y})-14=19.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({4}^{y})-14 & =19 \\\\ 3({4}^{y}) & =33 \\\\ {4}^{y} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{4}$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{y}) & ={log}_{4}⁡(11) \\\\ y & ={log}_{4}⁡(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y={log}_{4}⁡(11).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058e8375-7ab8-5c53-aaed-f328087fbbae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339170} {:answer/id #uuid "13bbc739-722e-5acd-849e-2f17b762834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339302} {:answer/id #uuid "45cfd739-1850-5ce4-b85f-97d485f27f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339748} {:answer/id #uuid "67edeaf5-1d83-588c-b487-2b408b66d99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340055} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f89706-38a3-50fa-a7ed-dd30403522fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340748} :answer-field/id #uuid "428de8a1-618a-5678-be18-aa4b392f0347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "70fc9979-f75c-5347-868a-dff8c003766b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73178" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}\n$$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44eeb69e-907e-5574-bb91-b2d66a01e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345948} {:answer/id #uuid "51699c92-8384-5576-b2f6-af2ce04b7660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346049} {:answer/id #uuid "65172d25-980d-56e0-a409-90511d8bbee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346230} {:answer/id #uuid "7a505864-b39a-501e-974f-2d6be156be3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346422} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a5e98e-6ce2-50ec-920c-514d7fc9ef85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346049} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd06476-afc5-55a4-b399-3d95d64a094f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83a8cf9e-0691-5674-a99d-97bc9d8ae44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79870" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8) & ={log}_{4}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(5) & ={log}_{4}⁡(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564231} {:answer/id #uuid "d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564298} {:answer/id #uuid "e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564338} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564349} {:answer/id #uuid "f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564338} :answer-field/id #uuid "9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e6ba761-66c0-5454-8a49-a1749f6fb11e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81999" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}⁡(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154fa8da-d8d4-5d35-a927-4d997f30031e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512146} {:answer/id #uuid "22ffff4a-deb5-521a-9391-9c312d39acfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512174} {:answer/id #uuid "29fbd695-065d-59f9-a978-64ab959589fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512195} {:answer/id #uuid "c582451e-1fd0-5671-8d95-3e20037da447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512575} {:answer/id #uuid "c67bb952-4b1f-5631-b6cd-9cff40172e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512577} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4740df2-7f5f-55f7-996f-1be23039010f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9bbddd9a-4e6f-51fe-9ece-2d2a83dcd62f" :question/math-academy-id "q-956" :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(32)$ without using a calculator." :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ and $X=32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239c326a-0a3b-5c29-850c-cac8919a3673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡16=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512177} {:answer/id #uuid "4779f4c7-29b4-5acc-9108-58e66d5a7ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡2=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512249} {:answer/id #uuid "4794e66d-dfd4-5289-9730-730f5a69be16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512250} {:answer/id #uuid "5e21fa3f-1c6c-5a3a-b2e9-4f260f1443e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡2=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512321} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e5741e-9798-5186-9c1e-7f4c4d89acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡16=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512250} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c2319c-52ab-5a4c-97b1-53dcd4e20292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32b42bfe-7421-54f2-832b-a9a8c0cbc5ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-99487" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[4]{16}=2$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235287 :multistep/context "Consider the trigonometric function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235788} :multistep/id #uuid "112693d0-1740-5138-b117-6da5b56e4836" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235428} :step/id #uuid "3522635a-0752-5e75-a456-aef1674081f2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235422} :step/id #uuid "4b192060-e068-5cf9-8ff7-05d95bad606d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235423} :step/id #uuid "99ae9d18-53ab-5024-834c-2ea212537454" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235426} :step/id #uuid "9ea1ed85-db0a-5e0f-9bd3-079a7117bc88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235769}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235424} :step/id #uuid "cc0a600e-6ebe-5898-adae-fbb2831dbad8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235425} :step/id #uuid "d6341953-e718-528e-83ed-632559b6c62b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235427} :step/id #uuid "e7736fd0-bbfb-5a64-a865-8864240238c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235421} :step/id #uuid "fabdb939-8287-534c-a578-97dbcce0652b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235647}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235301 :multistep/context "Consider the functions $f(x)={x}^{B}+Ax,g(x)=B{e}^{x},$ and $h(x)=sin⁡x+1,$ where $A$ and $B$ are constants to be found." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235659} :multistep/id #uuid "8fd21532-89f6-5c25-a765-13883174e1e0" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235453} :step/id #uuid "06023932-8236-551e-8944-bf02e1c2d973" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456} :step/id #uuid "3df2343a-bbb7-5b34-aa08-c03d63c3a1bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235457} :step/id #uuid "45bb2f18-2e9e-5b44-8e6d-835ab02f773e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235454} :step/id #uuid "5913bc92-a4b7-5aae-8c83-91eeba377481" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452} :step/id #uuid "5b1c7505-862b-5fd8-891f-db395428467e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235459} :step/id #uuid "9858bdd7-c68f-520a-814f-257f6c87fa70"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235458} :step/id #uuid "b4e39b02-a0ee-5216-8f15-fc24fb6ca6c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235455} :step/id #uuid "cc7fb253-09d5-5a21-9268-9ccb9408c367" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235636}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235311 :multistep/context "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235632} :multistep/id #uuid "f1e50ebd-abae-5c45-863b-21e026e0e2dc" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235449} :step/id #uuid "2ef227bb-d961-5627-9ce8-1adc2e46e55f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235446} :step/id #uuid "365ad924-2f64-5ce1-bc82-d9f1b9686b5f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235445} :step/id #uuid "3ba18776-1bf5-5afd-86aa-966c67dc2d07" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235451} :step/id #uuid "501fa45d-f17b-5536-baf1-a72fde985753"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235447} :step/id #uuid "81e372e5-5cea-5951-8fc8-e4dd6939a890" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235448} :step/id #uuid "d4ebee06-bcaa-5365-8838-5e174e415bb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235450} :step/id #uuid "e6a87120-867d-5ab3-bf90-5ba7a9d62bc3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235651}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7d82cf3-65ea-5dba-bf7a-27272f3b4d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234801} :answer-field/id #uuid "d978a7cb-e8eb-5315-b004-48bc6c03f7e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41926de1-ea87-5292-8ead-08a36cada277" :question/math-academy-id "q-138785" :question/problem "The area of the new housing complex is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nhectares." :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $R$ (the old housing complex) and the area of the image $T(R)$ (the new housing complex) under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(R))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(R)\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(R)=16\\,\\text{hectares}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of the matrix $T$ (found in part 1), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)-(2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(R)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the new housing complex is $48$ hectares."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f35e2d-66b6-54fe-a062-977604208172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234827} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d44031-6cdd-5336-a8be-754448adef36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6fce5f29-50bd-5618-8615-2260116a577f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166287" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1fdd1d8-f13a-5ad7-8a17-d28488331cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aabaf7f-52bc-56e1-9d7f-5a0f08068097" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44d89ad5-80c7-5f9b-a3ef-7a1ead4a6cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-166288" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\n![](images/67/67f6ec4f72e7bd74ac9ae5fa0f195cda1e90e8d8e6fad1bf85944a3dfd17fdfb.png)\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-5$ and $y=5$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }5sin⁡(2x)=DNE.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8be44b9-b42d-52ce-90b8-6bab2b790332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234913} :answer-field/id #uuid "fff82516-d651-5957-98ab-d8adc8fad4dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9588cc2-fb5a-5a22-93e8-5666e235065a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166289" :question/problem "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h))-5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5sin⁡(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c68de688-9020-52c5-8f89-e2e2109b6898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234856} :answer-field/id #uuid "77629536-10a3-54a4-b9d7-7a2ddbb9ca31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31d409c1-afdf-5625-8497-31b05aee312f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166290" :question/problem "![](images/47/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is $\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-10.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-10.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-10.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-10.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-10,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-10.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53583341-9630-5513-b858-817c53bdbf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234677} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b5801e2-fc41-5b21-8307-049a20814e3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a789eff7-38bd-5b16-985e-a4f6d93ab011" :question/math-academy-id "q-166291" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)}=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x).\n$$\n\n![](images/e3/e3f5c484211ef0b9cf5f3a29198d25c23933a1c07427d0dcbfad01edbb300d0e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x)$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x)=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54d79ab2-d3e3-518f-9432-9303fe5e9c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234680} :answer-field/id #uuid "6474d9b2-1a7c-5688-b8bb-0c1cf0bb04c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4245aadd-c6cc-525e-9e00-1a2a6ed2d782" :question/math-academy-id "q-166293" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\cdot sin⁡(2)$" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}[\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 5sin⁡(2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot sin⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da92529-d318-5dfa-bfa7-0df43fae7490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c7fd239-c698-5700-aea8-d260b2cef38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167328" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right)\\,=\n$${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b95d6c4-5d7b-5dc8-88d7-f9b4485ad8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234590} :answer-field/id #uuid "7988cab9-4497-5a8a-841c-22ed9c51d002" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d280c885-e199-544c-b66d-467329b4f2c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167566" :question/problem "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$v\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36418c5e-b281-5269-a172-082c7acea694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234634} :answer-field/id #uuid "75627b65-436c-57ac-b53a-07bd39b13c2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38bd2c5c-e834-539a-b221-ece037f8a75e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167567" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](images/36/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $2$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5afa7e6-c04f-5208-a625-922d31fe8db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{csc}^{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234934} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c8e574-e15b-5877-a041-10a0494c160b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "40e69bfb-65e8-5b73-9c0a-94d7d3dd4ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167568" :question/problem "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=4u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{csc}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4{csc}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d971a67d-9a39-5728-97d4-88d7a1a29d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234886} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed829ad-3fbc-5ade-9d74-02d9be633d41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6851a62-df7c-598a-8ba2-7eecd95e5c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-167569" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd74cf7-7832-5d4d-9bdb-4352f71d9f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234944} :answer-field/id #uuid "6614a1f6-a87a-5480-bfb4-b31cd6dddad3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c736f2-25bd-5d43-acf7-389cd352b94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234650} :answer-field/id #uuid "86dd5a39-356d-58f6-a814-f6ba7ef31193" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd151ce5-c877-5cb0-954c-57ccd397f03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234837} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb953ef-938a-5b5b-99c5-5fa2a0878578" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80153a7b-80ae-54f5-b4ee-35a981cc0cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234740} :answer-field/id #uuid "914f87c0-b0d8-5ddd-be80-6c4d6303ec75" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb796ec5-fb4b-5f50-95bf-f4781e9dc4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234943} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed723404-e7d9-5542-afad-7590ee940dcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed1a25d3-8778-5a6a-8c12-425fa1c7957d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167631" :question/problem "Complete the following facts about the function $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe domain of the function is $x\\in ($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$).$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe range of the function is $[$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-4}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$].$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The period of the function is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-5}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n![](images/96/96bd60d23237f836b77a26c5c0461a5c2a474554f0a14266b53fc5e7d999f1b7.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n* The domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n* The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n* The period of $sin⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5979fbe9-034d-5191-82a4-e0ccdb34ea12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234684} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c8f9ae-1e5a-5b02-b529-bd2c894e7f02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bffa0056-7e7b-560f-b52d-460b92da84c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167632" :question/problem "If $A$ is the maximum value of $h(x)=sin⁡x+1$ then $A=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n* Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n* Then, shift it by $1$ units **up**.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/db/dbe1701a42a160e982bff96b1085727080c39b87ca2c99886e3e153e2f5964cc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=sin⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\nsin⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $1$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡x+1\\le 1+1 \\\\ sin⁡x+1\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $sin⁡x+1$ is $A=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6608a0-c52d-5197-a2c4-db2292e6e723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234595} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ba6e34-7aaf-5bfb-b233-f307218d28f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8cb5259c-7ead-550f-9414-cb453c189596" :question/math-academy-id "q-167633" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e1d2c8fef007596dc6fd9b6ef0813fafeb7014490ff35d993f276a2a96ccb13.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nIf the graph above is generated by the function $y=sin⁡(Bx),$ then $B=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nThe graph shows that the function makes $3$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/54/544997dc11f7c88d5aa7b3f78d8dd1059a0ac08092726983897c5992d03acf8b.png)\n\n \n\nWe know that for the function $y=sin⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our function, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbff55-8f3a-5c54-81ca-c8b7d5227587" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4ea7df7-6856-52ff-9d9c-29beb9f68c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167782" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right)=\\frac{e^x}{8\\sin^2(x)}\\bigl(\n$${{field-1}}$\\bigr)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dda0765-68c6-5ee8-91f0-76cde5fc50be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{cos}^{2}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234618} :answer-field/id #uuid "61b8203a-d997-56ae-b5ea-ea33aa22c47a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "958cc2e1-a063-55bc-8ab0-10fbd4a3bd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-167937" :question/problem "Given that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}36{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}18{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d00ec8-e865-5151-9a4c-bad4652bfb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234805} :answer-field/id #uuid "079110b6-6c4b-5d0b-9a1e-a17faca83de8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f543150-38e4-557e-9ab8-57056b911cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167938" :question/problem "Construct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badc07a3-1246-57ab-8020-ef66917b8e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234832} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66b8368-5a95-5c46-b080-d106fa6765c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1266fc86-1b5f-5a90-babf-82e935d1b03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234568} :answer-field/id #uuid "de4c731c-ed74-5645-8ab9-3c59ddda4a72" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0ef2d04-e954-5c3b-a12b-42687de0c911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2z-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234846} :answer-field/id #uuid "df422da9-b336-5913-a0b2-622e6c8e0a04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a5631afa-3e26-5ef4-92ef-abe2aca2eb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-168621" :question/problem "$\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n$⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({y}^{2}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2y-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({y}^{2}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2z-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "116ed1cb-cee2-51fa-b983-0d044a80cbf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+ln⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235911} {:answer/id #uuid "11ab5490-6958-5907-9744-daca5ba83c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-6{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235917} {:answer/id #uuid "1855d4ba-79bb-5eef-8ebc-129a08271fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235966} {:answer/id #uuid "23384ad2-5c2b-5675-b1cb-2712d43e696a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236040} {:answer/id #uuid "23a1d755-1a8c-5412-a736-df2f34a61feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236046} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0f10c6-f01a-58b2-9e7c-e6f93eb8b719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+6{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236109} {:answer/id #uuid "2f85df34-ee93-5aca-8d12-bcf5fe3faa9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236117} {:answer/id #uuid "335087d8-fab5-5522-94d1-93d0d9655755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}-ln⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236140} {:answer/id #uuid "35399b5c-61ff-5ca5-be4f-4c1abe0d88b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236154} {:answer/id #uuid "3a832fbd-d544-586d-b133-c8ba1f4dffc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236184} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc5000e-2b32-58d7-ad16-c7de786b85e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(K|x|)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236225} {:answer/id #uuid "638c9078-bde9-50c8-942f-4e4ba2d0effb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236448} {:answer/id #uuid "6a533669-c40f-5a00-940e-dadb7f469489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236488} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4f0ac8-1dab-5270-b011-493f700ff938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+8ln⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236509} {:answer/id #uuid "7fce5c0b-fbff-51c0-9373-cb763d114999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236610} {:answer/id #uuid "8697299b-732b-5dac-b7fd-937118c6f358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236655} {:answer/id #uuid "89e62549-2f57-5be7-80e7-7b01223e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡\\left(\\frac{K|x|}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236681} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5e3191-58c4-58b2-85d9-7d6953772bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236705} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6f3b-c7d8-5d5b-a12e-71a1116a0a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} {:answer/id #uuid "90cac687-dd21-570e-a62a-f9e1d628421a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8ln⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236739} {:answer/id #uuid "96af5361-9440-53c2-b581-6ff64a3f4000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236774} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} {:answer/id #uuid "a162783a-2e70-5f31-8790-4cae86e77c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡(K|x|)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236827} {:answer/id #uuid "a77100c8-1980-51d5-9cdf-fe3f251c5336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236865} {:answer/id #uuid "b3489f8b-789f-5fa4-bb29-debd57283e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}-ln⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236952} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b65fc-6ad6-5255-ad37-78be530512d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237057} {:answer/id #uuid "c8374c9a-abe6-515a-a258-f3f08a5ed775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237086} {:answer/id #uuid "e22f3892-01f6-5e59-8632-9444a5c795ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237233} {:answer/id #uuid "e2988a8a-d70e-5324-b36e-117de13ef56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237238} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d4487-26a1-5a24-b5cb-498b6c3eac2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237408} {:answer/id #uuid "ffae31c4-dedd-5981-9bf8-843ca631db3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0737fd5b-96ea-5473-a44f-a41afd6c1553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=13,\\,y=25,\\,z=77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235846} {:answer/id #uuid "4537e0a4-37b3-5f1d-9fed-26057698bace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-18,\\,y=25,\\,z=77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236260} {:answer/id #uuid "a124349e-3884-56ce-9385-08f24fa6a176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=13,\\,y=19,\\,z=77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236824} {:answer/id #uuid "aad0b275-3680-5a33-969f-268cb505c79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-11,\\,y=19,\\,z=77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236897} {:answer/id #uuid "cf542f7a-ea1a-583f-908a-f1953ea01648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-13,\\,y=19,\\,z=57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236260} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4836e15-142a-5dba-b682-0f201894c5b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "062c7a4a-d791-5879-9bf4-0c469a15394c" :question/math-academy-id "q-10038" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}z+2y+7x &=1 \\\\ 3y-z &=-2 \\\\ 4y-2z-3x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+z &=1 \\\\ 3y-z &=-2 \\\\ -3x+4y-2z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7(-6+4)-2(-3)+1(9) \\\\  & =-14+6+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -8 & -11 & 34 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -34 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 77\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-18,\\,y=25,\\,z=77.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f4398f-18db-5bb4-ab9f-e4e728621f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{15}{11},y=-\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235973} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7a6fdc-7ce6-5e15-89a2-84e59db68519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=15,y=10,z=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236221} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1cb93d-039d-5852-a40e-003b41fbe609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-30,y=-20,z=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236697} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c46c33-108b-5ebb-8533-fcb6694cba67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{11},y=\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237004} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a6c04-3807-5923-9a4c-3658958aaca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-15,y=-10,z=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f2b74e1-36cd-50e9-b37c-03195ff066d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a8a38c1-8d83-5c15-8052-a8f7706f20a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-10074" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+3z &=-1-x \\\\ 4y &=-5z \\\\ x+6z-3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 4y+5z &=0 \\\\ x+6z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(24)-2(-5)+3(0-4) \\\\  & =24+10-12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 12 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 5 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{15}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{15}{11},y=-\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cf84eb-8019-5024-a9d4-f6d05cfd3269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=15,y=19,z=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235970} {:answer/id #uuid "4f20d1b9-9c63-50d2-a24e-b400decf2a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236328} {:answer/id #uuid "5b847704-0397-5367-962e-626db03c9d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3,y=\\frac{19}{5},z=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236389} {:answer/id #uuid "b802e7d8-4eb2-58b7-9ef0-5acd50e6d76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236989} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d25859-b8ae-5dba-8a88-d56b4edbf4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=15,y=-19,z=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236989} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b1f130-63be-5d63-8f37-389b677d9647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e779cc8b-bc48-5f02-aa7a-6ab946e667e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-10075" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3z-y+x-5 &=0 \\\\ y+2x+2z &=1 \\\\ z-2x-2y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=5 \\\\ 2x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ -2x-2y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1+4)+1(2+4)+3(-4+2) \\\\  & =5+6-6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -\\frac{19}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338ad7db-610c-5504-8b07-93ddf6989bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=19,y=-49,z=-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236141} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb5824f-641f-50ba-a60c-6f273e5bc745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-19,y=-49,z=-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236307} {:answer/id #uuid "87dae3e4-1d42-58e8-97db-0124534b5c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=42,y=-35,z=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236665} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dd7272-ba74-59ef-a368-6470a994b20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=15,y=-49,z=-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237366} {:answer/id #uuid "f757ba64-10e8-5058-8e1b-0c55eb97642d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=19,y=49,z=58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237378}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236141} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd21ae35-d429-561d-927c-fa4d44ff854e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e8e08af1-fca9-5664-99a6-119c31e3f669" :question/math-academy-id "q-10076" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y+3z &=0 \\\\ 8x-3y+5z &=9 \\\\ 7x-2y+4z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ -49 \\\\ -58\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=19,y=-49,z=-58.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4128ba71-30f4-5d9f-a1da-f0feb798ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-29,y=-12,z=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236233} {:answer/id #uuid "4a578053-2e19-50df-9af8-8b315860043c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-29,y=-12,z=-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236294} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4620a0-76d3-5e9f-92c3-382dca21ee9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=29,y=12,z=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236392} {:answer/id #uuid "88066dff-8cdf-5893-a4d3-81d269f7ee2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-25,y=-12,z=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236666} {:answer/id #uuid "b8391916-39bd-5633-8b71-eb03b0decc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-29,y=-15,z=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236233} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7b3d13-f904-586b-ba35-a7c8c21f7369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7f748e9-7cb4-573b-a324-159e0e3861ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-10100" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z &=6 \\\\ 4x+7y+2z &=0 \\\\ 3x+y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(7-2)-3(-2)+1(4-21) \\\\  & =10+6-17 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -17 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-29 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 100\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-29,y=-12,z=100.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ee7e0cc-1841-50f3-a5f2-a54b57df7f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8,y=-8,z=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236414} {:answer/id #uuid "79759bba-4475-51ba-874e-be7acc9f35b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236581} {:answer/id #uuid "e806513b-a961-5fd9-b571-b43cd8457290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2}{3},y=\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237278} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd5388f-1f93-52d6-b413-aa7b0e4d018d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8,y=8,z=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237302} {:answer/id #uuid "fd42bafb-20e3-522c-919b-8c6a78d5d018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236581} :answer-field/id #uuid "3334def8-af8d-5c3c-8b52-9e05eccf839f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8cc406f-67fe-55bc-b55e-b6858ce9be1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10101" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=0 \\\\ x+y+3z &=2 \\\\ 3x-2y+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1-3(-2))+0-1(-2-3) \\\\  & =1+6+2+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432a51a6-a859-539f-aad3-0c3afb4b46f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236251} {:answer/id #uuid "95ab3dfc-0bd9-5c63-b32d-b78ab929c832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=-4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236768} {:answer/id #uuid "c356000e-035d-577e-85f1-5fd545858205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237051} {:answer/id #uuid "c3eca95b-b2df-58cc-bfa3-67df1d256020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,y=8,z=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237054} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9f0f71-f5a0-5bca-9a3b-a6ddd72eabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5,y=8,z=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237051} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f236a0-c448-555c-b80c-c66d10276c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "267865d4-e8c6-50f1-8813-4866781fbd07" :question/math-academy-id "q-10102" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+2z &=1 \\\\ x+y+z &=3 \\\\ y+2z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(2)+2(1) \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d31247c-4643-5464-a30c-802705aaf50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236007} {:answer/id #uuid "3b878615-e206-52a0-bf52-3f4f50ce31b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236190} {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236358} {:answer/id #uuid "9e126482-0792-53fb-9f81-3febb0cec7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236807} {:answer/id #uuid "a16f91bd-4ea9-5f5b-b438-3d185dbe8f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236358} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea4542f-4e0e-5b2c-a84d-fa40f33689f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf78a5ba-4334-5b57-bc16-3a848e2f0dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-101590" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1d6f3e-9053-5690-b000-66d554fc0b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236110} {:answer/id #uuid "3532faea-48d8-5741-aa13-a64cbba7d647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ex}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236153} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1aa2e2-8a76-5713-9b50-46a772b85443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236316} {:answer/id #uuid "a1648056-d8f0-59f6-985d-fcda1cb5175d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{e}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236828} {:answer/id #uuid "d8be5480-6829-5df9-a0e9-113723bfaa4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236316} :answer-field/id #uuid "994834f1-4e9a-532e-b583-a55ce29002bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "319b8cc3-c619-5308-8487-fad516398fb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-101600" :question/problem "Given that $y=2eln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2eln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5235b2d2-41a6-571c-8e01-a03aeb01451b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236336} {:answer/id #uuid "7f36f138-72b0-5be3-99c0-4538ad3f84d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236605} {:answer/id #uuid "a14212af-f2a6-570c-8589-28ef6e251469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236826} {:answer/id #uuid "d3777975-3ec1-5d04-845f-b4ae29e575be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237153} {:answer/id #uuid "d511865a-f893-5e0e-83a5-afff6f3a9a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236826} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d97240d-1080-5a25-924c-3b346338aa89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "681375a5-0c42-5d37-8f3f-a0f73ddc205e" :question/math-academy-id "q-101604" :question/problem "Given that $y=-9ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "472e11dd-8537-5b30-ad11-59c4c14c5212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236273} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4f0ac8-1dab-5270-b011-493f700ff938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+8ln⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236509} {:answer/id #uuid "78e82567-e2b5-51dd-885b-f1af7319339c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236577} {:answer/id #uuid "90cac687-dd21-570e-a62a-f9e1d628421a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8ln⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236739} {:answer/id #uuid "97421f7d-e972-5d83-b422-5d436b8e6feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236577} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2000d3-5ddf-572d-be68-d0feed75463e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09ca9b0e-b19f-5aca-b14f-466e3c4366f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101726" :question/problem "Given that $y=3x-5+8{log}_{2}⁡x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5+8{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5)+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =3-0+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{8}{xln⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/ln⁡2} & ={(3+\\frac{8}{xln⁡2})|}_{x=1/ln⁡2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}\\cdot ln⁡2)} \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e58ea-86ce-5450-939e-02de03cdebfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236050} {:answer/id #uuid "2e56d505-7726-5af1-871c-95766070a7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236112} {:answer/id #uuid "5b86c06b-4025-5e46-b183-b3a382a369a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236390} {:answer/id #uuid "62be5c5d-404d-5b63-b96a-01802a0419b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236442} {:answer/id #uuid "f134bbd3-d5b3-51e4-9905-9b65f60e5fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236050} :answer-field/id #uuid "86665b82-4f5e-59d4-b139-f213cd90fc89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eeda4143-b348-582e-8960-ff5a4ad7dde2" :question/math-academy-id "q-10256" :question/problem "![](images/da/da529910e268ea4c124d7c27b085ca1799e255604459c539cc8d5809dda8f95a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a98e176-de94-501d-9654-ee79fa546361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235866} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbcf497-35c1-530e-8b8a-1e4520aff5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236401} {:answer/id #uuid "64a92041-6332-5b56-9a49-329279ae7898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236454} {:answer/id #uuid "c676c67b-ce2e-5ee3-a924-56c7b533c894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237076} {:answer/id #uuid "ea75476f-d42e-58e7-be1d-885fe5dc9408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237291} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8f08e3-d8d9-5787-8e9c-79e2c433c62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01f0c95b-ef07-5cce-9a1b-7f84a8200390" :question/math-academy-id "q-10257" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=2{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{cos}^{3}⁡t)=-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{3}⁡t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d582ab-25c3-5d89-b31c-eee9f2c1897c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236147} {:answer/id #uuid "671a11ec-27d8-52b9-9a59-8dd0d0ec54c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236466} {:answer/id #uuid "7dedda59-24a2-5ab6-bb9d-20a20b1f41ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236599} {:answer/id #uuid "c654b5a5-a703-55fd-9759-75e1cbdb9a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237074} {:answer/id #uuid "e227f87d-b8bb-5a30-92ed-b9b3c38e19ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236466} :answer-field/id #uuid "58761fb6-2552-5c63-b655-9819d63debb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27c17b75-37ea-5768-beae-14242dea6c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-10258" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,y=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa28b6-42d5-5647-8c67-6ad4ae934fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235862} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb02d36-4654-5167-a760-32462c48caf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235991} {:answer/id #uuid "3ead8f61-e137-5573-914b-52260b377c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236214} {:answer/id #uuid "478c7a12-33ef-5729-9381-971f513d4e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236277} {:answer/id #uuid "c3466be1-0698-5115-8533-dbaa1ef80524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235862} :answer-field/id #uuid "b04a5fcb-f5d2-5c81-be86-d5724d0bb948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "31e80fe7-2790-57b6-8a22-f24ca436c4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-10261" :question/problem "![](images/82/8288453c2625dac592a51054df332abf8ee49dbf8cbcabaf327c3ffe63b55f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+4-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d58fc9-233d-5a98-9f47-8a3790a441f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236443} {:answer/id #uuid "804c671d-3217-5280-aa02-fe2a73812ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236615} {:answer/id #uuid "88c43743-4bdd-5d6d-8482-949f26f0705c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236672} {:answer/id #uuid "944e0f31-80d6-5eac-8960-7ada905bec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236758} {:answer/id #uuid "d93bce69-5901-5324-bd97-83116ddf04a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236672} :answer-field/id #uuid "77c1b5e6-8017-5a27-ad73-0d376c902134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba2b3d0e-8f3a-5211-b064-2ffdb4af104d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102688" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,-1)=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be0c463-edb4-5b70-bb3e-8b6725c6a687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235993} {:answer/id #uuid "645b10c8-86f5-5531-9063-a8164bcd916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236453} {:answer/id #uuid "88340cf3-6a50-5271-95e9-c6eba2bc20d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236668} {:answer/id #uuid "967bd984-45f3-59ce-ae53-12a8f75333e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236773} {:answer/id #uuid "d0eb2000-ec27-545a-b0c5-217234035274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236453} :answer-field/id #uuid "b433b7ab-e64b-5175-a0d6-46daa6db6934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "adcce1ca-5433-59fb-aff5-f6facbbc8b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-102755" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 9-3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131f3abe-f8a2-5669-9886-def799d47576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235927} {:answer/id #uuid "2b612fff-53ea-514d-8a5b-b76c7c81f10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236089} {:answer/id #uuid "738df7e6-5156-5525-96bf-232a82f2db2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236550} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9618a-a75c-5f05-9717-45ae2330e19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236850} {:answer/id #uuid "a7505017-2816-551a-8db8-1abf0fd6e024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236864} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df7a8fa-8a02-5d72-8bb0-1c8583355179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "014f856a-246a-5cdf-b204-5bd60dd0e27e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102756" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfb2ad4-fa67-5792-8b5a-bb0cf61dae94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236099} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6389b2-e6ef-57e0-9bef-de9efd95310b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236692} {:answer/id #uuid "8f052d51-e97e-5d7e-8ee2-ea69ab6033be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236723} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c24bcf-0f31-5c24-ae39-b7fd92e40ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237082} {:answer/id #uuid "e3746c6f-2baa-5d78-bb32-b6322d8f2397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236692} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3820f69-7403-5b9d-a474-321a40bb1de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d7d77df-d1e1-57cf-b100-13b124e5f84c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102757" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨3,2⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨-6,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-12+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25757a6b-0934-5048-82f3-b1872f09625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236058} {:answer/id #uuid "7681af1b-9ec1-53a8-b458-886bf671a0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236559} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebad9c8-15e4-58ff-ad98-d9900ac0a3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236721} {:answer/id #uuid "bac9430a-dd6f-5e03-a05e-2e36c4335187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237012} {:answer/id #uuid "f9088ef8-1c5f-51c7-95f0-cb90fc5a2eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236058} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d6b8ea-54a8-58e8-b6f4-45d73f4a2279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dadf2056-3a4c-505a-a071-2867e2e0c57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102760" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15abd5a0-d823-5591-a936-77f5124070d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235948} {:answer/id #uuid "246bbd71-13ab-5f07-bf13-f84a10905517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236052} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3a7018-462b-5892-bfc8-f12a246602f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236606} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd2c6d6-c911-5817-93d4-a2bb73e2c1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236731} {:answer/id #uuid "fd50b349-aea2-530c-9367-89e894d39f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237424}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236731} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e6aa9e-8656-518e-8cee-819df6b6deaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d78b0711-46f8-5ff4-b111-2b027d41f69e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102773" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1(-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(6)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fae49b2-cd1c-586d-a8c7-6fb4ad86bb63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235898} {:answer/id #uuid "1b67df35-c823-5d37-b291-2b556c4e6c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235986} {:answer/id #uuid "35fff9f0-620d-5a8c-bdc2-dfce482c7957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236159} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d2abe2-5a67-5dfe-b4fa-665fe56ba2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236966} {:answer/id #uuid "e0af2809-d558-56bb-9ed8-d9ffad16ac35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236159} :answer-field/id #uuid "e10b51ec-b3a3-5c9e-b0b9-a50fb5372c57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fbbaa85-f7c0-579d-afb2-474f3846c51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102774" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-1)(3) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(4)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c228f5-54fc-5ef5-b7fa-50841413a64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235934} {:answer/id #uuid "39961e39-bc55-5d3a-92f3-aa5c6200f27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x<-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236182} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d9f260-dccd-585a-b532-95d62503e380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x<-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237062} {:answer/id #uuid "de9eb09c-253f-5040-9da9-19057889b89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{7}<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237214} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e9442b-117a-5d25-814a-c4da5ff44b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236182} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1f29ff-ed10-578d-b95f-3ea67464d752" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa64357a-e6c7-520e-a3fd-8fc5b09cc06a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102968" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{7}⁡(x+2)+1<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x+2)+1 & <0 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+2) & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $7.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+2)} & <{7}^{-1} \\\\ x+2 & <\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ x & <-\\frac{13}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⇒\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<-\\frac{13}{7},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{13}{7}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{13}{7})\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(-2,-\\frac{13}{7})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-2,-\\frac{13}{7}),$ which can also be written as $-2<x<-\\frac{13}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fade131-95a3-59ef-9154-0a3646cb9249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236120} {:answer/id #uuid "347570fe-05eb-5aeb-9a37-c7c29118ce61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236150} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6879c3-495b-59e2-8b10-9f7b4c2fb477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236601} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f158fe-9fdc-5116-9d86-bb16d50845f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{16}\\le x<\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236852} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a72748-3810-5229-90e4-7f3b94f0b440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\le x<\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236601} :answer-field/id #uuid "4853ea57-33fc-528e-a33f-9d2826fd60dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eef672bc-6f7f-5228-bdaa-a70d719658a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-102970" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)+3\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(3-2x) & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)} & \\ge {2}^{-3} \\\\ 3-2x & \\ge \\frac{1}{8} \\\\ -2x & \\ge -\\frac{23}{8} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{23}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n3-2x>0\\,⇒\\,x<\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{23}{16},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<\\frac{3}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}]\\cap (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})=(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}],$ which can also be written as $x\\le \\frac{23}{16}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482cd0a2-002c-5095-add4-19d1f47e0cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236282} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbae0dc-6caa-5073-8650-f067ac0ff2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236511} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1eee5e-fdc1-544c-94c6-0705585f9cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236687} {:answer/id #uuid "8b89bcda-c55a-5aa3-9d94-3e1c14e7b4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le x<\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236694} {:answer/id #uuid "e54038fa-fc46-5181-b23a-81dbd3b033a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}<x\\le \\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236687} :answer-field/id #uuid "f408d105-b906-5018-b063-1b15b4424201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5506f6d-7ff6-5f71-bdbd-49c5af679dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-102979" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(4x-1)\\ge {log}_{2}⁡(1-\\frac{x}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x-1)} & \\ge {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(1-x/4)} \\\\ 4x-1 & \\ge 1-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ \\frac{17x}{4} & \\ge 2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{x}{4} & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{8}{17},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{8}{17},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x<4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )$ and $(-\\infty ,4)$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{8}{17},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,4)=[\\frac{8}{17},4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{8}{17},4),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e29ce2-4554-5558-8c72-8422675e24c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236138} {:answer/id #uuid "3c164c19-1e2a-5b97-a1d5-77c725311763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236193} {:answer/id #uuid "66361318-9219-58c5-bcda-75129baaa714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236464} {:answer/id #uuid "8d01f3a1-7ccb-5a54-ae3f-83fd2498b7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236709} {:answer/id #uuid "b7289c68-237a-522e-b56e-57064d776048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236980} :answer-field/id #uuid "88322035-f59b-5d15-97b6-79b1ff1b2397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "721c17b8-891a-5aa8-81e5-901c21b4cfea" :question/math-academy-id "q-103081" :question/problem "The derivative of $y=x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =1-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd8d5ad-073e-55ee-92c1-3d941cfbf8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437300} {:answer/id #uuid "62442bed-3629-535b-844a-f1ed8cc3a547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437301} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b115472-4d06-5a5e-9cd3-15210c6010e5" :answer-field/key "The function is zero at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edfcf80-e21f-5e22-8902-7e7b1332485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437302} {:answer/id #uuid "9c74c49e-6c9c-5bab-9fb2-8efb50f61131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437302} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da1c4d9-8e1c-5e3d-8925-a2ad4ef01523" :answer-field/key "Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a3617f-5ddc-5f1e-9587-578cb2e5daae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437304} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3927d0-6855-5216-a9b2-c6bb7f951391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437304} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42ef86b-359c-5579-8280-6358310897d5" :answer-field/key "$y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4817b633-1cdd-52d9-b9a7-098236a7867f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103230" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$” is true. At $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, the function $y=cos⁡x$ takes the value zero.\n\n- Statements “Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$” and “$y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function” are false. The range of the function $y=cos⁡x$ is $y\\in [-1,1].$ Thus, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is not an output of this function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f7039a-2f3c-5e11-b655-d32aed6af515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437306} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e0e83d-6b7c-5018-bd20-49f606eedb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78118cd-b206-5d3b-aac4-77a392c6c22c" :answer-field/key "The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fbfbd2d-9a45-5a4b-b854-d3e63ee7830d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437308} {:answer/id #uuid "0c07a39f-6574-5ab6-9301-59c89b690d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437309} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b3d79d-1762-5848-9642-6db59cbc38e2" :answer-field/key "Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efed1b10-8121-5609-a029-df7005434c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437310} {:answer/id #uuid "82200c6e-534d-5c90-9855-36c2db1fd8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437310} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c76314-0f5c-552b-9634-7a579528542d" :answer-field/key "The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4afb86c8-970f-594e-bb88-36f6e2c3e725" :question/math-academy-id "q-103233" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $” is false. The zeros occur at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…,\n$$ which can be written as $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$” is true. At $x=0,$ the function has the value $y=cos⁡0=1.$ Therefore, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Statement “The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$” is false. The function has a maximum at $x=0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=2n\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe07cef-135a-5aee-b857-d8421aa48542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235900} {:answer/id #uuid "13614331-5578-515d-8943-8af84bd83334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235929} {:answer/id #uuid "89e2a1f7-298a-5958-86d7-db27000a04bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236680} {:answer/id #uuid "bb07dd52-f518-54f6-8f86-fe701c1ada44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237014} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f1e392-4f36-5478-9ad8-f088d40891d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237242} :answer-field/id #uuid "9baaf465-f7d4-5147-8968-33136247a92f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d276077-f8d6-5c9d-ba5b-27234154bb37" :question/math-academy-id "q-103242" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x+12\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot 6\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x+12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x+12\\pi )=-\\frac{5}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215fe18b-9f23-5c09-bf88-127529513e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236030} {:answer/id #uuid "706dc48b-2178-5af0-963c-8392b5818616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236528} {:answer/id #uuid "852258c8-e623-5916-a3ff-e2ea52c2dac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236648} {:answer/id #uuid "dab49c4d-12af-58e7-98db-dab868211f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237203} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d0f57a-a36f-5ba2-9697-d3011f1d0734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236528} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c72a16-c41c-5f7d-a59f-cf1e101272bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c027c724-5ec4-54a9-b208-10f063932ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103243" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14927c29-0886-540d-95a2-4473de589ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235940} {:answer/id #uuid "42b67f48-9900-5969-bb84-e376aa0e142a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236244} {:answer/id #uuid "b71cb3ce-28e0-585f-9894-ebfd9f88d9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236978} {:answer/id #uuid "bae2f1a4-b12f-55b2-94e9-674f5ea009e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237013} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3be85b-3abb-5d86-a3c0-ca3e422113ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235940} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f2a3ce-c6d5-5bd3-a262-29e4efe476df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4c5a6195-b8ed-52d4-86b4-4695109267db" :question/math-academy-id "q-103279" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5 & =4\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62743dd-6b08-537d-99a1-950e0129a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437312} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6522af-a085-5421-9c2f-2778b4ad8bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437313} :answer-field/id #uuid "7795f1b0-ab3b-568b-86a9-79ad8f59e765" :answer-field/key "The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cebc6c-fb9e-5e2f-a4dc-892c8730f19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437314} {:answer/id #uuid "7c865e34-f1b0-5ac5-896e-1465217ac529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437314} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a680935-e5c9-5bc1-9251-f1ef3483d3ef" :answer-field/key "The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438686} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1a71c62-3700-5f58-82ca-cdcc35abc891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437316} {:answer/id #uuid "f8103b2e-08e3-58da-8184-32ba8bfa5da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437316} :answer-field/id #uuid "895e53e0-0454-5c88-bc63-752876bb1bff" :answer-field/key "The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2215b590-8140-5323-92bf-8ae24e1b7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103289" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/73/732955302ca49a41f6c43cf242d7c869e630e88d804c4b56a5beb3b081f2d230.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $” is true. The function is defined everywhere, except where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n$” is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$” is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b1a6cb-d9b2-5499-9bed-f6591d1d13f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437318} {:answer/id #uuid "6d863ba2-7deb-5dbd-a418-153187c043da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437318} :answer-field/id #uuid "b085e966-e913-5fdf-8891-5d9c59a6837a" :answer-field/key "The vertical asymptotes of the function are $x=2n\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff2e6d4-d7e6-5cbf-8bd1-6ae518e69246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437320} {:answer/id #uuid "4419d897-28a5-5bc9-9d30-6eda47c75fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437321} :answer-field/id #uuid "772ee15e-615c-53a7-b9f0-b705e3e4cbdd" :answer-field/key "Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438689} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a96a27-f532-56b1-b071-6cec50f8503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437322} {:answer/id #uuid "d47830c6-0653-572d-a8a7-41bbd043f9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437323} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d41179-1f25-5250-b6ec-ea5a42b8c13b" :answer-field/key "$tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6f5925ee-40c3-50af-9950-ecf1ea188724" :question/math-academy-id "q-103295" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2d0606829588b01f6cba2fa4785b201c47a6d9ffa325d54e64544c9769d7e4a.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement “The vertical asymptotes of the function are $x=2n\\pi $” is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$” is true. Indeed, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,0).$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})\n$$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$”; $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})\n$$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13daedb0-269b-5528-8bf1-ef0e7db6d1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235936} {:answer/id #uuid "28c4e15f-0467-5409-9d3f-33bb38a32677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236073} {:answer/id #uuid "49879257-8766-5452-b769-0d155fc10028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236286} {:answer/id #uuid "91eebbc5-a9e0-57e2-8f0e-fea041e01a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236744} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3b257a-4b42-5083-8400-b4b598baf86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235936} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5d55f1-e6e1-538b-8968-4634effa5b5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "232fc51c-a784-5a0a-ab11-96190800b2c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103307" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=\\frac{1}{3},$ for some value of $x,$ find the value of $12tan⁡(x+5\\pi )-6.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+5\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x+5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $tan⁡x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get that $tan⁡(x+5\\pi )=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n12tan⁡(x+5\\pi )-6=12\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-6=4-6=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2efa17-ff90-5110-b52d-af24637f4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235979} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8cca99-6d65-568c-a0f8-a0fd7fe46ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236118} {:answer/id #uuid "707b921b-1acb-53f3-8f3a-b5470f3888af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236530} {:answer/id #uuid "a845e9d4-c867-568d-aa25-63440fc514dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236870} {:answer/id #uuid "aa563b5d-37dd-55e3-947b-a55588cc5905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236118} :answer-field/id #uuid "14995e9f-f94a-5811-a957-cbe2b6653873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89698ed1-75aa-53ec-b284-4e5a8a12ecbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103312" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=16,$ for some value of $x,$ find the value of $\\frac{3}{4}tan⁡(x-9\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+(-9)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-9\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $tan⁡x=16,$ we get that $tan⁡(x-9\\pi )=16.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}tan⁡(x-9\\pi )=\\frac{3}{4}(16)=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116ed1cb-cee2-51fa-b983-0d044a80cbf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+ln⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235911} {:answer/id #uuid "335087d8-fab5-5522-94d1-93d0d9655755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}-ln⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236140} {:answer/id #uuid "a77100c8-1980-51d5-9cdf-fe3f251c5336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236865} {:answer/id #uuid "b3489f8b-789f-5fa4-bb29-debd57283e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}-ln⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236952} {:answer/id #uuid "e22f3892-01f6-5e59-8632-9444a5c795ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236865} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1ee9b-e334-5197-b749-02b7be8b5397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d804d2ab-c752-5a11-a731-3f70b2a95171" :question/math-academy-id "q-103374" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-6}{2x}\\,dx.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-6}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2x}-\\frac{3}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35399b5c-61ff-5ca5-be4f-4c1abe0d88b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236154} {:answer/id #uuid "6a533669-c40f-5a00-940e-dadb7f469489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236488} {:answer/id #uuid "7fce5c0b-fbff-51c0-9373-cb763d114999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236610} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b6f3b-c7d8-5d5b-a12e-71a1116a0a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} {:answer/id #uuid "c8374c9a-abe6-515a-a258-f3f08a5ed775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+ln⁡(3K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236725} :answer-field/id #uuid "356e1270-3ef3-5f47-b27e-aac16e288e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12ca3bb3-d360-53db-af13-e44e401ba3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103375" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|+\\frac{9{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|-\\frac{9}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=3ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡|x|+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23384ad2-5c2b-5675-b1cb-2712d43e696a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236040} {:answer/id #uuid "8697299b-732b-5dac-b7fd-937118c6f358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236655} {:answer/id #uuid "e2988a8a-d70e-5324-b36e-117de13ef56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237238} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d4487-26a1-5a24-b5cb-498b6c3eac2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237408} {:answer/id #uuid "ffae31c4-dedd-5981-9bf8-843ca631db3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237238} :answer-field/id #uuid "d03682b7-7b79-5a58-b9e6-26e92886a8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "05650b77-8467-5403-8d37-57ab51debfc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-103590" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{10}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $10$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc5000e-2b32-58d7-ad16-c7de786b85e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(K|x|)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236225} {:answer/id #uuid "89e62549-2f57-5be7-80e7-7b01223e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡\\left(\\frac{K|x|}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236681} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} {:answer/id #uuid "a162783a-2e70-5f31-8790-4cae86e77c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡(K|x|)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236827} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b65fc-6ad6-5255-ad37-78be530512d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} :answer-field/id #uuid "684eb99d-9162-5ff2-afed-d23e9ffc201a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "57af19c5-8aad-56d8-8498-0ea830a26c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-103603" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $2{x}^{-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7c9cd2-6944-5863-bc69-62975c2906f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235876} {:answer/id #uuid "98578c54-03fb-5056-afe0-ab2b99fb4ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236782} {:answer/id #uuid "ab22f6fd-a96c-5284-bc89-14c5b0fabbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236898} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f243d1-8d1e-59c0-ba0c-0e8ecf122ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237190} {:answer/id #uuid "f855a1eb-9012-5b0e-946f-0d40498df3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b49880a5-1e24-51a4-9758-7aac4e75c637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c44a1c94-220c-5631-9e8d-0fa005c996ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103659" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e650522d-7d29-56a7-9d2a-a7366159781f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447666} {:answer/id #uuid "dc16e3da-5bd2-59c3-b215-ad267ab230ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447666} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19fe135-c616-5657-a6a1-2c289676f144" :answer-field/key "The range of the function is $y=(-\\infty ,-1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2d1b275-589d-55c0-856e-c1cd772557c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447668} {:answer/id #uuid "0c600491-3073-5d6d-af02-741e0459c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447668} :answer-field/id #uuid "75aee04b-be6f-5c41-b69d-bb24de8e7a75" :answer-field/key "$y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is an output of the function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae0e786-ec20-5fee-affb-d54045e12f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447670} {:answer/id #uuid "4a901910-3a43-5066-90cc-fea69b955c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447671} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaaac92a-feb5-5a84-8a55-b1c49a2f457a" :answer-field/key "$x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not a zero of the function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50bb01da-0246-5da5-b527-fc7a59519d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-103685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0f3e760be991c936baf5e9825cd582f086a73a8330032d24fd35fd5594b4864.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statements “The range of the function is $y=(-\\infty ,-1]$” and “$y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is an output of the function” are false. The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not an output of the function.\n\n- Statement “$x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not a zero of the function” is true since the function has no zeros."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02539af9-e857-51c7-84f3-870030588bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235810} {:answer/id #uuid "47a76f90-be63-5bf9-afd6-6ad5fbe85755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236278} {:answer/id #uuid "58ca919f-bec5-5e25-8319-36fc7f5e9098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236377} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a72551-5740-5d8d-9c0e-0b1efe13eded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236975} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c892b1-5afd-5ee9-8134-4e26339cf484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236278} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6503bc-35b2-51cc-a2f2-93c060a0d5d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f24e30dd-0b9f-5a83-b48d-b514813ba28b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103691" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then what is $sec⁡(x+8\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then $sec⁡(x+8\\pi )=3\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1868d529-5263-5edb-9f3c-7f0a2e46b4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235968} {:answer/id #uuid "28810aa3-39c0-55f5-8c74-a7073e21260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236071} {:answer/id #uuid "3f49e961-d212-5574-a9ba-1aae64f3b1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236219} {:answer/id #uuid "7333be87-91bd-581d-8249-28136980f4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236547} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fe76ae-fb99-57a9-9fe7-919e644fbaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236071} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e59f54-9e51-5fbd-9a16-83e9631379ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0324006-ba15-5af8-b792-48d00a58b824" :question/math-academy-id "q-103692" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{7},$ then what is $sec⁡(x-16\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x-16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{7},$ then $sec⁡(x-16\\pi )=\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "301add2e-4b2e-5b38-ba20-4c2da0bc5e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236125} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8608c7-9db3-5f9a-996b-15e401c114b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236302} {:answer/id #uuid "81dbd4c2-1d03-5f20-bbee-602f5efaa7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236625} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7449d4-b70c-5521-af3f-4bd59d62a834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236795} {:answer/id #uuid "f98af8e2-123d-52bc-8ecb-18673f9bedab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237393}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9288e8e-03d7-572b-8a87-b8c6acc40b31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "958f113f-43d0-503b-a06b-6788d1032931" :question/math-academy-id "q-103701" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then what is $sec⁡(x+14\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05453a19-05cd-5c8b-893a-dcc33caa1462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235836} {:answer/id #uuid "248943a6-4cb4-51fa-87df-2e7411a53bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236053} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7a4b67-d644-5aa3-8c4a-7ae0c9d0064f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236522} {:answer/id #uuid "b1da97b2-8ba2-5c1c-9a5a-b8cbe5fdb185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236945} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ad195d-44ce-55ef-89f3-f2656116ed6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235836} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb76649b-f6f5-500d-8650-e92b4e13bd88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a39634fa-bc7a-59a9-a879-3625bc7c31ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-103706" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then what is $\\sqrt{5}sec⁡(x+20\\pi )-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 10\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+20\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then $sec⁡(x+20\\pi )=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}sec⁡(x+20\\pi )-3 & =\\sqrt{5}(2\\sqrt{5})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ab5490-6958-5907-9744-daca5ba83c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-6{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235917} {:answer/id #uuid "23a1d755-1a8c-5412-a736-df2f34a61feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236046} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0f10c6-f01a-58b2-9e7c-e6f93eb8b719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+6{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236109} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5e3191-58c4-58b2-85d9-7d6953772bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236705} {:answer/id #uuid "96af5361-9440-53c2-b581-6ff64a3f4000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236046} :answer-field/id #uuid "051b7d4c-b18a-50b8-837c-5476d5b85f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "616e09b3-d157-50df-b328-51943da5bea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-103832" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e00f277-5143-5957-904d-a9dd31eb0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235892} {:answer/id #uuid "24bfddc8-84e0-588a-8616-05b13b833b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236054} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffac8cf-a6de-5f8b-b74d-5b6a9c38c64d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236123} {:answer/id #uuid "3673d5cc-88d2-50a8-a857-56a93c0d35f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236165} {:answer/id #uuid "5727ed7b-95e5-5433-8814-34b439a62973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236369} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe185be0-4aea-5d12-8405-2c55fa532bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aaa400a5-0216-5218-b483-3ceee85bd49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103834" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1bd01b4fe70a497cc66811a2d5b070908221b9932655ee396d9a8039471702c2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67b16f6-dc70-5d8b-9e47-2622982f487a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447672} {:answer/id #uuid "64620df8-e9fe-52ef-805c-2735ff596a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447672} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c0c7e36-7fa6-5632-b634-da3ec87f60da" :answer-field/key "The function has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5efab06-808a-55ce-99e2-98546c6701ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447674} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b73384-07ac-54b7-b92a-733a7793478e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447674} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a69a867-51ee-5250-be62-b7dd01e44c96" :answer-field/key "The domain of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1a94dee-8c64-57f6-82ef-5179129198fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447676} {:answer/id #uuid "e0562874-5693-5737-bbd2-a529e3eaac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447677} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4a53dfa-87de-5875-8f96-e8e1e35887fe" :answer-field/key "The period of the function is $\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e25815e7-4d17-5965-b1f7-fc15c12bd9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-103836" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/7785ad73f6e5212772c3053780ffd055f37cfb39927b6ddc3e40cd3a55b8ba4e.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$” is false. The vertical asymptotes of the function are integer multiples of $\\pi $, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The domain of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$” is false. The domain of the function is all real numbers except the values where the asymptotes occur, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The period of the function is $\\pi .$” is true. The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47084e96-4575-5ea2-bc29-d0119689a067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236272} {:answer/id #uuid "5c665951-8f9f-5315-b3f3-bac47dafdacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236396} {:answer/id #uuid "8127b6a0-4402-576d-a67a-897bdcc56f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236622} {:answer/id #uuid "d6287872-577f-5bdf-aa77-b5a797c91de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237171} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3ea81c-a5ed-52a6-801d-d9a3a71605f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236272} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca36830-c9ce-5e40-8c39-1b9f25663b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "57b3d195-da37-5216-80d4-504f546a07a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103837" :question/problem "![](images/33/3387790df770ac8645058c6f460b1f1285629d18d765668e362a8070ae4035ed.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "299c49ae-444e-50f6-85ca-6998b96136f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236078} {:answer/id #uuid "33d518b7-98ff-5513-8323-afc5f84fcc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236146} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6f42f9-2aaf-5114-9331-5e3c9dcdcd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236602} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d1084b-102c-5bff-b9ca-5cb0eb8cef1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236876} {:answer/id #uuid "c7aac726-61fe-5fa4-950e-291834c42379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236078} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f38ef5-cc99-5834-9b80-444f9f589ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d68f0a3d-2213-57b9-acdb-bffbcdc7fcae" :question/math-academy-id "q-103838" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=-4$ for some $x,$ then find the value of $3cot⁡(x+17\\pi )+6.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡x=cot⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=17$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x & =cot⁡(x+17\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =cot⁡(x+17\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot⁡x=-4,$ then $cot⁡(x+17\\pi )=-4.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3cot⁡(x+7\\pi )+6 & =3(-4)+6 \\\\  & =-12+6 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0133e54e-7ade-5d8b-b7fa-b6cdff889edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235805} {:answer/id #uuid "3660dfe5-e65e-5e65-9338-96cec3d962a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236164} {:answer/id #uuid "45573a5f-5931-539c-8a89-f57b3d53c9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236263} {:answer/id #uuid "c59bbfef-cc44-5971-95b5-e6281558c8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237066} {:answer/id #uuid "c636293e-d437-503f-aa20-b93e18e79a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237066} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab78bd9-7893-52b2-899c-399456a77a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d57f6ea5-a880-5f42-b5cf-5e3500fe1168" :question/math-academy-id "q-103839" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{5}$ for some $x$, then find the value of $2\\sqrt{5}cot⁡(x-3\\pi )-8.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡x=cot⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-3$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x=cot⁡(x+(-3)\\cdot \\pi )=cot⁡(x-3\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{5},$ then $cot⁡(x-3\\pi )=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}cot⁡(x-3\\pi )-8 & =2\\sqrt{5}(\\sqrt{5})-8 \\\\  & =2{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}-8 \\\\  & =10-8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034f25bd-dd66-5a88-a3ad-2f14d82420eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235816} {:answer/id #uuid "76dbbd9c-ab14-5090-9621-dcde636288e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236564} {:answer/id #uuid "8d93ec33-6eea-5578-8ed2-6e48fa6ef808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236712} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a4deb0-d6bb-59c2-8725-df2e92c862db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237048} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2aa39a-cb3e-54bb-