"selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96931358-f8ea-56cc-a85a-c2b66216308c" :question/math-academy-id "q-103842" :question/problem "![](images/03/038d135bd0d8d163808fe21be0159a1a08db37c3e33599c97033ca0d53abab36.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\n$$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "01edfff5-f123-51ba-a62f-aed1989315e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3cos⁡x-sec⁡xtan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238096} {:answer/id #uuid "0342af53-2f55-5c64-ada7-5e6f534a392d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{x}-{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238108} {:answer/id #uuid "0442097d-1b68-55b7-9cb8-c462931d1330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592