ove, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a787f9-cc71-5ee2-a0e1-b99585c88d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253705} {:answer/id #uuid "16d56a41-211d-5c70-8028-81878a75223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253866} {:answer/id #uuid "81b5e797-3b0a-5b21-8981-ffb0b7deb98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254819} {:answer/id #uuid "9a317ce9-dc1c-5e53-a908-c201f6c07a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255031} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c8b4d7-5e3d-529c-932f-88f0c9e1da29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253866} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7eb9b1b-f118-5a2b-a9b0-5db7af729615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ee8111c-4b37-517d-b1b7-ddd1df3f014f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120370" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={157.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {157.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{315}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0563d615-ffa2-5a2e-a34a-325a3b3cd4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253709} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3a13ca-0c91-59ed-aa40-aec07d5043d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254324} {:answer/id #uuid "79b3ea54-b3ba-5165-91dc-a1dbc40be7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254747} {:answer/id #uuid "7b13a578-e470-566f-813b-75474312fb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254763} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fe877d-f681-58d7-ad42-7a9c39a14713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254747} :answer-field/id #uuid "460c587f-198c-589f-9fc8-f74bfbf671fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93375bd6-9a3b-584f-abb5-493a4e9eddf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120631" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{({sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d2b769-b7bb-53fc-b947-c01c1ce161df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253851} {:answer/id #uuid "78a4b292-89ce-53d4-8475-fdda7bfe40c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254736} {:answer/id #uuid "a7224c22-fb60-5cd3-8f52-95f22c5e7f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255146} {:answer/id #uuid "bd06b950-4b37-5d7d-b639-ef073390a2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255361} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e3c92f-f95d-57db-a7ca-85394f5ddfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255850} :answer-field/id #uuid "7edb85ea-33a3-57f0-b0e9-29b6a66d467c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7483b5c0-3dc3-538f-b1c8-e2da92110f3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120637" :question/problem "${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4f3067-f5e2-5603-9f47-57a0e3782b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254310} {:answer/id #uuid "5aad7655-1dee-53ff-8800-4cd4da4305ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254470} {:answer/id #uuid "61c26385-74d8-5f38-8da9-58ca9b0204ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254546} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad6c2be-49e3-553c-a08c-58f79bce0594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254896} {:answer/id #uuid "d7945be3-0336-5203-b186-a3b5358f30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254310} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd23819b-a703-54dc-9f4f-5b6b30b48f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c121a545-3cc0-5d24-b274-39c1f2ae43da" :question/math-academy-id "q-120639" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a4def07-0628-5e63-a45e-449d2fb41e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254755} {:answer/id #uuid "870b4e4d-b7b2-567b-bb1b-157ef931ec5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254866} {:answer/id #uuid "b8560c08-8478-572e-bf56-91ad804c7cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255306} {:answer/id #uuid "d27d8aea-8cec-579f-be87-e42b719d066e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255546} {:answer/id #uuid "f7209d95-74f8-5507-9792-0f25f463ca06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254755} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aaecc1f-343b-5eda-a874-8b7a904d8ba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0185c52-5699-5bfa-957f-ad7bf856cfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120644" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a70c5da-3781-5b2a-b3d0-01d415d0046b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254048} {:answer/id #uuid "8da594c9-9f18-5bc3-bfe0-749dcf73d631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254919} {:answer/id #uuid "a91439d8-b0c3-5c88-bd0f-ee449c283038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255160} {:answer/id #uuid "bb95475e-c50d-5ad1-890b-13bb54c0f893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255342} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ffa73-fd1f-5920-9e2b-467b23740386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254048} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fbf6cbd-b928-5593-9590-60e92df061ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "073899cb-a0ec-51ac-92ec-faf9fd7d512e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120651" :question/problem "$\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c1a484-6908-532c-aead-33b816e61909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254267} {:answer/id #uuid "90079a6b-99a3-58e1-ba8f-4b3406cda978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254935} {:answer/id #uuid "a99148bc-1b88-5e2f-b08f-0fc60dd809e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255167} {:answer/id #uuid "b54f4930-c7c7-5a78-8cae-4edc271582ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255268} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bfeaa7-27c9-5375-a531-9b28a3d3f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255697} :answer-field/id #uuid "12c1913c-48ce-5922-b686-38b55407977e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c59c3ea2-945d-5573-80ad-ccc1ae2f546f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120652" :question/problem "$\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-(1-{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c60bbc-557b-5be8-b035-a4f25b8b14e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254000} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae75e6e-d9a7-53a7-9c93-e93a17e83d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254181} {:answer/id #uuid "62801300-ea8a-5736-87ba-6643e169f421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254553} {:answer/id #uuid "c17929ca-1fca-5cc0-9d5f-1d5cb0ef90a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255397} {:answer/id #uuid "cff0c88e-228c-5701-9884-837468e6d734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254000} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd0a44a-becd-5faf-b1eb-0e6fd1304b9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea2a7b2e-b8a5-5fa2-860c-a68a4d82e89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120655" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}=\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce8fd15-b91e-59d1-81c9-58484689b5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253776} {:answer/id #uuid "0eba9484-8340-5e15-861e-9c9af85a9097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253797} {:answer/id #uuid "426cdd7e-6f1c-501f-9e15-f8954d1d45c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254236} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0f5c03-c859-58db-8cab-b40af3b4dbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254342} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ef8d91-6b04-56af-97d6-aafedb51fd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254236} :answer-field/id #uuid "83a7647a-f01a-56fa-ba8f-a9dac8ad5b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1861048-306a-5c9d-bc3a-136c2abd3f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120657" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1600150b-e299-5a29-a081-d6ad757d6024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253861} {:answer/id #uuid "36ee151d-ca7d-5e3c-aa38-15c8fba9ba8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254143} {:answer/id #uuid "786f3e80-9812-5d1a-ab02-79e48cb0864d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254734} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0e7801-7ae0-50c7-ad06-185952a47da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255467} {:answer/id #uuid "d41aae6a-4701-5934-a51e-3ae99324f0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255556}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e13b05c-91cb-5450-bcc2-18278fee0b59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "236e7298-9f51-5ebd-93c2-69c07e82f7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-120660" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1 & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}}-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b05a139-7aa1-53b5-b36e-50eba4ec3401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254051} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd7159d-b505-5b2a-9037-00f16c93bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254338} {:answer/id #uuid "67835721-9c6c-51b0-ad92-02a37d3e0f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254607} {:answer/id #uuid "70b93435-2f26-5881-a8f2-a0c3f339f156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254668} {:answer/id #uuid "e65c3fb3-45ae-5836-9776-610237f69f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254051} :answer-field/id #uuid "64389f47-01d0-5bd8-bffa-3868e67d38fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bba4c94f-f8c9-5ab5-a2b2-4d71544990cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-120661" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)^{2}} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05207395-02f1-5d5f-9dc3-81acf6c01fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253706} {:answer/id #uuid "295e109a-6294-5fe6-9b3a-bf16636fb649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254040} {:answer/id #uuid "851af266-742f-54a9-a2c7-0b59d5c8ba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254852} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa7902-3669-5423-ac92-e34b15e642b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254967} {:answer/id #uuid "fa687d80-88a9-583e-b3f7-707957e10df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254852} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef30c5cd-87ee-54bb-9ffe-d545fe802caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b8a55c6e-077f-5804-ac89-ec1be2eb0726" :question/math-academy-id "q-120666" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression and use again $(2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)-1 \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3959de01-495d-544d-9125-44db1e7db8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254171} {:answer/id #uuid "77f842c4-7d54-5c19-b720-bb9d73585d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254730} {:answer/id #uuid "af23b77f-bd4a-5f78-9caa-a4980a60efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255210} {:answer/id #uuid "df9c3e1e-05fa-505d-99c4-64e78d20d579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255655} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb4522b-0809-53f0-aa52-1dc2ba32a95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255655} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1db88f-e765-5456-a536-941d43bf50d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ba60b74f-b26e-513b-8666-50189177373f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120667" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0cdb3c-fb26-59c1-933d-aa2024348c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253745} {:answer/id #uuid "1de75852-c7d3-5842-951d-cfd1e0a14b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253921} {:answer/id #uuid "644722ee-1bb5-5291-ba1c-e832404a2120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254567} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca99b2-ee2d-5993-a662-a216864fcaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254875} {:answer/id #uuid "dd787f7a-8a7e-5397-9b33-b1f85f80136c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{tan}^{4}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254875} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f9d053-e97d-55f1-8264-1a2ea92818d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e823d758-1614-515f-a561-96579da144df" :question/math-academy-id "q-120806" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1=({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use simplify our expression and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} & =({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2} \\\\  & =({tan}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={tan}^{4}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1={tan}^{4}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f6f627-2f5f-5347-9503-ff016a5b6080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253702} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc1c20c-56c7-5e18-9f59-20d43cd28e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253786} {:answer/id #uuid "314c66e0-d6ef-5926-95cd-7d162c322e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254100} {:answer/id #uuid "391480c9-2b7d-5c4c-af59-42877898e593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254165} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7a2ee0-bb9d-53c4-bd98-ed75d405853a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254165} :answer-field/id #uuid "77493d8d-47a6-584a-b0bf-26e515610f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "93b7d709-aec4-5e7c-b981-7907596e3508" :question/math-academy-id "q-120807" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}={cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ec5f68-9728-531c-a5ac-13c79f005e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254688} {:answer/id #uuid "94b3e784-be6b-57fa-81c2-67618b3ed429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254976} {:answer/id #uuid "aa29f67a-9529-55c6-826f-4b22638ea11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255174} {:answer/id #uuid "cb767150-a33c-5e2f-9db5-2a5e59fdef7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255477} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f93ec-a0fa-5b8d-8457-2f8b02e7dfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255477} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b1f671-fc3b-5f37-ac21-8a3aecfff2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "38275387-16ad-5210-9a86-c85c7908724b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120808" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155c45f0-3e06-5b9f-9c35-5c6ac49993e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253855} {:answer/id #uuid "20a207af-afec-5bc7-970c-6f2fb486d01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{4}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253957} {:answer/id #uuid "51a6ca2d-69ad-550c-8763-e6e9817426d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254377} {:answer/id #uuid "66af897e-5095-58f3-86e9-f42ca2beabb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{4}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254594} {:answer/id #uuid "6edfb8f4-8dd7-5241-aed2-c6ec5108de96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254594} :answer-field/id #uuid "330ae8fd-1ee7-5f5a-8b2c-1abe69ac81f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7513b531-411e-52b6-bdf7-4cddcd08fc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-120811" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2}+2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2}+2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1 & =({tan}^{2}⁡x)^{2}+2\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2} & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sec}^{4}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2}+2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1={sec}^{4}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0373251e-751a-5fc9-9a1b-edff90c48991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={cos}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253697} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5d8494-53c7-5ff3-9917-1726228d7409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254633} {:answer/id #uuid "812d40a6-7b27-50e7-a82c-c40780f8e7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254813} {:answer/id #uuid "d32072fb-9cf8-592c-96bb-ac157b819612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255553} {:answer/id #uuid "f303656f-8dd7-5cf9-99ed-f5c7cbe9d0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254813} :answer-field/id #uuid "d169037d-aea5-528e-868f-946fa64e54fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed245dea-226c-5469-a3a0-44e480a6cf7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122393" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={sin}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2101350a-8239-5482-b969-02deba016e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253961} {:answer/id #uuid "99035da6-f3f2-54b5-b981-b2a041a9b5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255022} {:answer/id #uuid "9eca87f8-0f83-5f18-9369-ff1f5bab115c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255069} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb814d0-e382-5c62-a7c8-deebd241e10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255498} {:answer/id #uuid "f1104489-4015-57f8-8ee5-881460bd6945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2+4{e}^{7}}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255022} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f3c835-a9de-59ad-bcd7-3b129870afab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d260e2aa-2690-5911-adb6-91c0182848ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-122441" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e27774d260c053a7835b30e62c2d4b41be4b8dc22efcba8f7aa95704590561cf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as  \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\,{e}^{u}{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =4({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{3}}-\\frac{4}{{e}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae37851-a0d1-53e2-b501-6d36f95804d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254473} {:answer/id #uuid "6660ad6b-14ab-59ff-aae0-62de46bd7c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254591} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d5f8a9-f3a6-5e11-b9a9-f21dc78cfe93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255388} {:answer/id #uuid "c200c6de-7fef-560d-818d-ca3a15b06302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255403} {:answer/id #uuid "ee479383-a77b-513c-bb8f-65445e7f3153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255754} :answer-field/id #uuid "1204ced4-8121-563f-8cb8-ab8c27eb4c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "214438e3-d9f9-5d15-b742-ace73de66292" :question/math-academy-id "q-122489" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f15af8c-32d6-5aa3-b320-b89e907fe7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253938} {:answer/id #uuid "852a03b1-1167-5ff8-958a-c32cfaebddcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254854} {:answer/id #uuid "953e7744-4e3d-590a-88af-b3e0a91bf19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254985} {:answer/id #uuid "982ec2aa-735d-53d6-ac72-9b22316aa3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255014} {:answer/id #uuid "bf19f1aa-b923-5a95-91e9-8bafce7cd836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255373} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ed9b4f-68da-5e9f-9b1c-a422b25f34a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "892ed6cc-0843-54c9-a9ff-34ccf4e6699c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122504" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=25?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-1,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a776373-f429-5be0-ba85-32f70d52ce11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254311} {:answer/id #uuid "5598da66-919b-5760-acaf-a9ba9d0f5bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254417} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa15fe8-b053-529e-84d6-7f8f1a917a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254469} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9999a0-0d5e-535e-a58e-5d84feddc2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254513} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a21c4d-4259-5fa3-80ea-1bd6ff39ab24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254469} :answer-field/id #uuid "c27de963-889a-5fb8-9fdc-d9457ea163b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "23bdc624-e12a-5128-a012-26376205e5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122509" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{3},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=36?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $6$ centered at $(1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }((6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)^{3})\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5520fe-6ab1-5d68-b311-7d6bbbfb4a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253784} {:answer/id #uuid "66b868b9-92c9-5245-bf92-5b434425ba8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254595} {:answer/id #uuid "73d16daf-1804-5c1e-bec7-d32ecdd1b4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254694} {:answer/id #uuid "9a42e08d-134d-5414-be69-9f60dcfad824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255032} {:answer/id #uuid "d29d20a7-3189-5479-84cb-33d33d3909e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253784} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b064df-3568-5880-bd1a-d500949fc69c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0117a72-0d3a-519c-be24-ba7b707d85db" :question/math-academy-id "q-122568" :question/problem "![](images/76/761643a777650909b8c7ea5477b4fe246ac4032fe183df5d9ad5cb5e08ac1ecc.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5b5878-e66c-5d89-a069-df95f1af2ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253807} {:answer/id #uuid "2089a759-6345-5eb6-a52d-89eeac81f26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253954} {:answer/id #uuid "8790c59b-26d8-5124-b4e3-563fab281d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254872} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0962bc-a03c-5602-9533-d839323ade83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255196} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a65a67-f000-530e-8c27-8720d208565f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253954} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ce45c6-93a5-5940-8785-b3ea519f3645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e86005d4-e24a-5391-881b-d539e48399be" :question/math-academy-id "q-122570" :question/problem "![](images/44/4403bd230338dee226429d3f8f3ac8eaede1152aff0b6b18bd2e7ad9f3a3b8e3.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}$ along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e25f90d-4eba-51e9-9c10-92596577dce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253790} {:answer/id #uuid "46e4335d-ff19-506e-9ba3-baf59da9b85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254270} {:answer/id #uuid "5660c651-944f-5e37-9d2a-bae3abe05290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254430} {:answer/id #uuid "792ce311-05a4-5b24-bd61-c3e021e63d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254744} {:answer/id #uuid "8129299e-f565-5b69-8982-bff3fadcad2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf560a84-d70c-5563-a7d2-34dd9c9a62f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3791cf9-31ca-5ac2-874d-a229dc8db457" :question/math-academy-id "q-122576" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt[3]{y+1}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $6$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt[3]{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =6{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a10f201-92c1-5e24-a44f-00da36be42a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254458} {:answer/id #uuid "6024ef46-fb9b-5a80-8340-8aec0ee1ddb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254530} {:answer/id #uuid "b76eb8ba-8345-54e5-80e2-6aaa674f671f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255296} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdc0132-fa84-542c-8ffe-9876cd8ff77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255731} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3e25cf-0e52-56fc-bd4e-432e9387087f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255731} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca86e6b6-0f72-55e9-91bb-d1066f8ecb1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "151e5563-0581-510d-abbf-3279630a4d40" :question/math-academy-id "q-122578" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ traversed from the point $(-5,0)$ to the point $(0,-5)$ in the third quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $5$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/2e/2ec6f01ab00c17d4888dfefb37c2813139af9c412e99acd1a38680274b0f0ddd.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6129b724-10d8-519a-bca7-8de0c1d1de2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254539} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1564d2-4049-5952-8cf8-8721ca5faa56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254638} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ffff94-c256-5876-8539-a3579cf454f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255248} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f7cffd-dd73-5f19-80fc-5850b9853698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255566} {:answer/id #uuid "d63c0a22-8c8a-5a64-9594-c373865a8541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255567} :answer-field/id #uuid "142e2698-bc6f-500d-abdf-875dc2caffce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c3d31cf-d2c4-5bcc-be8e-1dfd53a52afe" :question/math-academy-id "q-122580" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=y+1$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t & =5[-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5[(-5+5)+(2\\pi -0)] \\\\  & =10\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d06ecf-0b51-5d9a-89dd-b5cca2cb14d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253743} {:answer/id #uuid "37c25feb-e5d2-53a7-9998-df6ed4eba696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254152} {:answer/id #uuid "68de523d-243e-5336-b731-2670a4aeca58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254616} {:answer/id #uuid "9b001d57-ca13-5e7d-ada3-71e4c50cd037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255036} {:answer/id #uuid "f9773ef7-0698-5c97-8997-d1557e98dc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254152} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ba78323-749e-5156-9b8b-c6548d988531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa3483b0-637e-515f-b376-9929022d28f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122585" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086fb221-1aca-5cef-acb6-fa9c3c393c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253731} {:answer/id #uuid "93c55021-efb7-5747-8fda-774f5bfc2278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254969} {:answer/id #uuid "9c11729d-6dda-5428-bc33-5d1fd201a879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255044} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f893a0-de42-547d-b6a9-7658ef5c91a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255234} {:answer/id #uuid "e878d6b1-a6a3-5da2-84fa-8bcd9bdff3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254969} :answer-field/id #uuid "a040da3b-bc69-5fb6-a488-4b9511cb7f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8375f957-b8d1-554f-bda6-bf390e58a77f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122586" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/d0/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4-4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d61f17-a73a-559a-b50b-18f6f240d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253688} {:answer/id #uuid "110f990c-69a9-5c16-bf2a-0df9b2de43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253817} {:answer/id #uuid "b0054a2d-40ef-507a-8575-0ddc0dd0fbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255218} {:answer/id #uuid "b93ab612-f57a-586f-81a2-541b766d962a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255317} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc77689-ca6e-57f4-b470-6565e808667f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255218} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c81516-a50d-5012-8200-e332f2f9ae1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "882408e8-c52c-51ae-91bc-6f3258db0d8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122594" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x-3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-3 \\\\  & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =9[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9[2(0-0)-(2\\pi -0)] \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499dac47-8436-5953-8ee0-05cfcfef1cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254302} {:answer/id #uuid "96cce4a7-f0e8-5694-8100-43eebcab4066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254996} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd0ab4d-f773-5ebb-b399-99f5db30cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255057} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa1acb7-4f14-5098-9c0d-77b5d6c62c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255076} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4d1ac9-93cb-598e-8067-b8360dcce9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254996} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdfe6782-8ca7-592a-8bf5-c0e936a345c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ba16060-8bcf-52b0-a3d3-d301abe872e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-12279" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f24ec004411065e89b68793e32c80231c7eff6f9ff31e46bc530f78c9c270064.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 4=14.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=5\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-4)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,7\\,|\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =98.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200960c9-a2fa-5dea-ad5e-8bab9dfcdf9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253948} {:answer/id #uuid "3f566b9f-4308-5720-9f6b-67f4c1cb0208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254216} {:answer/id #uuid "51bae210-66e3-5301-b73f-421bea3f6878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254378} {:answer/id #uuid "9d542e19-f81a-5b33-988c-44407117fe1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255053} {:answer/id #uuid "a432fa1f-2fe8-575c-ba4e-419779e2ca83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255115} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea64167e-f9cd-57c8-b1a8-32595d297c34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f758fdb-93df-5eb6-a710-8c50d46f4c4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12281" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9e210f0bfd9ada2dd147068fe0cf06fa85ad049f79bb7e844931b6037867f97.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 4=8.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=5\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d9642a-0618-5f33-a26c-24cdf692b697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255009} {:answer/id #uuid "b42d4173-8afe-5b6a-b489-4e81a34b73b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255257} {:answer/id #uuid "bae5276e-cc00-52de-b024-969838198b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255334} {:answer/id #uuid "f81b4572-15ee-5c0c-852e-721d793902df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255841} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf84acc-a444-5fd0-8351-295a21dfb5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255334} :answer-field/id #uuid "c34fbcdc-57e4-5c27-be08-ce6c1c8f87db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb32c4f8-cf60-5cb1-af2c-6de567006d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-12282" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa0dbfe03050a0ca52f2fbe1988a9e6475cd24437c619dba27a4fed984736fc1.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 6=9.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,8\\,|\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c65048-1bd8-5574-a9e0-ec600f77776e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254007} {:answer/id #uuid "2943610c-b458-56d4-bb3f-f27b602d8f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254039} {:answer/id #uuid "7222c014-590a-580d-bcd2-5f89ee56ea92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254681} {:answer/id #uuid "7702c729-8b14-568b-ab41-781a2678ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254723} {:answer/id #uuid "a9281d40-c16f-5476-80ec-f24aab8ca235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254007} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdbd67e-cb8a-553c-8fd4-1077676fab3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb707a49-2312-560f-8806-ec259a1b8ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-12283" :question/problem "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/3c/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=4\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,10\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ce2d39-71bb-5535-8d23-e5a27a44d98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{3}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253959} {:answer/id #uuid "6140fc0a-1a42-5e54-be95-1c6d206972a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sec}^{2}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254541} {:answer/id #uuid "64c78482-075c-5ee2-8c43-f4f574be0653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{sec}^{3}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254575} {:answer/id #uuid "ad62f5fc-36a4-562d-a32e-1dbc1ff9a104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255198} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb28cca-8601-52ae-b437-84e2bdae33a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254575} :answer-field/id #uuid "e21aeaed-71fa-5a3c-9412-3122a28f69c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cdbe76ee-c0ab-5eaa-a5a5-f6fd4bc3bd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-122884" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+7.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)}=-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5t))}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10{sec}^{2}⁡(5t)}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "588836e4-5eb6-541f-8c8b-b6376ce409bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254447} {:answer/id #uuid "a67f757f-f076-5974-98f9-9c295d1adbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255137} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fe875b-a80a-59e4-bcdb-ab5d745e6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255409} {:answer/id #uuid "d1028432-c454-5405-bd1d-355788a24a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255534} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b6eea0-760d-5988-ad2c-b73df215fded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255409} :answer-field/id #uuid "075ced90-6313-50ba-a2ba-09025bda52b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9232c011-690c-5d8a-aa34-6b7d7e62803f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122888" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-10,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+15.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t))}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d3df3ec-1125-5bbf-833e-06b9811c8a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253915} {:answer/id #uuid "35c849b3-ba48-538b-8146-b45958d48376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$  (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254133} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac2ec77-86f7-5765-9203-29f4055c3628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254761} {:answer/id #uuid "81d69d11-e154-5a32-9a10-b7833ac1b59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254820} {:answer/id #uuid "de0f0cb6-4a3c-55d5-aaa6-e2ad4d3562e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253915} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec03a8c-c264-5202-8472-a929a797fcef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0adf7a1-cd23-5667-b787-5b9256cecb86" :question/math-academy-id "q-12296" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $S-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nS-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(S-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ -32+8\\lambda -4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 8}{2} \\\\  & =-6,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd876e1-c9b9-5b87-a75f-a3d363969ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+48}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253763} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1cd2c8-52c9-5ab6-b00e-67eb570c1c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253891} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0b295e-1b78-58bc-b55b-950e2c86ae9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253937} {:answer/id #uuid "5c23a348-1bab-5cce-89e2-80b0d0a93c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254486} {:answer/id #uuid "8203150f-d07d-5127-8583-74d01adb1f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+48}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253891} :answer-field/id #uuid "63d38714-2af8-58af-a8bb-76be4f32ead0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0fec02e0-b697-5c02-8381-19cf8f50796e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123145" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}3y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $49{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $7,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+49{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=21\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }21\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b57976-d6f2-5ccd-8420-30d2522d5e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254162} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2d0b5d-a4bd-5465-9a2a-d689190b0998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254200} {:answer/id #uuid "a357bfd1-8f00-5536-8fe9-40ccc92277e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255110} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e2de13-fb87-55d1-ac6d-d1c9c0e07301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255326} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f664b9-01ab-5775-bd17-2feae4507ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254200} :answer-field/id #uuid "110bb947-9aba-539f-b588-efdeeb45a55e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba96e982-97fe-5a69-b758-ecab420f15cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123150" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $8{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2\\sqrt{2},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡t+8{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0beba301-c733-5e10-8e07-4b8152f4d846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253764} {:answer/id #uuid "503c3200-1df8-5aa5-99b4-7a35099ec078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254368} {:answer/id #uuid "6facd5e1-8104-5a50-b043-3d4fb6c15076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+7}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254660} {:answer/id #uuid "94d1e08e-e72d-542d-9b55-eff9e5a436c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254980} {:answer/id #uuid "9795d897-70f0-59db-93d8-00436b3ac7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254368} :answer-field/id #uuid "6505a891-b230-5cca-92f9-e0605dad6291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "225dcca1-72c4-569d-848c-f9b3a115bc27" :question/math-academy-id "q-123154" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-8{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}=8,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t-8{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =8({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-8{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "00f83140-8d60-5e3b-8557-38a73fea7b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256790} {:answer/id #uuid "01825306-ac24-5300-973f-a0c4b791365b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256796} {:answer/id #uuid "02791ce3-0e59-59cb-a7c2-9c1f5f9b8efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256804} {:answer/id #uuid "0343f371-7601-54b1-8398-d5ab0afc3056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256808} {:answer/id #uuid "05f4f537-9c38-5a4a-92d9-1fc05c60f90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256834} {:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0f377a60-29ab-5311-9b73-3ae7081133c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta +3)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256927} {:answer/id #uuid "1299b40e-048f-5482-a01f-e8968646b834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{2n+1}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17