ue "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267858} {:answer/id #uuid "c412de53-dcb8-561f-b75c-83b12fa53e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267896} :answer-field/id #uuid "6edf94d4-6780-5605-a47c-83b77d601c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9cb5aaff-6467-5367-a002-45fca048bd67" :question/math-academy-id "q-13464" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1076296e-0d39-54aa-9d61-983621a47ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266283} {:answer/id #uuid "61fa310c-32e5-5949-8701-02963a28655b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267008} {:answer/id #uuid "9977cede-35b5-52b8-adf4-6c1a2da125cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267530} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e6ddb6-6378-5269-ba00-9990281db1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267892} {:answer/id #uuid "c93b3fc8-2b43-547e-b2bf-8e821ba7f18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267949} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0413551-8e99-57e8-bb7f-e5638a7ca7b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5999fc8e-7257-5b70-9229-12117f86cb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135529" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-1(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-6\\pm \\sqrt{(6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 13}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da76e31-175a-5286-8fd4-289cd1f97760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266265} {:answer/id #uuid "11f7b69f-6687-5032-9df4-3edb457b07c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266293} {:answer/id #uuid "19df29fb-9a7f-5158-96dc-263f0f5b9675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266357} {:answer/id #uuid "2af3b11f-beef-5eaa-81fa-1d192c81b682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266520} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3e5dd9-6830-5b8f-bed3-1b4efd1cbd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266951} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1fbf41-3329-5a41-b300-0f02dc01bdbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62cbd463-845c-5e66-8284-1a8dd6e26538" :question/math-academy-id "q-135530" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )^{2}-4(-9)) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-144}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 12\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6acb102e-8d6a-525e-9de8-55099a7f7d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267089} {:answer/id #uuid "757069fa-428b-54a2-99ae-d7dcae304b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267180} {:answer/id #uuid "8695605f-a9ca-55d8-84ca-8940d7835bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267334} {:answer/id #uuid "d64920fb-dd45-59ec-a37a-7a0a3ddef957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268053} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a9cfca-d318-540a-a959-31b72182fde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267180} :answer-field/id #uuid "658bc83e-904d-5c3a-aa95-50294fca2ea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db28644e-74d8-503f-b9d3-0480c9df4e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-135532" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )^{2}-9(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=6.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 10}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 6\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09453baa-a289-511e-a896-b13f03b5beee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266229} {:answer/id #uuid "40690e47-09af-5da7-942a-0e966c1349a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266726} {:answer/id #uuid "41bc83b7-59e2-585c-8677-a8b317305531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266734} {:answer/id #uuid "4bee7e6b-a0a9-5582-8ecc-c18d1040efbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266817} {:answer/id #uuid "e5612e12-7588-53fd-91ca-dc91e75ad2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268171} :answer-field/id #uuid "e888e966-398c-5a9f-a88c-d34cff679809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "347adf6b-9095-5d7e-affc-8139f406fdab" :question/math-academy-id "q-135533" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -10 & 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-2(-10)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +52) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-3.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 52}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8c81ff-4fba-5662-9d08-3e487f024917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266814} {:answer/id #uuid "670db349-289c-59c6-92ff-3b70af48ecc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267059} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed5f3c0-e1f4-5079-9eb6-011ae7b51c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267576} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cd01a2-51bc-5011-b65e-f6c8137a55cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268010} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ee0fa6-ec3c-5515-be0f-532aac7019ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267576} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b631103-d455-5323-9f2f-94279388a60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24acf1d5-f36a-5ae6-986a-efa25c9f1966" :question/math-academy-id "q-135534" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-5(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +29) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 29}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2bb761-228f-5f80-bf79-35f3d1041925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266257} {:answer/id #uuid "481e6dde-7d1a-5d78-a456-ad0587ac7f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266787} {:answer/id #uuid "797076b8-a12f-58b4-b185-deebef2ea5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267217} {:answer/id #uuid "808abad3-8cfb-5cef-9ba9-9528bd396002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267275} {:answer/id #uuid "a07667fe-d0f2-58cc-9174-0cc9d29da7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266257} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b0f223-3ed8-5328-a462-a0050169775e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25442a2f-79df-59e1-a659-8bc32badc551" :question/math-academy-id "q-136559" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=\\frac{1}{4},$ then $4cot⁡(-x+4\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x+4\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 4) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4cot⁡(-x+4\\pi )+1 & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+1 \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141a3561-8082-5952-8157-48e8492b89ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266317} {:answer/id #uuid "236b1937-c70c-5d80-bd20-4a0deacafb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266469} {:answer/id #uuid "5723196c-1a9f-5821-bcc0-4995e363efda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266919} {:answer/id #uuid "57b4c869-f488-5ea7-8101-96dd19922d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266926} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff0c2c2-d85e-5c31-bca9-09d26e8c98d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266469} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb95d12f-f206-531d-98c8-72185e45a0cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2fe46fba-1d41-5471-9453-bf3b903ae99c" :question/math-academy-id "q-136588" :question/problem "If $cot⁡a=-9,$ then $cot⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-a) & =-cot⁡a \\\\  & =-(-9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab4e5a5-d01d-5b94-b66f-40ffd554bb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266516} {:answer/id #uuid "532266d1-d0e1-5115-a8c4-85a96b74cb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266888} {:answer/id #uuid "8c86ad21-1640-5f9f-9b73-bb8b2abc9e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267399} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a38dad-8f50-5347-874b-d8a86b45b729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268139} {:answer/id #uuid "e54e6588-ec99-5e96-ad56-fe5d65f7143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268169} :answer-field/id #uuid "c001196a-3808-5fd0-8d66-d8b745c20636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "06c3c5c0-f8f8-5cfc-a337-ba456adc0f37" :question/math-academy-id "q-136623" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=-2,$ then $5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+12\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 6) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1 & =5\\cdot (-2)+1 \\\\  & =-10+1 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140ba07f-f580-5c24-931a-2c9ada0600f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266316} {:answer/id #uuid "453a992c-8d96-505a-af25-1ee40b13ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266762} {:answer/id #uuid "635a8f50-8a72-5509-b60a-7857cf6c0a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267016} {:answer/id #uuid "692cb440-51f9-5635-a3c7-fd9a52547187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267078} {:answer/id #uuid "a00338ce-0831-57f5-a2de-008f7a2dad0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267588} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3acaf6-b914-543e-8a3f-378f6fc83e69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22d66223-2ab5-5bd5-a6f4-cbd5d5a40689" :question/math-academy-id "q-136625" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=-10,$ then $sec⁡(-x+4\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+4\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sec⁡(-x+4\\pi )=-10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ba9a98-d418-5054-94e5-129c6b76bfac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266186} {:answer/id #uuid "19edb75b-03b9-59e8-bc8e-e9f768d59cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266360} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2ed2c4-f432-5dc5-84ad-45be5e6c0e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266975} {:answer/id #uuid "5e32262d-72d6-5e5a-894e-153eb250705f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266977} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b9b848-8717-52db-b148-c872c7061d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266186} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7bd887-2e1e-5f25-8630-dbf79ed567a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8cc1f5d-680d-525d-9686-5ade22a43012" :question/math-academy-id "q-136627" :question/problem "If $sec⁡a=-12,$ then $sec⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-a) & =sec⁡a \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a49b518-1242-5538-88d2-11c15ad409a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266511} {:answer/id #uuid "47bd735c-d78a-5fa1-af23-606d585fec22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266785} {:answer/id #uuid "d7848761-d53a-5319-b2fc-3092efadcb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268060} {:answer/id #uuid "f7519539-e4d9-5d01-ab39-32fa53d98166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268336} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6c5ae7-fa41-560e-9dd3-471731162c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{91}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268336} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efb648d-3ab0-5adb-a826-86d01fca5115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dcab682-c7ea-5000-bb2e-9cbc57f39eb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-136629" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10},$ then $cos⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519b8fb0-5928-5ec9-8613-12c9d1306f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266873} {:answer/id #uuid "60890db1-c6f4-5143-9b9a-ae422da31c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266994} {:answer/id #uuid "87157abc-6212-562b-a343-90c57626ecf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267338} {:answer/id #uuid "8b99c65a-2714-56e5-af5f-38d5a8ea0940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267396} {:answer/id #uuid "919ec147-de01-530b-97cb-da754894b913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267396} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c52e3dd-dd13-5927-892c-f499bebd302f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "996adb7b-cb39-5733-9d5d-ec3bcfe1a670" :question/math-academy-id "q-13663" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}2{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /9$ | $\\pi /6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}2{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038a1bc6-e647-59b6-aaa7-d35e90993bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266176} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7c73d5-90cc-5170-965c-525cdbc8308d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266399} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3b8949-b671-5cf2-bbef-476092a89eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266656} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb40afd-ce8b-5929-b1b9-111a96d60fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267848} {:answer/id #uuid "f750f91c-c511-5f89-913b-c6b563802807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268335} :answer-field/id #uuid "e028f60d-3a78-5cef-a248-395baca88d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2a3460c-5045-59bc-aae3-13b2bea30fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-136631" :question/problem "If $sec⁡a=-8,$ then $sec⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-a) & =sec⁡a \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378e1e66-9bf4-50e3-a7c1-262bf4e92781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266624} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef10c28-e626-5937-ba80-feb997f872b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266707} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3e15ed-e7b6-5ea7-b9ed-17bf737d8274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266811} {:answer/id #uuid "c3857dc6-9f35-5def-8ee2-c64c55acc069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267889} {:answer/id #uuid "d30ef100-9315-5778-b153-4a1a0a22e9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266811} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a81cb7-756d-567d-b8be-538028bb5ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5606ff1-4177-5d4b-892b-30b0ba862cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-136639" :question/problem "If $tan⁡a=\\frac{4}{5},$ then $tan⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1254fcec-98e0-5f67-a76b-6f8a8e44308a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266302} {:answer/id #uuid "2df7ae72-0cf7-5370-8c96-6a976c483816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266551} {:answer/id #uuid "94c0889d-4b4f-5d2e-afac-f8d8f354ffd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267474} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4b89d4-801b-51a5-9299-c541da6954c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267708} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9364d1-6361-5047-8594-cb93beae9749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87b8c31-bac0-55a7-9f5f-8efffda69a26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8d94a83-3a6d-514a-949b-8d2d5e661234" :question/math-academy-id "q-13803" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e0125b-e672-5de3-b0bf-e83001144caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266592} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2fae57-ad45-507d-8c5c-954002d5296a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267373} {:answer/id #uuid "b99e95e1-68cc-501b-8753-06189a9b5c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267808} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4eaaa5-fa79-518f-aaf9-393c8ce56e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268099} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b5064a-a188-58a9-a5cc-edc293399ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266592} :answer-field/id #uuid "709f832b-380c-5dc3-9199-423e6cfbc1c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8822d1a2-73e9-5933-adc5-14944ec98673" :question/math-academy-id "q-138066" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b2b2d4-4a92-5ca7-8773-585dc47545e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266158} {:answer/id #uuid "1579bd24-79ab-5ae5-855a-9e01a8e981c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266325} {:answer/id #uuid "61936403-30d9-53a2-b50b-9d5bdd921634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267005} {:answer/id #uuid "7da1a3b0-3738-55d7-b984-bee73e145107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267254} {:answer/id #uuid "d74a7220-a06c-5935-bafe-a64c4952b8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267005} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb5642c-7627-5dca-a62a-292e78327201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0245ff8a-62ef-503c-845b-a63c21512dda" :question/math-academy-id "q-138067" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -21 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +7) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=-7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233cbc8c-e991-527f-9870-8237e9259827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266464} {:answer/id #uuid "56e3fb50-941c-5071-8184-b8b5efbbc951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266917} {:answer/id #uuid "75092e81-eb62-517d-a2e7-5d3e74d2d302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267174} {:answer/id #uuid "86732239-f564-5d20-a6f0-287cc9127ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267329} {:answer/id #uuid "c2215b72-50a3-58e0-b851-56494e72a6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559be84-8136-5eb8-a035-4f4b66f98088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87883ff5-3c1f-5958-9ae9-a0cb7b8aff97" :question/math-academy-id "q-138068" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +21 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -7) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028b5d24-5343-5712-ab0f-d2940e077210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266165} {:answer/id #uuid "2296eb71-af04-5c41-943c-a5d4e6ebc126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{2}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266454} {:answer/id #uuid "238496c5-76bc-51bf-9369-36c9de851713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266471} {:answer/id #uuid "47641199-4887-5f5e-986c-9437b93c2232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266781} {:answer/id #uuid "92224e92-8249-52e3-bd3f-d168ab26bf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266471} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bcc82a-73ac-56e2-ab50-09b7c5de2e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6641fae0-7624-5b24-b01b-edaaed427bd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-13807" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{2}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}\\cdot  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ff6694-ed52-501e-9c74-482fc606115b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266189} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac22209-68a0-5ff5-87ab-24d903edf82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266807} {:answer/id #uuid "69d1b0b5-a3c7-5bd0-b0ea-07afbce368e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267082} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca167eb-75e4-5231-9e14-28b84b7d28c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267401} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb91b01-0006-51fd-a05f-946591dc8083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267402}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266189} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b848da-440e-5f9d-8e85-bf37dd01b592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6c7fbad-0cbf-556a-b0c9-e63c761b749b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138070" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122eee3a-20b1-5dc1-8032-b0a7364b8fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266297} {:answer/id #uuid "5b986832-b913-5e58-b854-ef43db21ccb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266955} {:answer/id #uuid "731a0fbb-6cd3-54a2-a7ff-666306420f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267155} {:answer/id #uuid "89017047-09bb-592f-a23d-58f513967ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267364} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b6d881-d2c4-5ba8-8b1d-c3579ea3869d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266955} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f57b6d-dac7-57b3-94d1-767059918990" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e90ca9ef-a872-5eac-99c1-91a2d454f0ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-138074" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc61afe-1f09-5c43-9bcf-9f7a93b45d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266254} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfe0c04-fca0-5511-91ac-85b8a6eb9a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266267} {:answer/id #uuid "241dfe50-c27b-570b-a643-a7ca66632117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266473} {:answer/id #uuid "907366c7-6c93-5a0f-b134-cc843d610cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267430} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5c9ea6-44b8-585f-a1dc-edfd28c5628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268395} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8e03a6-145a-59ae-adee-a94e649f904e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7affa7e5-177d-56c4-b158-cb7cb8121dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-138075" :question/problem "Find the  maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62a6d5f1-07de-5c5d-8398-135e03352f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{20}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267011} {:answer/id #uuid "77019ed7-47fe-5d52-982c-ad34ce8562bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267188} {:answer/id #uuid "85f9f46b-dc8c-592b-a59f-a5b84b64441a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267325} {:answer/id #uuid "a50456f6-7cbc-5cc0-9fb5-0e45d0022ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267631} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0828c2-83a4-5bb3-becb-3137f80bc0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267631} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ab36119-acc0-5be9-8df1-ce5162907a11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6aad317f-209e-580e-8101-2d395c89970f" :question/math-academy-id "q-138086" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1))x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05babc80-2859-5f1a-8b0c-87dde6cecf89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266195} {:answer/id #uuid "7521208c-37a3-5d02-8b01-1a8e8c876524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267176} {:answer/id #uuid "b2995bfc-506c-5873-9d39-f1d95af9a6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267741} {:answer/id #uuid "bda5621b-64a9-5503-9ef9-acb5fd561b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{ln}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267846} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ffa9d6-f461-53f6-b21d-35a722cd8f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267176} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d2b40df-82dd-5065-96cb-1179e4734ac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9dd71bce-d500-5352-b13b-ce18dffed7d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-13809" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228b048a-6de3-56b6-a3e7-0871c1de3843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266453} {:answer/id #uuid "22995146-b5cd-5ac6-8749-c7773c7a4883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266456} {:answer/id #uuid "722a44b6-ca00-50fc-94b1-d4f12a5f8cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267145} {:answer/id #uuid "a8194835-83cc-5f13-b03f-53bc498ecbc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267666} {:answer/id #uuid "ce67314a-1abb-56f6-b237-a46f04d3d378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267145} :answer-field/id #uuid "8681df6c-6663-5b99-800e-3e89e88d4c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc5eba78-8576-573c-99ab-7374b3ba103b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138132" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122c08d1-d87e-5c2d-b5fd-55849221e198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266296} {:answer/id #uuid "b0df363d-24c0-57cb-8740-440c49dc95b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267737} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf414aa-2630-5475-a5c0-66b83d890140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267850} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cf4c06-bbf0-5fcb-8f39-d44a90645423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268155} {:answer/id #uuid "f265a1cd-cc5d-5b68-ae41-46b1bda457d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267850} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f75a84-b0fb-513c-94dd-af85fccbabb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03bfb990-1f26-5661-91b6-3c45e83b54ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-138881" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c7ced12-adf7-50f3-8fa2-2ec760ae813e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t{cosh}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266386} {:answer/id #uuid "3703b92c-5129-5033-ab98-50cdcbc36343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t\\sqrt{1+4{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266619} {:answer/id #uuid "6f246751-a997-50e1-bf3a-7397694c7bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267119} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b60e5b-029c-5dc5-aa8c-ac5e6717b17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{sinh}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{1+8{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267758} {:answer/id #uuid "b515549f-a4fa-5988-a574-c6ea0c940d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267119} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef12ad6f-e35f-52af-bb0c-cb7d265a65e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46772b4c-c81a-56f5-89f5-3541c46cf9e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139027" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t)=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4{sinh}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4{sinh}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+16{sinh}^{2}⁡t{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t(1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015afdf6-11bd-580a-965b-b8d83ff6fdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266155} {:answer/id #uuid "38db1f3f-5191-5ef9-bfce-3999d249e48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266638} {:answer/id #uuid "8677cb69-793a-5c0c-a1a7-c9610ffe99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{2t}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267330} {:answer/id #uuid "cb37778c-6a0f-57ee-9c23-b9a653728e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267964} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bc3a7f-8221-579c-94cc-1890a895d5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{2t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267964} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80e8748-cd2e-5e95-b128-8e8a1b4af013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "67c8f908-9d83-5f3c-afc0-5d179ff299fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-139058" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={3}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{t})={3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{({3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee47220-a4e2-5159-8d37-677dd4043a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267417} {:answer/id #uuid "9067b7fc-a784-5978-a465-6f6a6dc216b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267429} {:answer/id #uuid "adc5904c-70e0-54de-b29f-39558bf28f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267716} {:answer/id #uuid "c54b2e91-b69e-516f-841a-2f92146c6439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267912} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0b6ea6-3030-5898-b7bd-3d23d852d40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267429} :answer-field/id #uuid "273204cb-906b-5113-89a6-a83acb7746a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3bd931f3-299c-5a93-bcdc-18fadb4baee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139063" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06644b91-4fbe-599a-9474-1ebae7c48b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266199} {:answer/id #uuid "233d620a-e698-50bd-9389-aa9f5b7ca47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266465} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddc55e6-09e8-59cd-8647-eda874503ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266692} {:answer/id #uuid "e18ae79f-f50f-5f4c-a737-f0dd234b95b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268138} {:answer/id #uuid "e42c7b91-6155-5bf2-b915-b086395733d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266692} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40b29e1-b1eb-51b7-b542-712ddb83463b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2faf3390-54b5-574c-8816-982dbf7402ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-13939" :question/problem "![](images/55/555cdb44522fab12ad67b54042ee4e47a085802df65c2b32a512eace16b92dd4.png)\n\nCalculate the area of the parallelogram in the figure." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨1,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-5\\,|=5\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186ee2ac-da11-5516-b903-e3b5e9480103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266346} {:answer/id #uuid "303f5039-136f-57c4-95b6-e45427ec1075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1f355e-f850-51c9-9c2a-fb7bc5bbb357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266684} {:answer/id #uuid "92a8e1d3-8eda-5c2f-9bb2-70ec28082ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267452} {:answer/id #uuid "a686bd68-cab0-5c69-85ea-bad821967fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad50c151-5c0e-566c-ac0d-79838b4014d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cad6730b-4b4a-5d91-acf2-b03ce7ce4d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13944" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a70285258e584708a7296a2a008536dec9c97750b48e5fe0eb2f8cb7761bed1.png)\n\nCalculate the area of the parallelogram in the figure." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-10\\,|=10\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daa79a2c-7393-51fd-aa68-53f7bcbfd36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449532} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7d5d0c-57fa-5f8d-a238-f9a8292d8c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449533} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebff15a-46bb-5e25-b5f7-52cc4f246811" :answer-field/key "${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450720} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1909387-b83c-52db-b6e3-ab497516fded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449534} {:answer/id #uuid "8eebe797-5a30-55f7-8a3a-e1cba2f6d82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449534} :answer-field/id #uuid "79794586-10dc-5ee4-b484-f880ca056d6d" :answer-field/key "${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f5b011-a5ec-5e29-8cff-d8e9fe83ea41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449536} {:answer/id #uuid "158c0776-5611-5095-a30f-0b3db7bfac21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449536} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2987329-85fb-5d6c-a910-7bce43831da5" :answer-field/key "$A$ is negative-definite" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "906865b3-32d2-5d7f-b7fc-d38ff3d60bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-139462" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” and  “$A$ is negative-definite” are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d768a08-43a7-50f3-bc2c-9d0aec2d145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266547} {:answer/id #uuid "49c440bd-ae95-5742-ba82-03be224e0b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266798} {:answer/id #uuid "650a0407-08e2-55ea-afa8-98afe8bbc506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267039} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1ac682-0ef5-54bf-9790-851d28380cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267230} {:answer/id #uuid "dba30746-1bcd-5e32-976d-2a26e890257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268088}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266798} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ea674a-8479-5833-a353-9cc54ecf9166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19e02ca3-a6f7-5909-838c-8df1ebb7ea40" :question/math-academy-id "q-13951" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd02af3-9a0c-5ac1-80bc-1a94c3cda463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267103} {:answer/id #uuid "99857b79-bba8-5e11-8d5b-9f8eca4bdc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267532} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ef5443-4611-587c-adf8-f3e45a7a1b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267750} {:answer/id #uuid "de774392-670c-5a82-82bb-af1b9713659c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268109} {:answer/id #uuid "fdda59bb-ef2c-52d1-a08b-48c083a05eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267103} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ea4afd-09dd-54d7-aa86-07bc86565b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "725abed1-dcc0-5071-8bbf-bf93146b75d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-13953" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,6\\,|=6\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce84160-5ec9-55ee-b547-a4aed4d425a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266542} {:answer/id #uuid "6157f8d3-2b30-5816-8bf6-bd729ac45fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267004} {:answer/id #uuid "c5981032-60de-5c0e-a811-b258f8c8389e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267915} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa553b-bb6e-5889-bd58-956b196cc07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268130} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4501b1-3781-50ce-9d47-87f91ce10ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266542} :answer-field/id #uuid "642908b3-d337-55ac-b499-7256cc0df68c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1cdc9189-fe4f-5385-a547-6d74f5ba4b18" :question/math-academy-id "q-13958" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨0,2⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨3,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-6\\,|=6\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0170b556-ecdf-51e2-9b01-9166ccf27649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269272} {:answer/id #uuid "03364fe5-c4cf-5047-9205-8b8f7865c01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269298} {:answer/id #uuid "10c68d72-483e-5e6f-bc5d-9a17b61d2b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269418} {:answer/id #uuid "10dd7d25-8cfc-52a1-acc2-3277c8eeb2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "det(I-{A}^{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269419} {:answer/id #uuid "18875e14-af74-540a-bcc0-ee4fa098d2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269474} {:answer/id #uuid "1cad2d58-8497-5fbc-a7d0-ba994aa73163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269504} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9e852a-b77d-53c9-8db0-26e5edd78831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269525} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddd3d31-aad7-54e3-bac9-d739185ba62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269681} {:answer/id #uuid "4d148a20-673