7-add8-5891a536ebb4" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(A)=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450786} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af8c890-adc0-5501-b776-d23219000bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449666} {:answer/id #uuid "78ea7642-4a88-5dd4-9c0e-b9cddd5255a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449666} :answer-field/id #uuid "e11f78a0-08ce-50e3-88fc-e1011761342e" :answer-field/key "$A$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "634c8a5c-c46d-507d-a2df-cc1a7726c974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449668} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e28a0-079e-52e9-9123-05a2f2341769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e79aa7-abeb-5d53-9e24-d52f8f63b70a" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5342a760-4f6b-57f7-989c-35eadd9600b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143533" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\right\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{3} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=3=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\right\\}=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $det(A)=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb8c6d5-62f2-5f9e-9dff-a6c6cf210773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272526} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8111fb-6012-56e0-a9b6-3275417d9a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272916} {:answer/id #uuid "8340b495-0e27-5ab2-8265-89b8a8f42f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273514} {:answer/id #uuid "ceceedd5-348a-5f86-9a42-9b4e036292b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274185} {:answer/id #uuid "ed397ac8-10b1-54c0-9046-d661bdad85ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b42dc950-3ab6-53ee-bb79-c0cd1d57e61e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "587d6141-4c7d-5ff0-9ea2-3cc64d30a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-143672" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a90f799d2dfd2dbd971e15eb195f4109ab7b6ceb204943a8ed2fd6c83bb620c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=cot\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/0e/0e94fbec60ae4d47d3a4fba50f8a875d9bb3938776940cc9688413d2cb22aa9e.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a941d2-0004-538c-b3cd-647a6020db00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{43}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272461} {:answer/id #uuid "4023d5d3-1807-53c8-a6c0-65921408fc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272937} {:answer/id #uuid "5225563d-48bb-51d5-a198-0d482b4970d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{77}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273084} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0df09b-c1ec-5de9-a816-049ff257188c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274163} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7c6684-b943-55f7-bd43-f70946609a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273084} :answer-field/id #uuid "7941d771-580d-5eea-a4dc-414c95ace302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a78bd195-69bb-5aa4-8a43-6887179edeb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143698" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{15}{17}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}-\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{77}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a344ed7-da9c-57a0-af2a-71951270f044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272764} {:answer/id #uuid "45172f34-9a52-5299-830e-7d4b21517809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272979} {:answer/id #uuid "56eb90ac-0ae9-554d-b1cf-a0e6ac4b635f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273131} {:answer/id #uuid "8317d80f-7e82-5091-8159-51e2495412d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{37}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273512} {:answer/id #uuid "c08ab04f-2c08-5746-b7ca-17c82640fef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{117}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272979} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f65c0b-9e4b-52d4-9016-5e4d942c0430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1688d8c0-f85a-5a6b-9eed-e20e5ffca899" :question/math-academy-id "q-144398" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{7}{25}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-7)^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{24}{25}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{7}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{125}-\\frac{28}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc039c6-5665-5166-aa53-9a15c6c4c91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272795} {:answer/id #uuid "8b464717-8539-5469-8060-dac626a0833e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273588} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ea6766-0861-5a8d-8643-ea7eabf5bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274253} {:answer/id #uuid "d920ab5e-c0b5-5fd4-a193-1324a5a2e7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274284} {:answer/id #uuid "e41b2b91-8816-5099-b2fa-6f471a4a0905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272795} :answer-field/id #uuid "49db0e6a-b203-52c9-99a3-d2fa536b85d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4554a25e-9962-5d01-9655-91afa5f0b772" :question/math-academy-id "q-149815" :question/problem "![](images/62/624bb949d80c1da50cba951b00210a2a26aeb3c44d77d4c2eed69c8e3ba0f4d4.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67650d05-1bb4-5986-982d-3bfb1387f409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273275} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8d92c4-3e25-5f3f-a530-90d16bb99434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273583} {:answer/id #uuid "b9609ff5-338d-5888-94a6-03ab48c42b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273994} {:answer/id #uuid "c97eeb85-98d2-5ca2-b4bc-6076b4c9402e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274135} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6fdec-beb9-587a-b77d-b332069caf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274135} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda32cdf-7d6e-52a9-a5bc-1d25ba7de216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7434f2a6-72f3-5e31-9dcb-4b1f63dafdbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-149909" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=-\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1022878e-d352-5a40-98af-1bb2ccf2fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272531} {:answer/id #uuid "3292d624-f6e3-5c69-b937-44c88a9d05bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272840} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9c3178-255e-5187-b40a-4f7592f65d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273032} {:answer/id #uuid "abe84d44-fe73-5960-98d9-12a315d6a3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273869} {:answer/id #uuid "e11a327b-4d63-5b24-86f7-0b18599b1b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274371} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b7b1d5-93df-59ca-b71e-4d9fe5feeaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5af8ac95-f846-5704-b546-5b84be0ef466" :question/math-academy-id "q-149913" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197f7f3c-1814-5539-88cc-8d324898ee5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272609} {:answer/id #uuid "2fea7db3-4e7c-5614-ae83-fa65014e2989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272814} {:answer/id #uuid "6bfeaa14-af54-5709-b268-7fb92c5fb931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273318} {:answer/id #uuid "a60c1e22-63e1-5366-8ce8-00afd5a3b3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273824} {:answer/id #uuid "db9385c8-9e1d-581c-b45b-9f71e6e47ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273318} :answer-field/id #uuid "a555f44f-209a-564b-9155-a69db2cccbc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "329ee6a2-6455-51c9-9cfd-81f75012b6f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-150015" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(2-x)$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{1}{x-2}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)-2} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,0)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =(-1)\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f627df1-adde-57f5-90e4-09efa69ff493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272664} {:answer/id #uuid "9adcea7e-7161-588b-9a67-61c17173cfeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273726} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6f3505-29fa-5085-abdb-d93a28a2158d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274166} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa293b5-f3ac-5c8b-a37b-f89a115d333f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274364} {:answer/id #uuid "e71092c7-0d47-53dd-a9ce-c8db47ee94d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274166} :answer-field/id #uuid "be32e887-d36f-5576-8d64-d4f02bbc18f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ac3b5b3-7e20-5bd6-a6af-097e3f624a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-15122" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c54104b-b3dc-5569-a45f-4825e0d27a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272635} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb5aa76-e451-50b2-91fb-baabb9a3700f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273315} {:answer/id #uuid "ae56957b-c439-5d47-a819-1031c5bf0206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273892} {:answer/id #uuid "c676dbe5-5207-5293-af38-9e035d17772c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274109} {:answer/id #uuid "f54e6d65-362a-5898-9764-0fd2e605a7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273892} :answer-field/id #uuid "c075fa06-e768-565d-b8b4-20d1444a6cc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e06591c4-f9ec-5725-a82d-146c8f26267d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15129" :question/problem "![](images/59/59214bcbfd4b1d718faa0c2355e932ab3cedae5b785b17497cef554ee3ec099b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+1+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41612c4c-f870-5ed5-87f8-b382715e8648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272945} {:answer/id #uuid "751d78c4-b19a-5d89-b496-847e248ea026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273388} {:answer/id #uuid "afd5220f-e133-5338-b18e-873ee1f218a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273908} {:answer/id #uuid "b03474d7-bd8f-5753-8615-74c0bc59287d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273913} {:answer/id #uuid "db564748-e979-5e7d-9d93-c9c418e8c841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273913} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba365ec3-d4cf-55b8-8901-8ccc6da95432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "915da383-5cd0-54bd-ae8a-9f0bc95c9cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-15131" :question/problem "![](images/00/00e5e0f5c47a056beaca2d7bc1b1d10a6a11f96398df7400e69c023f5780b8a6.png)\n\nWhich of the following integrals gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$ (shown in blue in the  diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f016b0-2fd0-50db-a608-6fb63624e44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272994} {:answer/id #uuid "69d385f0-e607-521b-a6d5-ff44ee22ff0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273301} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a87c9-2f3a-5822-a579-01d6eb0bbb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273473} {:answer/id #uuid "8df9ee48-5fdc-55b9-9efb-127f8dd6bac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273611} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71957f-f727-5cbc-9851-57e268064b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273473} :answer-field/id #uuid "18bf7715-0000-5c6a-ac8b-11821a5550c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b418c2bf-fd1a-57e4-845f-5c5fb4e1bb93" :question/math-academy-id "q-15163" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\n$$\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=5$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0c82e4-a410-5545-aba6-e6bc3809dd5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272760} {:answer/id #uuid "632f8488-1426-51de-b79c-859a00953830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273240} {:answer/id #uuid "8f09bfeb-fa40-5b8c-a65a-f33bf93f0996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡y=\\sqrt{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273624} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa6a988-751b-5fc3-92dd-5e4dfbf0e530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\sqrt{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273767} {:answer/id #uuid "df59ea5f-fed1-5499-abd7-c6c018da530f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡y=x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274358}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274358} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a01516-b9bc-55d0-ac76-1962bc75b5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2654f6e-f38e-5fe7-bd83-e2905edb3c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-154357" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡y}\n$$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡y} \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡y & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡y=x+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ebf211-8fd3-5d62-86cd-f2533a48899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272399} {:answer/id #uuid "12a69fcb-0dc6-5fa9-bd2a-a2e5a39f64a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272554} {:answer/id #uuid "15f814db-6448-5cda-9229-260b29950688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272581} {:answer/id #uuid "8375590b-cff0-54ea-b0bc-fa6207c8f526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273517} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f2d620-3791-5c3e-ae34-afd6b2851a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272581} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a010213-a60e-59da-898c-ff1887132d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "afe695ae-563f-5bc9-9f85-ce61b12f7c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-15639" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{3}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244e842d-6715-5aeb-b13b-adc8b6cf5d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272706} {:answer/id #uuid "5194416d-ecec-5f21-915f-c325c30b65c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273082} {:answer/id #uuid "75ed1eed-b51b-5097-9cd3-a39f055f5c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273390} {:answer/id #uuid "7e002927-2ce6-5656-8e9d-e32f1ff37552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273462} {:answer/id #uuid "c988e25e-fd28-52c7-b118-2623bf44beb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273462} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9cd3aa-2595-5209-9d8a-dc52f793d875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "afa04175-238c-5ba8-a826-771eab746826" :question/math-academy-id "q-15710" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8f1b2b-173b-5b92-b859-1ec4e15fc869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272505} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0ec060-43c6-5507-a4b4-71bf27bfdb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272798} {:answer/id #uuid "3d24278c-1604-5a0e-92c8-91b968fed491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272912} {:answer/id #uuid "6793780e-4123-58f7-8dac-1bc0e63caa25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273279} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9ffd0c-5022-5c83-b63e-3c3cbf9ed38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272798} :answer-field/id #uuid "30cc30d9-eb2f-5ad6-9d67-21da1b5948c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ccbcc93-cb2d-5c7d-ae5c-a7e728fdad2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15792" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019119ed-0828-5d14-913a-ecb47950b217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272395} {:answer/id #uuid "0b63defe-eba9-54f7-8c7f-b0bba025e804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272491} {:answer/id #uuid "612cc7e3-fab2-5707-b558-c6527bd1837a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273228} {:answer/id #uuid "83e2e646-4e20-564b-867b-ae7e68e0f341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273520} {:answer/id #uuid "a01c641c-59d0-5fde-8400-4b249323e3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272491} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdbfba5c-9780-5693-999b-a9c027c35c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "253f761f-d9dd-516e-a877-3691e4e75b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158013" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}$ for $y\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342f578b-d80d-5790-a353-a30b6906380a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272852} {:answer/id #uuid "4f68c603-8ad9-5107-bdbf-c0638fd5673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273054} {:answer/id #uuid "56a45d67-387d-5bbe-a775-e3b834b28aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273125} {:answer/id #uuid "bfebdf4e-3dbb-5491-aac5-c1dd56989347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274047} {:answer/id #uuid "cffa510c-caa9-52f5-a1e0-3c92d3101954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273125} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d5e25b-e9dd-5b67-a360-a93e26d946ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8cb683f-c83e-5880-b481-a77cc9d224dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158038" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=3,$ then $tan⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108e0db6-e384-55c4-99d1-574ed07b4c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272535} {:answer/id #uuid "2d056441-eef5-508e-8a9a-a14472e5ee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272787} {:answer/id #uuid "3408c295-dfdb-5479-b58d-4c80e71521e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272850} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb4819f-626e-504a-8ed6-1d686dc99901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273327} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b17a8c-3a77-587d-bb57-4517e5e4a287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272535} :answer-field/id #uuid "5337af0e-e303-5162-b9ed-2cc7c66f30f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6710fdcd-157b-570c-95e4-ed16e27fba49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158041" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-4,$ then $\\frac{1}{2}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi )+3=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi )+3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}+3 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18aaf96b-5573-534c-a8e7-fc0d31c5da28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272601} {:answer/id #uuid "57c133bd-eaa9-55d9-bb09-72cf7db7ed66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273140} {:answer/id #uuid "777a0b70-733d-5f72-b574-a701dbcee54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273406} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf83161-ce4a-5041-a5d5-913c94a728a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274338} {:answer/id #uuid "ef670251-f792-57a2-bd14-17a43c2e0766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274504} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5f260d-527b-5fa0-891f-24c0985d4726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b84288a-81b8-54b0-94c6-6bddb05e4bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158044" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{6},$ then $sec⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4513555a-9108-5088-907d-beecce571174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272977} {:answer/id #uuid "87ddb98b-6f18-5542-9e00-38ccd036fdd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273552} {:answer/id #uuid "af5d1927-4709-58ef-bf27-8dace68c851c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273902} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb032b7-f1b2-5154-9e14-aba1200cc20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274477} {:answer/id #uuid "fd56bc52-d5a9-5db6-8188-6c6b48c73474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272977} :answer-field/id #uuid "e257a236-bcb1-507a-a11a-a984805bc856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e6dd4ab2-db9a-5355-9ce0-ec6183a9429b" :question/math-academy-id "q-158046" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-2,$ then $\\frac{3}{5}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+\\frac{4}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+\\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 2+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d52117-fe8d-523e-ab7c-8b69d3b778d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272473} {:answer/id #uuid "4872ed05-3948-52c9-8e10-8c290bcef73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273004} {:answer/id #uuid "4e09fa24-3a43-5607-af82-37743fb17fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273043} {:answer/id #uuid "5f648814-57a1-50af-86b2-a08f76c8a680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273209} {:answer/id #uuid "c3887aae-20b9-5b6d-a82b-0513e7162821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272473} :answer-field/id #uuid "667d8cf5-9214-5663-8a40-187a54d0f77e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274810}] :question/difficult