orked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+24\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 12) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1308c252-3f5f-5a02-be87-52593ac89917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272558} {:answer/id #uuid "33cb8823-ce66-5677-84d7-89e4c81c03e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272846} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccbdf46-9ee5-5738-a68a-6a4c7e94d746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273746} {:answer/id #uuid "c35f06e3-925e-57a7-8e44-c7ad3fa649fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274082} {:answer/id #uuid "e42382ea-4217-5fb3-b74a-833c31fad439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274404}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274404} :answer-field/id #uuid "d811a9a8-c409-5b01-851f-64933cb4d3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0221b117-6ad9-5a2f-80bb-79e143dc5ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158055" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $cot⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349c1465-e1c5-5405-831d-aeac214a7a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272855} {:answer/id #uuid "97e38bc7-5092-51a8-b670-a71ac0a2b2fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273705} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ac9ace-856e-53ad-9bd6-30e0fde5df05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7+5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273839} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ccc6ec-5159-593f-8e17-52790462977a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273916} {:answer/id #uuid "ce14a41a-ee20-5b15-8747-e08ada594875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273916} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2e1d44-c66b-5894-8499-d54f711331c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1d482a12-cb86-542a-9d10-1273500fd3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-158058" :question/problem "If $cot⁡a=-7,$ then $cot⁡(-a+5\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-a+5\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-a+\\pi \\cdot 5) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-a) & =-cot⁡a \\\\  & =-(-7) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cot⁡(-a+5\\pi )=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275f4184-6614-5fb5-b945-c0a925615360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272735} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7df9b3-60b6-5ef6-8973-d6465d2aa3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273582} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1e5805-1c6c-5d57-b929-84303eb5f147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273626} {:answer/id #uuid "ae068c2c-1302-562e-8e3f-5924d2a8dd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273888} {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea032dc8-afc8-5574-9882-b42bbb63200d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a595d8eb-425e-5670-9569-3c6cc685c869" :question/math-academy-id "q-158059" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=0,$ then $\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric over the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+18\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pi \\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4110e0-8cbe-509f-8768-e57ad22b3e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272490} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfc4511-a4c7-5555-89b3-9b7095440042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272509} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd51020-1a5c-56db-8c08-63b63d83c079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273636} {:answer/id #uuid "bf721ced-4517-5626-a108-41e175743956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274043} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d362da-9dbc-5d76-bebd-946b5b66ed7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272509} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3c6ee6-6e94-558d-95c0-4e54abe1c6d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2a79aaf7-1dcc-57ab-a0b7-6d93a2b468da" :question/math-academy-id "q-158064" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=-6,$ then $\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 15) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6+7 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}+7 \\\\  & =3+7 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "478400f5-12d3-537c-9f87-fbc0de611e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272998} {:answer/id #uuid "79df43db-fad2-5496-a61c-154bea4bfb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273436} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8588fe-ba26-52d2-96ad-dd3b2374c528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273632} {:answer/id #uuid "a0166d3e-5891-5775-9bb7-edb418a91f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273772} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4bd2a-0dc8-5886-be0f-d0fa3a157987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273436} :answer-field/id #uuid "d322600e-3d33-5ca9-9728-eaa166ff5e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "210e9083-358f-508e-9376-e356564e0e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158104" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|24|=24.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27aa8c33-edfb-5fb2-adfb-8de8ec26ee80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272737} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9e26c9-c473-5f4d-8f65-9bd62434b33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273182} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bc95c5-7da6-5228-b9f8-40a00f91ebed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273968} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7acf39-2806-5c1f-bac9-2f76b89d98e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274027} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81e9da-6f65-5e19-8784-3030b7eb61ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274324} :answer-field/id #uuid "1470b275-7731-546d-9f3e-40a3009a30fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "edcd069d-5898-5a9f-9b4f-3515e782b5c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-158106" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-72.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-72|=72.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8d63a0-e6e8-5621-b14e-0a6afb7468b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272792} {:answer/id #uuid "4e356ba3-0766-580b-b544-969b6db926d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273046} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9af943-2e3a-56aa-a3d4-69f1c9aa6ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273306} {:answer/id #uuid "7b178126-366e-5772-8fae-0734a917447f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273443} {:answer/id #uuid "e3683848-ab39-5f23-91ed-e22f1d4d80fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273306} :answer-field/id #uuid "16f94c49-7116-5254-8071-00edbc36756c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32e6e8c1-9b26-5825-ac5f-18dfd683a674" :question/math-academy-id "q-158108" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=7\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-105.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-105|=105.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c55fd61-b7c5-584d-8bde-77e2feec81f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272636} {:answer/id #uuid "85bb8804-af94-50ef-9fa7-689080c4022c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273537} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7b9016-9a9f-57ae-89d0-4f976fd13493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274167} {:answer/id #uuid "e82989cc-6d22-523a-8fe3-705ed41b63aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274439} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9defb5-0bb6-5fb4-bfe9-64fb5350454a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274439} :answer-field/id #uuid "5580ad36-9518-5ea6-9b9c-bbc10a029ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33a0a4e7-70bf-5d4f-8473-1e3ebaad90ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-158109" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|180|=180.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14dc93bc-a38e-5377-a83e-cdfd8429aad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272575} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfab9dd-4d38-5d0a-8ae4-4bb8f2dfb58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272627} {:answer/id #uuid "51509c36-6629-523a-8205-b2bb3effda20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273079} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeddc74-6832-5dcd-b316-c663fd53aeae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273441} {:answer/id #uuid "7e044c6a-8d91-53c4-961f-fd4db697aee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272627} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc8de92-2ed5-584b-9065-852c4f8079e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4e39b2b-6ddb-522a-a369-dfb16a50311d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158110" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5=-180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-180|=180.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade1b1c2-bb18-5ec5-a08e-f2fe191dfc0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273887} {:answer/id #uuid "d02cdc25-7525-505a-846a-c4d1135329bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274202} {:answer/id #uuid "d235c9d6-d0dd-5309-9d9d-e70481f4d759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274224} {:answer/id #uuid "f06e744b-0c4e-56b4-9ccf-073c04c36996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274515} {:answer/id #uuid "f7eafda1-95e4-50d3-9e5d-c6169be19dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274560}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274202} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95e766c-71b6-5648-a6e3-04cdaca450da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3a8456a-5f67-54bf-8d0a-07a8bbe82bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158114" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{3}} & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fdec51-bb15-58a1-ac35-a4628bdaa895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272966} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc399b8-24ed-566b-807a-98321753ba77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273449} {:answer/id #uuid "c672699b-78d4-5fd4-9230-acc5ec657314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274108} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b57a70-f0e5-5f77-8a9c-b1e730d2744a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274436} {:answer/id #uuid "e9793388-0d77-54db-82ac-a9919ac4db9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272966} :answer-field/id #uuid "80c6b84b-18ea-5a42-80cf-65bc4466c515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d8d55196-4015-578e-afc9-4c54361316e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1583" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the definition of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡A$ to rewrite the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-{tan}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $1-{sin}^{2}⁡A={cos}^{2}⁡A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡A}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e51492-c3b7-54b6-8660-3327203595bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272699} {:answer/id #uuid "28eeb732-2c18-5922-b6a9-bbd2d7eec5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272751} {:answer/id #uuid "3084793d-d7d4-5080-9297-c5dbc7fc686c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272817} {:answer/id #uuid "b981ba03-1c73-5fee-958e-9f516f79590f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273996} {:answer/id #uuid "ed17d628-c951-506b-b286-13e3eb6a5ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272699} :answer-field/id #uuid "56164ca7-3d80-5b0c-bf3e-27409397895a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c594e512-d19a-53c5-8f50-4caaf295111c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158368" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{6}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049a84c1-52fe-5a96-80d0-bdfba1ece812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272428} {:answer/id #uuid "2e002483-bff0-5696-8b17-34e6d65302e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272797} {:answer/id #uuid "650c50e4-830c-5672-a10f-2d86d1f80ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273251} {:answer/id #uuid "686c0b94-d2c7-570f-a49e-0b44f7a5e887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273289} {:answer/id #uuid "f965d840-d861-5dd1-be3a-b2a400272e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274574} :answer-field/id #uuid "6428d2da-cec3-5130-8fdf-f3195ddd3a25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d7bd54b-d293-5ee2-b790-9af4e0cb0ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-158369" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb8b630-1858-5675-a8ea-afd745123540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272641} {:answer/id #uuid "25136c9e-920f-566e-a408-ea4a7af1ef37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272712} {:answer/id #uuid "28f41079-6423-5124-b7dd-2b748bc5ac50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272753} {:answer/id #uuid "37c41e9d-c90b-5532-80b8-70fb57bf191a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272875} {:answer/id #uuid "39e96dc4-16ab-55e3-b742-85d0597e033b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3f55ee-40e6-566b-9375-89a8f6437390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "94fb05dd-7b6c-5fef-becd-0abc3ccf0dc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158371" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289e4e70-c759-5e50-89d8-b925f0235620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272745} {:answer/id #uuid "3625ba2b-ce13-5cb3-b6ab-d5fa443ed6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272865} {:answer/id #uuid "607ff479-be80-5283-965c-9fc367a3aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273221} {:answer/id #uuid "ada1be8e-4913-5803-90a3-85876ba9e172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273884} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3b42fe-9570-5764-bcd8-983b4bbbe01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9d8eb2-a7a3-5dc7-958b-4f150f94c065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "75e7ea31-6bb1-5da3-8967-5b9dbf480524" :question/math-academy-id "q-1584" :question/problem "$\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡t={sin}^{2}⁡t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{-1\\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the denominator using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡t}}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡t}} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t}=-1\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4003cf-623f-50ad-a594-99b52136077d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275594} {:answer/id #uuid "22090efc-b5f3-5f43-ac36-b7fe4c4588db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275783} {:answer/id #uuid "2809705b-8db8-563c-a442-e6f1acbbfe7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275842} {:answer/id #uuid "545a0196-2f20-52f1-bf5a-4cf4121796a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276225} {:answer/id #uuid "58f7ed97-5375-5cb9-940f-d7302ec3d531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276278} {:answer/id #uuid "5b74d1d6-b038-5bd8-b06c-ee2a3dd3ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276296} {:answer/id #uuid "7056a72c-612b-5f7f-841e-1d1c109d83f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276489} {:answer/id #uuid "71c1c996-c586-5df6-8332-85e7a81d2723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276505} {:answer/id #uuid "75e654f0-1e0f-53da-a9cf-fc82e17a3138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276542} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8c508f-4a2d-5ecc-be82-514c6f516af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276615} {:answer/id #uuid "a13f06d6-3684-5189-9263-82c1a846137d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡z+\\pi -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276930} {:answer/id #uuid "b9250b04-8df7-5d4c-af3c-374371efb26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119} {:answer/id #uuid "f13dcfdf-9280-5fd0-9cca-4509d35df4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡z-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044aa764-a402-587b-9d4c-b41637dfa030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275516} {:answer/id #uuid "8324a325-41e1-54bc-8b74-1c52b4799e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276657} {:answer/id #uuid "c4127d31-6dc3-521f-8215-809b40a67775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277220} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c6cd3a-dd09-5f85-a646-0e12c483a833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277337} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c3aad5-29e3-5b96-b737-92612b8b8109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b8f849-09df-52e8-a517-f7b406de368f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48febb37-71be-5629-b005-ff375ac1902f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15843" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d79f35b-9c6f-5ea0-bc36-b70cf365f94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276011} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0ddcf9-5550-54e5-b71a-85b4e14d8483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276631} {:answer/id #uuid "93cec608-e557-5ec2-a838-72d1783b34ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276815} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd9f428-0306-5272-8687-ba40f9700e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276894} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc78b51-a3ef-5a56-85ea-de62572a4123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277297} :answer-field/id #uuid "227bfbb7-c8a5-52a9-9c16-7187fd6462d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e339dfa-5444-5946-8701-7661eae41839" :question/math-academy-id "q-159757" :question/problem "If $z=-9-\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-9-\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=-9,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(-9) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-9-\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-9+\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(-9-\\text{i})+(-9+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-9+(-9))+\\text{i}(-1+1) \\\\  & =-18+\\text{i}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a9f99e-0bb6-5785-9503-3098c54ef996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275947} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd337b2-240e-502d-a899-8d8003dca03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276165} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8c508f-4a2d-5ecc-be82-514c6f516af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276615} {:answer/id #uuid "96898644-dfd7-5a2f-9c62-29bb6eae00db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276830} {:answer/id #uuid "f0afcf90-2bb9-5832-b516-c6b381642909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276830} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a2d6395-ac0c-53bf-a1da-247a9e5b5c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bd35fbf-98e6-549b-868f-29cc78eccc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161051" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y),\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4003cf-623f-50ad-a594-99b52136077d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275594} {:answer/id #uuid "58f7ed97-5375-5cb9-940f-d7302ec3d531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276278} {:answer/id #uuid "5b74d1d6-b038-5bd8-b06c-ee2a3dd3ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276296} {:answer/id #uuid "75e654f0-1e0f-53da-a9cf-fc82e17a3138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276542} {:answer/id #uuid "77402893-a60d-5f84-88a8-fd3309fcd100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276556}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275594} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd0831a-89e3-5cff-b445-fee1bf8d9c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c1ab1d22-20a5-53e7-b1d4-b12bfa53d601" :question/math-academy-id "q-161061" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi /4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f293826-0c6c-5cdd-896c-4a8c0a7187cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275760} {:answer/id #uuid "2d60278c-5b07-5880-86ac-1ded5d7dc119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275886} {:answer/id #uuid "48b79190-1595-5f38-bc0f-dfd059c396b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276123} {:answer/id #uuid "5464e3d5-8862-5c94-b087-36c29598aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276227} {:answer/id #uuid "e709f368-118d-58f3-91f5-8518d0e15cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277494} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e08377-634f-5706-a6c5-0cb5547e9e6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "121067f9-6a02-54b7-8d8d-5663e9e8648d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161063" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ and    \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince $f={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2z{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037c2ae5-735b-5d61-8c5e-6bbda3d980ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275509} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecb851a-dcc4-594e-adee-ea8b3568d2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276179} {:answer/id #uuid "6131e7e9-fdcd-5f94-9e3f-6bb354dba509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276353} {:answer/id #uuid "baef686b-ae73-5fa6-a824-007061121272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277135} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8f9d0-7b73-5fda-8905-2e2351bd542f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275509} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2ca0bad-7398-527f-a25c-7bae8e910ed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e2af22f9-f266-54cc-bd8b-aaed5dc9194d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161073" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\text{_______}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [1,4]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [1,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f9bb02b-ba61-53d2-a85a-916f0c607652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276485} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab54011-28c1-51d9-be48-677bd5c8ec09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276734} {:answer/id #uuid "903ee0ec-a332-5b9a-84b7-3cd391216474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(x-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276782} {:answer/id #uuid "d43557fa-9d0d-52d9-93b6-3ca8dd22e26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277346} {:answer/id #uuid "e5785c11-3215-5baa-96fe-e88a3c94d023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b043bc41-1c81-5ac0-a33e-21fbd7d487d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26bf1e92-1d3b-51d5-9a0f-70ddbedf5cdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161076" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=(x-2)^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,2]$ is given above. What is the $1$st  component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=(x-2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=(x-2)^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2(x-2).$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2(x-2)^{3},\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac160c0-9e31-53a2-8e67-6a875cc69c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275726} {:answer/id #uuid "4321ec57-3aa3-5435-bef9-7196ceb02e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276073} {:answer/id #uuid "432e387d-bcda-51fe-936c-0efd0034da26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276074} {:answer/id #uuid "50b08d4f-b9f3-5da2-9255-56d9e80228dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276197} {:answer/id #uuid "767527c1-5b06-5f0f-af49-a862e51ad660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276197} :answer-field/id #uuid "977aba5e-30b2-5f02-a704-7229f7441b6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbbd01ee-4c13-59a2-bf89-170f124c5003" :question/math-academy-id "q-161093" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4adcb04b-1399-563d-83bd-c37ab7760896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276137} {:answer/id #uuid "8a69e950-36a6-52f0-a36f-0c30e9405e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276730} {:answer/id #uuid "a113e5ec-2575-5e37-a0f6-4c881b6ee91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276929} {:answer/id #uuid "b255ec78-7a4b-5375-9272-ae3700481059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277075} {:answer/id #uuid "f4777aeb-2cc7-5270-b236-76130eaf1b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276929} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce9bea7-205a-5663-9d0c-9b1f1c07e730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb050a56-f41b-5b04-8f29-e8dcbba53ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161094" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={e}^{x}(x-1)$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={e}^{x}(x-1)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [2,3].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+(x{e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ea2ba7-71f9-5435-99b5-c516c98e140b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275492} {:answer/id #uuid "3dde53ce-8492-5c3a-b3f8-1c59e0bda728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276012} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8d2bd-1378-5e16-adbc-0267dc563005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{2}\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276126} {:answer/id #uuid "8902e0a6-d051-5e59-9f7a-48d434c42822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276714} {:answer/id #uuid "fb811851-c609-56aa-8a68-cbc02c0fc191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{z}^{3}\\sqrt{1+z\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275492} :answer-field/id #uuid "551ceaa3-c382-57eb-a31a-44c2844bba61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b159f438-a41a-55ac-9a0b-7a41de1c7f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-161097" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=z\\sqrt{z}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=z\\sqrt{z}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1504b562-ea07-5020-baa3-bce4b8c73fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275675} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9511a3-47fe-51ed-b124-e1105e4f0c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275766} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba037df-7fdf-5532-a494-ac03592db52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276747} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e00aa8-6165-5f31-8310-6e1e1a406117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277096} {:answer/id #uuid "f908b8f9-5e49-5941-bd3e-e05f0081a353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275675} :answer-field/id #uuid "11ef57f9-a018-5ee8-a2b1-622897978c22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8a98dcae-fa38-562f-8676-270d1756205a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161099" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006bcbfc-d28a-5f99-aa49-be2cd9602e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275483} {:answer/id #uuid "22090efc-b5f3-5f43-ac36-b7fe4c4588db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275783} {:answer/id #uuid "545a0196-2f20-52f1-bf5a-4cf4121796a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276225} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3366ce-9fb2-57e8-b87c-6c8c24bfc9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276318} {:answer/id #uuid "7056a72c-612b-5f7f-841e-1d1c109d83f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276489} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e5eaa4-5870-549b-aad4-063ad6b17389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "134aff90-1d55-5793-9da0-c59e5f46f5e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161140" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf7a5921936b44a2e672fcee524c5a230800e6130f6c0a8683e6ce46829df58.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f26c9a8-c236-5b49-a03a-a9c4e306b3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276025} {:answer/id #uuid "56116490-cd88-5ee8-914f-70566119e239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4867883-7969-53b3-9ce6-9041f96c5468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276959} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ede688-6fec-5d91-b089-01387832a30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276993} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf48e5d-5a33-563d-899e-f6003b1ae552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276959} :answer-field/id #uuid "85db4747-8718-5aa2-9149-1e2cb6cc044a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "433fa395-a287-58c1-aa07-6bcf4cb6b740" :question/math-academy-id "q-161144" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\hline u & 2 & 1 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd3e50e-24fa-5deb-bbdd-9633a0c613ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275586} {:answer/id #uuid "35403652-42fe-538a-b9fd-83d5e2b92837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275952} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3f8e3d-2fea-5759-8fa1-3622d0408c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12(\\sqrt{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276308} {:answer/id #uuid "689e2b88-2195-53b4-8791-16e01eb464d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24(\\sqrt{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276415} {:answer/id #uuid "8d52904d-fd93-5210-9d97-36e2d89a49aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276415} :answer-field/id #uuid "38951be5-e63a-5491-9887-982734a968e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b297dd92-b10c-5ab9-9d26-be624175b717" :question/math-academy-id "q-161276" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $$\n\\displaystyle R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{e}^{2}\\le y\\le {e}^{5},\\,0\\le z\\le 4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $$\n\\displaystyle R=[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]\\times [{e}^{2},{e}^{5}]\\times [0,4]\n$$ is a rectangular box, and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z & =[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}+1)\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}+1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =24(\\sqrt{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ef1d15-ab53-5c1e-ae63-22e77b303a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275493} {:answer/id #uuid "6732bd4b-5bae-5817-8033-888f275b28fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276400} {:answer/id #uuid "67a2f8f7-b5b8-58b8-9b71-9fb46c231333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276404} {:answer/id #uuid "770b7a40-3fb3-5938-a6fd-72477b0e9ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276552} {:answer/id #uuid "af7eefd6-d587-5e8f-ac1d-55687b70130a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6d5d4c-02e0-5686-9189-c711d926bc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7e7a3fcc-a15c-5e4a-92b5-d91d52ab682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161301" :question/problem "![](images/52/5276afad13c3e52854d33a3ff3404ab401781e08d1888214121cf2d56c0bb304.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+2y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c6bd5e-0a05-54b2-8760-1af815a21d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275528} {:answer/id #uuid "67245e6c-b985-5f9e-a895-775b667aacaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276399} {:answer/id #uuid "77e3d978-da8c-5d68-b60a-4a8194925e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276562} {:answer/id #uuid "78ac9cfc-a243-510f-9152-b7aa90fe5547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276571} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e77ba-76c5-54e5-8b35-467bdaf0f222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275528} :answer-field/id #uuid "455d1d44-da82-5aaf-86ed-4b3d398efe8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc8526c3-3b66-50ba-a72f-6803fda09a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161311" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d4b598-7e8d-541e-bbd6-3dbbb07b4ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275841} {:answer/id #uuid "4304b64b-94d9-5a07-9565-9ca1566abcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276068} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf0425b-4482-59ac-acf5-655dac3d6002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276156} {:answer/id #uuid "7347d307-a529-5778-9cc3-b43ce3b89820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276522} {:answer/id #uuid "ec21c7c2-b53f-5a6f-9b9f-2e82eb49b46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d3d767-8116-56a7-b22f-ca77b6227aaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "23220eb4-f0ea-5f75-a067-2a7409386baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161318" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d6cedcc2d8c5a232d8d7ed89f050fa7a545ed0da6075ddb44eec67e065b9e87.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=-4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4272be20-e9f6-5f58-8d65-20ec49dcd88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276060} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9849ff-8b7c-559c-9004-711a9263b908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e-1)\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276484} {:answer/id #uuid "875101c4-2932-5d0c-8dfc-50974a95eb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276696} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea90df1-70bc-5a3d-bc73-2c004006066f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276772} {:answer/id #uuid "ec474ca4-06b0-5ff6-8cf7-8b695d4c2c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276060} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c03fe3-db1e-5523-92c9-13f7cee7e078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cedd414d-7a92-5f38-8d1c-98c03492493d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161322" :question/problem "![](images/92/9215246969b621002a96dec3aed8a012045b8061371e4c6a1234f54d53de29cc.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{3}y$ whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "505c7e4b-7779-5b74-903b-e90c8023eae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276196} {:answer/id #uuid "68c320d3-66a6-5afb-b396-ac305424f7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+2z-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276418} {:answer/id #uuid "a13f06d6-3684-5189-9263-82c1a846137d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡z+\\pi -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276930} {:answer/id #uuid "a8dd82f1-56c8-5209-97a0-5e47bf0e59c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+2z-2y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277000} {:answer/id #uuid "f13dcfdf-9280-5fd0-9cca-4509d35df4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡z-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276196} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f08b750-8569-5722-9291-eebf799c1107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29632302-5883-5021-9de6-855ebf0325c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161566" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-8\\le y\\le {sin}^{2}⁡z,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi ,\\,-2y-1\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+2z\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a9/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-2y-1,\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)={y}^{2}+2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-8\\le y\\le {sin}^{2}⁡z,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-8}^{{sin}^{2}⁡z}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-8}^{{sin}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=0+\\pi =\\pi .\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "23c7a7e9-da7d-5c85-866c-b2fe189ab9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} {:answer/id #uuid "3af6f758-92f1-5104-b867-e764a1444773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279116} {:answer/id #uuid "96ae20c4-d2ba-5df1-9ef7-ee8dda62071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279925} {:answer/id #uuid "d5735b14-23fc-5b32-8069-3b4c3988f129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280475} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd0405b-e35c-5d85-8bc3-a5a6eedb7d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2358d472-2501-5ea9-a695-06324117b98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278908} {:answer/id #uuid "5834e7af-b14d-5b59-9e7e-d2fcd562d148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279404} {:answer/id #uuid "87a4d350-525b-58ca-88ca-8efff4435311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279803} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d6a756-d7d9-5580-bb4e-17e2f088d5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280444} {:answer/id #uuid "f1de4843-42b0-5299-bed9-f1552ef424b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280699} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb40ecc-4401-539e-bdf0-61baf2ff9424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "644f8abf-d61c-5475-b3d7-b08f23c7f271" :question/math-academy-id "q-162178" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-4|=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bfe566d-4865-5f8f-a37a-83e03b0dc780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279122} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2ac78f-5d63-5921-99df-bf96a10edf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279141} {:answer/id #uuid "61b9d18a-afc1-5120-aa3a-df30c677fefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279482} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbd27d0-1210-5743-bbf5-a053bc29a4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280011} {:answer/id #uuid "d745bf79-0be7-5b58-bb77-c24d73c4606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279482} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2479707-e030-591f-98f1-9c405d78511c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "02e4a694-0c9e-57f7-ab0e-3cb84ca52e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-162182" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-1|=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a628431-fe0e-5a95-b2c4-47aa673ce54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278830} {:answer/id #uuid "498c939a-fe60-59d7-a7c4-1f3afb25c361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279250} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f591d5-06dd-5ed0-8b36-a300e71e632a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280101} {:answer/id #uuid "c8aff3f4-4fc5-5df9-b512-f1441b80602d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280348} {:answer/id #uuid "fd652f9e-0cf8-5991-ad8c-30975d53e3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280348} :answer-field/id #uuid "f68428f8-5fcb-52be-8b85-e3551a45aeea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a00aae13-9eba-5781-8e1a-54e7168af3f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162188" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y=u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v-2 \\\\ u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 1=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-4|=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321edb11-0508-5234-8c46-ee8a76c6c12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279030} {:answer/id #uuid "4266fe87-6a47-55cf-84ee-c2fbfd71bb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279188} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5dfdec-9cf8-52ad-b780-94d879248546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279288} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d03c1-2970-5cb6-8914-27b990e945c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280550} {:answer/id #uuid "f5956008-0a0a-5fec-b68a-490734e1070f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280742} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc35142-d7e6-5722-9ac8-6ae09af1cefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf48a6fd-c438-5f8f-9914-46d60d716c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162190" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=2v-u-5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 2v-u-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b77f94-9b2e-5986-9a43-c22541a1c39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278743} {:answer/id #uuid "7a11c398-9165-5c5e-b795-7af0e1679f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279680} {:answer/id #uuid "94ee1b63-9a47-5ca7-aacf-a87f30e1d00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279915} {:answer/id #uuid "95d85816-8b14-528a-a28e-a8e5a48909b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279922} {:answer/id #uuid "fffb02da-41f7-5223-94e2-b3b8c0b034e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279915} :answer-field/id #uuid "523714f3-7d37-5296-ad4e-2a05740f19bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faa815d3-1a15-5270-95c0-06e76987590b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162193" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+5v+2,\\,y=1-u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+5v+2 \\\\ 1-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+5v \\\\ -u\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 5=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|5|=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1283e1d9-5638-5cf5-8afe-fccb48c4513e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278761} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e99c71-ce7b-5a57-8b35-eff03f28886a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280088} {:answer/id #uuid "bee5e71a-fc3b-5642-9353-bcf840af6970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280271} {:answer/id #uuid "d650e02b-bf8f-5b88-a546-cd6aa55f62f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280483} {:answer/id #uuid "e733f81f-7e5b-56da-a2cd-22f5cd7afff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280483} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5d13f4-2cb9-5ffa-b608-20e5bb1d0372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79b6b1e2-201b-5b88-aae8-abebb7edf5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162195" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-2v-3,\\,y=u-v+2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-2v-3 \\\\ u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-2v \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|0|=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086b2610-2023-519b-a8e8-91e8082fe456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278671} {:answer/id #uuid "0a885a75-ef44-5b04-a856-76c492be9fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278692} {:answer/id #uuid "1b430cc2-04e6-53e3-8792-e3477c685484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278844} {:answer/id #uuid "21f1b945-693e-5bf9-9e09-ed3b2d638d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278898} {:answer/id #uuid "79335a04-020e-533a-baf4-a95091808bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278692} :answer-field/id #uuid "90e2fed4-7709-5047-b4b1-cf83d0815012" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7aaa6c3c-ccc5-5e4c-bbad-d399e1496037" :question/math-academy-id "q-162196" :question/problem "![](images/57/576d60f179b56159504efc59178ada3db798046d01a7dbc92986cd1162f770ea.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u+2v+1,\\,y=2u+v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+2v+1 \\\\ 2u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6e4373-347b-586f-904c-be59818f4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279110} {:answer/id #uuid "5d56f302-e7a0-5f70-b0e0-272d1328de15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279448} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf17246-a02f-5adb-adca-449dd47e93a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280536} {:answer/id #uuid "e92272a3-0f88-52d3-9b2f-9e2c60c5273f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280642} {:answer/id #uuid "f3652a9c-788f-5fa8-a9ee-b638c4ee6bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279110} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f360b7-8b35-5cc3-a7cb-82ef205491ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a4d15c5-f7e2-5db8-ae64-0fe650e3a234" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ee9371-864b-5659-8348-9612cc384780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278770} {:answer/id #uuid "aedef6d0-9498-5fc8-9341-b912125c0014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280138} {:answer/id #uuid "cb75cefb-350e-5cc4-8296-b8900df17d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280373} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bf0838-f644-5edb-b030-be38e9e0f44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280581} {:answer/id #uuid "ed82b094-7ea8-5cf3-8ddc-7b3d81bb2458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280581} :answer-field/id #uuid "0933377b-1185-5c82-81ba-572b2e374bc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d640e1ef-8b1e-5a87-a6d1-5fc3bd1f3743" :question/math-academy-id "q-162200" :question/problem "![](images/da/da6c0fb32e62b6effe133c527fee528c47b6f1c8dc7dd1d77d7d04f42b4c3d2b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023367fc-8545-563a-b592-eb5deed72636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278601} {:answer/id #uuid "09147043-07da-5971-9bf3-822cad3ff0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278678} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7879f1-8a5d-5414-a209-0e2adedbafed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279737} {:answer/id #uuid "8de0e8cb-1f2b-53b2-b630-7bf2d1a789e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279855} {:answer/id #uuid "977fdb51-6e85-5ca3-96b2-572872bb338c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278678} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d40