d #edb/ref 17592186279944} {:answer/id #uuid "bbcb8b58-fc45-5071-a477-b457f2f1ed98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280244} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e8043a-a238-5611-b6e7-ef351d2dd477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279085} :answer-field/id #uuid "d543117c-c7fd-56e3-93a4-09da102f0831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b8f12922-35f6-5a3b-8472-719f4cc88613" :question/math-academy-id "q-162202" :question/problem "![](images/89/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05708488-ccd8-5605-95df-2765b075bf65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278641} {:answer/id #uuid "369dff19-98b2-5c47-b178-8eff0acee120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279069} {:answer/id #uuid "68ecfa8f-5ed1-5148-9ee1-64b89343f9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279544} {:answer/id #uuid "70576f09-9a47-5c10-aa7b-96bd3fb89db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279611} {:answer/id #uuid "80a75519-4bb8-5f8d-bf66-bc6c8b0d6879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279544} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a82260-c990-515f-8f88-567fb794bf93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d076341b-6900-5a76-9cb3-7d856b14ee11" :question/math-academy-id "q-162203" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfbd80c4e052ae3c286d25b2cd7bf5a1ffcd76ac5a030b7f9b633d0f12517231.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9-6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0924fc26-ee91-542f-8fa2-ecc58bfba463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278680} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9548de-c3e2-5c7a-962f-b96ff5beb190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278834} {:answer/id #uuid "8e25faf2-3527-5984-b7f1-7a324619549a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279858} {:answer/id #uuid "ec716651-bab0-5363-a2ed-e132ee549eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280668} {:answer/id #uuid "fc26313f-bb67-5173-816d-d31934e140d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279858} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c88e06-5051-5ab1-8460-abd7ee08e85b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "40140618-61fa-5647-9056-c00bfaadeaae" :question/math-academy-id "q-162204" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2cb5cf6d3307c1291dbbb785cd71ed6edecba3d448afbc2f5f5c2d1128b457c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c7c0bb-04c8-5b68-b839-a1fd420d22a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278631} {:answer/id #uuid "4274a419-61b5-5bce-9898-d900ed5ce294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279190} {:answer/id #uuid "92ea8eef-5503-5300-bc03-4d743e1f2f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279897} {:answer/id #uuid "bab63e57-284a-556d-a8ec-1e457aefbc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280237} {:answer/id #uuid "c98c1779-760c-5b17-a6d3-2f66a18d07d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279897} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c99b77d-a70e-5910-8a1b-617d614ff6ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd1aad0c-e4b0-51b3-9d8d-afbb5058a343" :question/math-academy-id "q-162207" :question/problem "![](images/57/57948af956c9b83ed944fafc26e81a5c003401f753bc520e088ffdbd71915c3a.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a triangle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1426060a-b9a8-5ec4-8710-e6c7c2d38ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278772} {:answer/id #uuid "339b5c5c-ee84-5443-a987-f2c7e4d8b1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279040} {:answer/id #uuid "80867be3-b045-5cee-98c3-52438d048a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279745} {:answer/id #uuid "e00b27ca-b875-5773-9299-68d6b7cf0bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280571} {:answer/id #uuid "f725b8b3-2207-5775-b510-4923933df0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d6fff09-af1e-53fd-b0c2-3a6ff70f73e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ee569a9-51ca-558e-aa25-dba25b19b61f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162208" :question/problem "![](images/02/0231cc5aaf661cc873f18ff676a03d66927be48b49d38649efc45860197d18c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411cf09f-aaa8-5213-a15d-e483b24650c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A tetrahedron" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279170} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc0aa9-2d9d-5344-8ce5-d4d954e92fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} {:answer/id #uuid "99cffdbb-a38c-5a63-9d04-87167a638bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279951} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f74625-2c51-5fb6-83b9-61fbae0292bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280479} {:answer/id #uuid "e51121c0-fae9-532e-b1aa-79a388ca1f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec2e96d-de39-5f66-aab1-77904959b9e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a795336-4ab0-5101-bbca-aa94f4ca2a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-162214" :question/problem "![](images/00/00151dc7a58722a649a52494434950695a27cb4e7cad14f32f76abbddf629a91.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-3v+2,\\,y=4v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-3v+2 \\\\ 4v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-3v \\\\ 4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195bb2b0-3c00-5188-a9b8-26a0a9ccdf97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278815} {:answer/id #uuid "40a9cc60-2352-5a99-93fb-5f67de8ebc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279166} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb619f-eec5-5b5f-acec-793e11542403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279297} {:answer/id #uuid "80bfc1b2-c5dd-5eb9-b31c-ba89843e8c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279748} {:answer/id #uuid "b9da83b0-baf3-5459-8002-3bf2e296dcbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278815} :answer-field/id #uuid "e482fdd7-8e2a-5c19-b286-23e1eb8eb9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ae18cba-a48e-5294-b453-329b86fcae7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162217" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1ee8bc2c212708ed583c709420a856b28f6e2129c24690a504b4716b2692aa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-2v+1,\\,y=2u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2v+1 \\\\ 2u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2v \\\\ 2u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2caab8eb-db16-5bdf-96dc-2168d092e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278984} {:answer/id #uuid "6a83847c-1e16-5393-bea7-d87851c125e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279559} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4374f4-2af6-558f-accd-d53004d33ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279566} {:answer/id #uuid "8a15b67b-ab7d-5421-98e9-271ea88c8de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279823} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb84763-333b-5784-9255-e80858f6a6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279559} :answer-field/id #uuid "85eb8117-14af-5ac9-87c7-29d7a050f538" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f95513aa-0be5-512e-82ea-b1dcd0aafc49" :question/math-academy-id "q-162222" :question/problem "![](images/95/957d55e3fb66bb2633798cf13d5c6049ffa4524ca585515ffaf88771ee32fe9b.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c423a78-0db9-5f90-983b-9dfbb047e9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279124} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea410e2-60c7-51c0-9002-7d8a661b4542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279860} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b22b8d-787b-5280-a5f2-57f6394bbe69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280587} {:answer/id #uuid "eb788c5f-15bc-5b4c-bbd8-95a2b365d823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280660} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ed7cb4-543a-55f8-a67b-979cd22e5cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280660} :answer-field/id #uuid "228341f6-d27f-56b7-9f4c-9d5fa6515e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef668553-5d10-5944-a4d2-e24693f30a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-162223" :question/problem "![](images/52/522a51527563645261a3b4c5a1472510bcb3e890fc82d3b4c1b2ccdae59b9dbd.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the rectangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c78548a-862a-5246-bc1b-00578ccf3514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279276} {:answer/id #uuid "71afa1df-e22f-56d7-a584-9af2514d05a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279623} {:answer/id #uuid "9e198494-80d4-58da-996d-eb4de786f578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279994} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8d7ef9-8b78-57e0-9208-4006881080c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280403} {:answer/id #uuid "ef008053-89a0-534a-9bfd-e9f8124ce870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279994} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba133b2f-ee36-5e2b-87c2-26ccbd0be401" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32ed1c68-126b-5577-85f4-628388a45f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-162224" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b57fe5ce4b75c7daa5c866f3eb537ed62546e5fced67f6e2cb82af768cf382cb.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6f4259-f8e6-50d2-a27a-e21a3dbd842f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278885} {:answer/id #uuid "26605c63-dc1f-553c-9a90-202cae212a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278933} {:answer/id #uuid "41dbf191-5e3f-5815-9255-4e8bf000acff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279178} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2a63fe-e4fb-5e2e-9aa6-50ac6644284c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279722} {:answer/id #uuid "d09f332e-616c-505d-a0aa-63486360b032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279178} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c149585-db76-5f50-b0c3-8e2eb75eb5e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4197fe63-5776-5546-b6a0-81601947295c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162225" :question/problem "![](images/af/afee2a3c30384baa044088177550b0ac5ed2de228b14e48dd7f729e8a7493318.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} {:answer/id #uuid "268b4c33-c226-5773-b9b1-46201fc827d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278936} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc11660-2038-5011-af7b-4ccf1db2e5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279836} {:answer/id #uuid "c41bff5d-e1eb-5b17-a3c4-b0bc834579fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280318} {:answer/id #uuid "c71ff19f-ae04-593f-8810-e601bcf14f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd81652e-0bcb-5dc4-b49d-1d1e352b840b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55c78036-de48-5eea-9253-f540a61a0813" :question/math-academy-id "q-162330" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5f4ec9b9b12411f3057bc074a14a1f541c768c30d3a9746d7a0484b0491ad2e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/077fc8133673cd57aba570cca9a6e0f5f25aa742720948c46db2b9e1d05add69.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}y\\,[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156a47fd-2c85-5208-95ad-5d50ddbcf677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278783} {:answer/id #uuid "57ad6671-46bc-5a4d-9af6-b9f89965cadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279397} {:answer/id #uuid "8e121028-a9bf-5ba0-a4cb-3fb4471c6815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279856} {:answer/id #uuid "b259ada9-1a8f-5265-8931-8e97c4a5a526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(2\\pi +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280167} {:answer/id #uuid "e55e7203-a0f9-5ce6-ac49-337eb9365639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278783} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0dcb97f-3379-54fe-a421-8db4631dab43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63019a17-1ba4-5f61-b529-aa74e153abd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162331" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2yz}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d3865f790df8c2a228f08c290a113954687ade63a275a754c4cb3199c234d5ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/08/085b2ce5a9ecd2632f70f287bbef5e9f04566871fc8adf7ba10076e2e875ca2e.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{[-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)]}_{x=0}^{x=2yz}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}(-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y+z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[y-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y]}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\frac{1}{12}[{sin}^{3}⁡2y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)-\\frac{1}{12}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4166d235-26fd-55c2-ba75-a545a0c6714c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279175} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4f5558-c7b3-50d9-9d22-de8fab0fd956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} {:answer/id #uuid "4d57d1fb-25b1-5c73-89a3-d36dba6a22dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279287} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4e5861-a325-58b9-b231-448c50cb7791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279588} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e25be-40ab-5eca-8a7c-df19ac612398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e1b3a4-df1f-517d-a217-9d6a9ba2488c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7c93d46-b6af-5493-bffe-c7cad1a03b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-162476" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c7a7e9-da7d-5c85-866c-b2fe189ab9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} {:answer/id #uuid "3af6f758-92f1-5104-b867-e764a1444773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279116} {:answer/id #uuid "96ae20c4-d2ba-5df1-9ef7-ee8dda62071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279925} {:answer/id #uuid "d5735b14-23fc-5b32-8069-3b4c3988f129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280475} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd0405b-e35c-5d85-8bc3-a5a6eedb7d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b447dd2e-4a24-53d5-85f0-852828cc0edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d2059e5f-93a8-5b5f-ab4e-bc10c8876989" :question/math-academy-id "q-162534" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2}\\right)}{{\\left(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4}\\right)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f5a574-0cca-57fa-a4fd-7d5cf83c8022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278811} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc014a-0203-582f-8cb4-ea9a4980b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279135} {:answer/id #uuid "5d71a5fd-a1a5-578b-b16d-463e3b9ca06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279450} {:answer/id #uuid "8070acd2-0eb1-5b3d-8631-c79cba8f6cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279743} {:answer/id #uuid "b07a55f4-358b-52f0-8ce8-2a341242e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb211f7-a024-5d85-9487-56111b032805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "636e23a3-a873-5229-81ee-5d1161e02dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163070" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡(0)-{sin}^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253a5862-fe76-59bf-bc9f-e5b4c2c81cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278922} {:answer/id #uuid "38badd4c-30f8-554d-b11b-dbcc78a85dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279087} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9f3b1d-5608-5c20-993b-fb7acb5da074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} {:answer/id #uuid "a4996288-fed0-5a92-95cf-6f46d536635d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280051} {:answer/id #uuid "d76a9632-4cec-5ffa-99d2-34ae09bdb959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c588e84a-c201-5164-ad12-e6f62f15b38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f8309ca-8cff-5376-85e1-fbcd4f2e85f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163075" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} & \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-2=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2+4+1=7.\n$$"}]
